[[:db/cas 17592186179602 :question/problem "![](images/f7/f7e61f72ed7488fd490f42ff519316ed2e87d3c997d64e2cc6b7af36f951dbb5.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $\\mathbf F,$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r =  0$\n\nII. $\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r > 0$\n\nIII. $\\displaystyle\\oint_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r > 0$" "![](images/f7/f7e61f72ed7488fd490f42ff519316ed2e87d3c997d64e2cc6b7af36f951dbb5.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $\\mathbf F,$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r =  0\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r > 0\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle\\oint_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r > 0\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186179602 :question/worked-solution "The circulation $\\Gamma$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d} \\mathbf r\n= \\int_a^b \\mathbf F(r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t) \\; \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r > 0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r <                               0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Since the force $\\mathbf F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves around $C,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/06/063836a4780ecb4be6e8c6149a422e3c8e9f2770304e659a8116691b91e3d7f1.png)\n\nSo, $\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $\\mathbf F$ remains unchanged.\n\n![](images/70/704915d2852e1654aa31d17bd2dfb07a3022fe3d8b597f37bf98bd5af2de1d1b.png)\n\nSo, $\\displaystyle\\oint_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "The circulation $\\Gamma$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d} \\mathbf r\n= \\int_a^b \\mathbf F(r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t) \\; \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r > 0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r <                               0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Since the force $\\mathbf F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves around $C,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/06/063836a4780ecb4be6e8c6149a422e3c8e9f2770304e659a8116691b91e3d7f1.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $\\mathbf F$ remains unchanged.\n\n![](images/70/704915d2852e1654aa31d17bd2dfb07a3022fe3d8b597f37bf98bd5af2de1d1b.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle\\oint_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186179604 :question/problem "![](images/29/298b7b4293ff104a62107670fa2aba603b8a23e9ad042f7e270a4acf6267a2fa.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path where the curve is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r <  0$\n\nII. $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r =         0$\n\nIII. $\\displaystyle\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r >  0$" "![](images/29/298b7b4293ff104a62107670fa2aba603b8a23e9ad042f7e270a4acf6267a2fa.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path where the curve is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r <  0\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r =         0\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r >  0\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186179604 :question/worked-solution "Remember that $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r$ gives the total work done by a force $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along $C\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r > 0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion  of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r <                     0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. Since the force $\\mathbf F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/cd/cd71f379a02405bc21aecfb0bb14e9798cfbc4d7fd2c1d3a1d5221aa8d64638f.png)\n\n \nSo, $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $\\mathbf F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/b2/b2284e7ffa962fd46333c4eca47a77473007fdd96e0813b9928f0303df8170f3.png)\n\n \nSo, $\\displaystyle\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along $C\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r > 0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion  of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r <                     0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. Since the force $\\mathbf F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/cd/cd71f379a02405bc21aecfb0bb14e9798cfbc4d7fd2c1d3a1d5221aa8d64638f.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $\\mathbf F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/b2/b2284e7ffa962fd46333c4eca47a77473007fdd96e0813b9928f0303df8170f3.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186179605 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) =( e^y+x)\\,\\mathbf i +(xe^y+y^2)\\,\\mathbf j.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\mathbf F$ is conservative\n\nII. $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C\\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r = 0$ for any piecewise-smooth path $C\\in\\mathbb R^2$\n\nIII. $\\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_C\\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r =0$ for any closed path $C\\in\\mathbb R^2$" "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) =( e^y+x)\\,\\mathbf i +(xe^y+y^2)\\,\\mathbf j.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\mathbf F$ is conservative\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C\\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r = 0\n$$ for any piecewise-smooth path $C\\in\\mathbb R^2$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_C\\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r =0\n$$ for any closed path $C\\in\\mathbb R^2$"] [:db/cas 17592186179609 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_\\limits{\\theta \\to 0} \\:\\dfrac{\\tan 2\\theta}{\\theta}$." "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{\\theta \\to 0} \\:\\dfrac{\\tan 2\\theta}{\\theta}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186179609 :question/worked-solution "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(θ → 0)\\frac{\\tan 2θ}{θ} &= lim_(θ → 0)(\\left(\\frac{\\sin 2θ}{\\cos 2θ}\\right))/(θ) \\\\\n&= lim_(θ → 0)(\\frac{\\sin 2θ}{θ} \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\cos 2θ}) \\\\\n&= lim_(θ → 0)\\frac{\\sin 2θ}{θ} \\cdot lim_(θ → 0)\\frac{1}{\\cos 2θ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0$, both numerator and denominator approach $0$. So, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n$\\lim_\\limits{\\theta \\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\sin 2\\theta}{\\theta} = \\dfrac{0}{0}$,\nwhich is an indeterminate form. Instead, let's recall the following special limit:\n$\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\sin x}{x} =1$We rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n$lim_(θ → 0)\\frac{\\sin 2θ}{θ}|= lim_(θ → 0)(2 \\cdot \\frac{\\sin 2θ}{2θ}); = 2 \\cdot lim_(θ → 0)\\frac{\\sin2θ}{2θ}$Let $x = 2\\theta$. Then, since $x\\to0$ as $\\theta \\to 0$, we have\n$2 \\cdot \\lim_\\limits{\\theta \\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\sin \\color{blue}2\\theta}{\\color{blue}2\\theta} = 2 \\cdot \\lim_\\limits{x \\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\sin \\color{blue}x}{\\color{blue}x} = 2 \\cdot 1 =2$.\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n$\\lim_\\limits{\\theta \\to 0} \\: \\dfrac{1}{\\cos 2\\theta} = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos 0} = \\dfrac11 = 1$.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nlim_(θ → 0)\\frac{\\sin 2θ}{θ} \\cdot lim_(θ → 0)\\frac{1}{\\cos 2θ} = 2 \\cdot 1 = 2\n$$" "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(θ → 0)\\frac{\\tan 2θ}{θ} &= lim_(θ → 0)(\\left(\\frac{\\sin 2θ}{\\cos 2θ}\\right))/(θ) \\\\\n&= lim_(θ → 0)(\\frac{\\sin 2θ}{θ} \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\cos 2θ}) \\\\\n&= lim_(θ → 0)\\frac{\\sin 2θ}{θ} \\cdot lim_(θ → 0)\\frac{1}{\\cos 2θ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0$, both numerator and denominator approach $0$. So, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{\\theta \\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\sin 2\\theta}{\\theta} = \\dfrac{0}{0},\n$$\nwhich is an indeterminate form. Instead, let's recall the following special limit:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x \\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\sin x}{x} =1\n$$We rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle lim_(θ → 0)\\frac{\\sin 2θ}{θ}|= lim_(θ → 0)(2 \\cdot \\frac{\\sin 2θ}{2θ}); = 2 \\cdot lim_(θ → 0)\\frac{\\sin2θ}{2θ}\n$$Let $x = 2\\theta$. Then, since $x\\to0$ as $\\theta \\to 0$, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle 2 \\cdot \\lim_\\limits{\\theta \\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\sin \\color{blue}2\\theta}{\\color{blue}2\\theta} = 2 \\cdot \\lim_\\limits{x \\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\sin \\color{blue}x}{\\color{blue}x} = 2 \\cdot 1 =2.\n$$\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{\\theta \\to 0} \\: \\dfrac{1}{\\cos 2\\theta} = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos 0} = \\dfrac11 = 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nlim_(θ → 0)\\frac{\\sin 2θ}{θ} \\cdot lim_(θ → 0)\\frac{1}{\\cos 2θ} = 2 \\cdot 1 = 2\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186179610 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the generating function of our sequence as\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\sum\\limits_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n\n$$\n\nWe start by multiplying both sides of the recurrence relation by $x^n$. This gives\n\n$$\na_n x^n + 4a_{n-1}x^n = (-2)^n x^n\n$$\n\nIf we sum this over all values of\n\n$$\nn \\geq 1\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty a_n x^n + \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty 4a_{n-1}x^n = \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty (-2)^n x^n\n$$\n\nNow, notice the following:\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty a_n x^n = \\sum\\limits_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n - a_0 = f(x) - 1$\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty 4a_{n-1}x^n = 4x \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty a_{n-1}x^{n-1} = 4x \\sum\\limits_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n = 4x f(x)$\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty (-2)^n x^n = \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty (-2x)^n = \\dfrac{-2x}{1-(-2x)} = -\\dfrac{2x}{1+2x}$\n\nTherefore, our equation can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(f(x) - 1) + 4xf(x) &= -\\frac{2x}{1 + 2x} \\\\\nf(x)(1 + 4x) - 1 &= -\\frac{2x}{1 + 2x} \\\\\nf(x)(1 + 4x) &= 1 - \\frac{2x}{1 + 2x} \\\\\nf(x)(1 + 4x) &= \\frac{1}{1 + 2x} \\\\\nf(x) &= (1)/((1 + 2x)(1 + 4x))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's denote the generating function of our sequence as\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\sum\\limits_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n\n$$\n\nWe start by multiplying both sides of the recurrence relation by $x^n$. This gives\n\n$$\na_n x^n + 4a_{n-1}x^n = (-2)^n x^n\n$$\n\nIf we sum this over all values of\n\n$$\nn \\geq 1\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty a_n x^n + \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty 4a_{n-1}x^n = \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty (-2)^n x^n\n$$\n\nNow, notice the following:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty a_n x^n = \\sum\\limits_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n - a_0 = f(x) - 1\n$$\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty 4a_{n-1}x^n = 4x \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty a_{n-1}x^{n-1} = 4x \\sum\\limits_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n = 4x f(x)\n$$\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty (-2)^n x^n = \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty (-2x)^n = \\dfrac{-2x}{1-(-2x)} = -\\dfrac{2x}{1+2x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(f(x) - 1) + 4xf(x) &= -\\frac{2x}{1 + 2x} \\\\\nf(x)(1 + 4x) - 1 &= -\\frac{2x}{1 + 2x} \\\\\nf(x)(1 + 4x) &= 1 - \\frac{2x}{1 + 2x} \\\\\nf(x)(1 + 4x) &= \\frac{1}{1 + 2x} \\\\\nf(x) &= (1)/((1 + 2x)(1 + 4x))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186179611 :question/problem "Find the generating function for the sequence that satisfies the recurrence relation $a_n-a_{n-1}=1-3n$ with $a_0=2$.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty n x^n = \\dfrac{x}{(1-x)^2}$.*" "Find the generating function for the sequence that satisfies the recurrence relation $a_n-a_{n-1}=1-3n$ with $a_0=2$.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty n x^n = \\dfrac{x}{(1-x)^2}.\n$$*"] [:db/cas 17592186179611 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the generating function of our sequence as\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\sum\\limits_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n\n$$\n\nWe start by multiplying both sides of the recurrence relation by $x^n$. This gives\n\n$$\na_nx^n -a_{n-1}x^n =(1-3n) x^n\n$$\n\nIf we sum this over all values of\n\n$$\nn \\geq 1\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty a_n x^n - \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty a_{n-1}x^n = \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty (1-3n) x^n\n$$\n\nNow, notice the following:\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty a_n x^n = \\sum\\limits_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n - a_0 = f(x) - 2$\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty a_{n-1}x^n = x \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty a_{n-1}x^{n-1} = x \\sum\\limits_{n=0}^\\infty a_{n}x^n = x f(x)$\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty (1-3n) x^n = \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty x^n -3 \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty n x^n = \\dfrac{x}{1-x}-\\dfrac{3x}{(1-x)^2}$\n\nTherefore, our equation can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(f(x) - 2) - xf(x) &= \\frac{x}{1 - x} - (3x)/((1 - x)^{2}) \\\\\nf(x)(1 - x) - 2 &= (-2x - x^{2})/((1 - x)^{2}) \\\\\nf(x)(1 - x) &= (-2x - x^{2})/((1 - x)^{2}) + 2 \\\\\nf(x)(1 - x) &= (x^{2} - 6x + 2)/((1 - x)^{2}) \\\\\nf(x) &= (x^{2} - 6x + 2)/((1 - x)^{3})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's denote the generating function of our sequence as\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\sum\\limits_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n\n$$\n\nWe start by multiplying both sides of the recurrence relation by $x^n$. This gives\n\n$$\na_nx^n -a_{n-1}x^n =(1-3n) x^n\n$$\n\nIf we sum this over all values of\n\n$$\nn \\geq 1\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty a_n x^n - \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty a_{n-1}x^n = \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty (1-3n) x^n\n$$\n\nNow, notice the following:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty a_n x^n = \\sum\\limits_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n - a_0 = f(x) - 2\n$$\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty a_{n-1}x^n = x \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty a_{n-1}x^{n-1} = x \\sum\\limits_{n=0}^\\infty a_{n}x^n = x f(x)\n$$\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty (1-3n) x^n = \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty x^n -3 \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty n x^n = \\dfrac{x}{1-x}-\\dfrac{3x}{(1-x)^2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(f(x) - 2) - xf(x) &= \\frac{x}{1 - x} - (3x)/((1 - x)^{2}) \\\\\nf(x)(1 - x) - 2 &= (-2x - x^{2})/((1 - x)^{2}) \\\\\nf(x)(1 - x) &= (-2x - x^{2})/((1 - x)^{2}) + 2 \\\\\nf(x)(1 - x) &= (x^{2} - 6x + 2)/((1 - x)^{2}) \\\\\nf(x) &= (x^{2} - 6x + 2)/((1 - x)^{3})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186179612 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the generating function of our sequence as\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\sum\\limits_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n\n$$\n\nWe start by multiplying both sides of the recurrence relation by $x^n$. This gives\n\n$$\na_n x^n + a_{n-1}x^n + a_{n-2} x^n = x^n\n$$\n\nIf we sum this over all values of\n\n$$\nn \\geq 2\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\sum\\limits_{n=2}^\\infty a_n x^n + \\sum\\limits_{n=2}^\\infty a_{n-1}x^n + \\sum\\limits_{n=2}^\\infty a_{n-2}x^n = \\sum\\limits_{n=2}^\\infty x^n. \\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nThe idea now is to write each of the summations in equation $(\\ast)$ terms of $f(x)$.\n\n- The first sum on the left-hand side is almost the same as $f(x)$. It's just missing the first two terms. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{n=2}^\\infty a_n x^n = \\sum\\limits_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n - a_0 - a_1 x = f(x) - 1\n$$\n\n- The second sum on the left-hand side has a mismatch between the indexes. To correct this, we can pull a single factor of $x$ outside the sum, as follows:\n$\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{n=2}^\\infty a_{n-1}x^n = x \\sum\\limits_{n=2}^\\infty a_{n-1}x^{n-1} = x \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty a_{n}x^{n}$\nThen, we recognize that the summation is almost $f(x)$, but the first term is missing. Therefore, we have\n$x \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty a_{n}x^{n} = x \\left(\\sum\\limits_{n=0}^\\infty a_{n}x^n - a_0 \\right) = x (f(x) - 1)$.\n- For the third term, we can pull out a factor of $x^2$ to correct the mismatch of the indexes, which gives\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{n=2}^\\infty a_{n-2}x^n = x^2 \\sum\\limits_{n=2}^\\infty a_{n-2}x^{n-2} = x^2 \\sum\\limits_{n=0}^\\infty a_{n}x^n = x^2 f(x)\n$$\n\n- Finally, the right-hand side can be evaluated as a geometric series:\n$\\sum\\limits_{n=2}^\\infty x^n = \\dfrac{x^2}{1-x}$.\n\nTherefore, our equation $(\\ast)$ can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(f(x) - 1) + x(f(x) - 1) + x^{2}f(x) &= \\frac{x^{2}}{1 - x} \\\\\nf(x)(1 + x + x^{2}) - 1 - x &= \\frac{x^{2}}{1 - x} \\\\\nf(x)(1 + x + x^{2}) &= \\frac{x^{2}}{1 - x} + 1 + x \\\\\nf(x)(1 + x + x^{2}) &= \\frac{1}{1 - x} \\\\\nf(x) &= (1)/((1 - x)(1 + x + x^{2}))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's denote the generating function of our sequence as\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\sum\\limits_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n\n$$\n\nWe start by multiplying both sides of the recurrence relation by $x^n$. This gives\n\n$$\na_n x^n + a_{n-1}x^n + a_{n-2} x^n = x^n\n$$\n\nIf we sum this over all values of\n\n$$\nn \\geq 2\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\sum\\limits_{n=2}^\\infty a_n x^n + \\sum\\limits_{n=2}^\\infty a_{n-1}x^n + \\sum\\limits_{n=2}^\\infty a_{n-2}x^n = \\sum\\limits_{n=2}^\\infty x^n. \\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nThe idea now is to write each of the summations in equation $(\\ast)$ terms of $f(x)$.\n\n- The first sum on the left-hand side is almost the same as $f(x)$. It's just missing the first two terms. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{n=2}^\\infty a_n x^n = \\sum\\limits_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n - a_0 - a_1 x = f(x) - 1\n$$\n\n- The second sum on the left-hand side has a mismatch between the indexes. To correct this, we can pull a single factor of $x$ outside the sum, as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{n=2}^\\infty a_{n-1}x^n = x \\sum\\limits_{n=2}^\\infty a_{n-1}x^{n-1} = x \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty a_{n}x^{n}\n$$\nThen, we recognize that the summation is almost $f(x)$, but the first term is missing. Therefore, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle x \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty a_{n}x^{n} = x \\left(\\sum\\limits_{n=0}^\\infty a_{n}x^n - a_0 \\right) = x (f(x) - 1).\n$$\n- For the third term, we can pull out a factor of $x^2$ to correct the mismatch of the indexes, which gives\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{n=2}^\\infty a_{n-2}x^n = x^2 \\sum\\limits_{n=2}^\\infty a_{n-2}x^{n-2} = x^2 \\sum\\limits_{n=0}^\\infty a_{n}x^n = x^2 f(x)\n$$\n\n- Finally, the right-hand side can be evaluated as a geometric series:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{n=2}^\\infty x^n = \\dfrac{x^2}{1-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation $(\\ast)$ can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(f(x) - 1) + x(f(x) - 1) + x^{2}f(x) &= \\frac{x^{2}}{1 - x} \\\\\nf(x)(1 + x + x^{2}) - 1 - x &= \\frac{x^{2}}{1 - x} \\\\\nf(x)(1 + x + x^{2}) &= \\frac{x^{2}}{1 - x} + 1 + x \\\\\nf(x)(1 + x + x^{2}) &= \\frac{1}{1 - x} \\\\\nf(x) &= (1)/((1 - x)(1 + x + x^{2}))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186179618 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 5} \\:\\dfrac{\\sin (x-5)}{15-3x}$." "Find $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x \\to 5} \\:\\dfrac{\\sin (x-5)}{15-3x}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186179621 :question/worked-solution "To get the corresponding semi-log plot, we apply $\\log$ to both sides of\n\n$$\ny = a \\cdot b^x\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= a \\cdot b^{x} \\\\\n\\log y &= \\log (a \\cdot b^{x}) \\\\\n\\log y &= \\log a + \\log b^{x} \\\\\n\\log y &= \\log a + x \\cdot \\log b \\\\\nY &= A + Bx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nY=\\log y, \\qquad A=\\log a, \\qquad B=\\log b\n$$\n\nLet's now consider an approximate trend line\n\n$$\nY = A + Bx\n$$\n\nfor our data:\n\n![](images/99/993d24a9df00fc2458663c2f9c6d6dbb44e6b424d2d534ba04453042c09596f9.png)\n\nWe now proceed to calculate the constants $a$ and $b$:\n\n- Looking at the trendline, the $Y$-intercept is $Y \\approx 3$. Therefore,\n$A\\begin{vmatrix}\\approx 3 \\\\ \\log_{10} a\\end{vmatrix}\\approx 3; a \\mid \\approx 10^{3}; a|= 1000$.\n- The trendline passes close to the points $(3,4)$ and $(9,6)$. Therefore, the line's slope is\n$B \\approx \\dfrac{6 - 4}{9 - 3} = \\dfrac{2}{6} = \\dfrac{1}{3}$\nand we obtain\n$B\\begin{vmatrix}\\approx \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\log b\\end{vmatrix}\\approx \\frac{1}{3}; b \\mid \\approx 10^{1/3}; = \\sqrt[3]{10}; \\approx 2.15$\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." "To get the corresponding semi-log plot, we apply $\\log$ to both sides of\n\n$$\ny = a \\cdot b^x\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= a \\cdot b^{x} \\\\\n\\log y &= \\log (a \\cdot b^{x}) \\\\\n\\log y &= \\log a + \\log b^{x} \\\\\n\\log y &= \\log a + x \\cdot \\log b \\\\\nY &= A + Bx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nY=\\log y, \\qquad A=\\log a, \\qquad B=\\log b\n$$\n\nLet's now consider an approximate trend line\n\n$$\nY = A + Bx\n$$\n\nfor our data:\n\n![](images/99/993d24a9df00fc2458663c2f9c6d6dbb44e6b424d2d534ba04453042c09596f9.png)\n\nWe now proceed to calculate the constants $a$ and $b$:\n\n- Looking at the trendline, the $Y$-intercept is $Y \\approx 3$. Therefore,\n$$\n\\displaystyle A\\begin{vmatrix}\\approx 3 \\\\ \\log_{10} a\\end{vmatrix}\\approx 3; a \\mid \\approx 10^{3}; a|= 1000.\n$$\n- The trendline passes close to the points $(3,4)$ and $(9,6)$. Therefore, the line's slope is\n$$\n\\displaystyle B \\approx \\dfrac{6 - 4}{9 - 3} = \\dfrac{2}{6} = \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\nand we obtain\n$$\n\\displaystyle B\\begin{vmatrix}\\approx \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\log b\\end{vmatrix}\\approx \\frac{1}{3}; b \\mid \\approx 10^{1/3}; = \\sqrt[3]{10}; \\approx 2.15\n$$\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186179624 :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\dfrac{1}{2}gt^2\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\n- Let's summarize the information for the first dart:The initial velocity of the first dart is $u_1=63\\,\\textrm{m/s}$. The first dart is in motion for $t_1 = 2 +4 = 6 \\, \\textrm{s}$.The acceleration due to gravity is $g=-9.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2$. Notice that $g$ is *negative* since the initial motion of the first dart acts *against* gravity. Substituting these values into the formula, we can find the displacement of the first dart:\n$s_{1}|= u_{1}t_{1} + \\frac{1}{2}gt_{1}^{2}; = (63)(6) + \\frac{1}{2}(-9.8)(6)^{2}; = 201.6m$\n- Let's now summarize the information for the second dart:The second dart is in motion for $t_2=4\\,\\textrm{s}$. The second dart covers a distance of $s_2=s_1=201.6 \\, \\textrm{m}$, the same as the first one. The acceleration due to gravity is $g=-9.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2$. Notice that $g$ is *negative* since the initial motion of the second dart acts *against* gravity. Substituting these values into the formula and solving for $u_2$ (the initial velocity of the second dart), we obtain the following:\n$s_{2}|= u_{2}t_{2} + \\frac{1}{2}gt_{2}^{2}; (201.6)|= u_{2}(4) + \\frac{1}{2}(-9.8)(4)^{2}; 201.6|= 4u_{2} - 78.4; 280|= 4u_{2}; u_{2}|= 70$\n\nTherefore, the initial velocity of the second dart was $70 \\, \\textrm{m/s}$." "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\dfrac{1}{2}gt^2\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\n- Let's summarize the information for the first dart:The initial velocity of the first dart is $u_1=63\\,\\textrm{m/s}$. The first dart is in motion for $t_1 = 2 +4 = 6 \\, \\textrm{s}$.The acceleration due to gravity is $g=-9.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2$. Notice that $g$ is *negative* since the initial motion of the first dart acts *against* gravity. Substituting these values into the formula, we can find the displacement of the first dart:\n$$\n\\displaystyle s_{1}|= u_{1}t_{1} + \\frac{1}{2}gt_{1}^{2}; = (63)(6) + \\frac{1}{2}(-9.8)(6)^{2}; = 201.6m\n$$\n- Let's now summarize the information for the second dart:The second dart is in motion for $t_2=4\\,\\textrm{s}$. The second dart covers a distance of $s_2=s_1=201.6 \\, \\textrm{m}$, the same as the first one. The acceleration due to gravity is $g=-9.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2$. Notice that $g$ is *negative* since the initial motion of the second dart acts *against* gravity. Substituting these values into the formula and solving for $u_2$ (the initial velocity of the second dart), we obtain the following:\n$$\n\\displaystyle s_{2}|= u_{2}t_{2} + \\frac{1}{2}gt_{2}^{2}; (201.6)|= u_{2}(4) + \\frac{1}{2}(-9.8)(4)^{2}; 201.6|= 4u_{2} - 78.4; 280|= 4u_{2}; u_{2}|= 70\n$$\n\nTherefore, the initial velocity of the second dart was $70 \\, \\textrm{m/s}$."] [:db/cas 17592186179625 :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\dfrac{1}{2}gt^2\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nWe need to find the height $s$ of the cliff.\n\n- Let's summarize the information for the stone:The initial velocity of the stone is $u_1 = 0\\,\\textrm{m/s}$. The acceleration due to gravity is $g = 9.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2$.Let $t_1$ denote the time it took for the stone to reach the ground.Substituting these values into the formula, we can express the distance covered by the stone (i.e., the height of the cliff) as\n$s|= u_{1}t_{1} + \\frac{1}{2}gt_{1}^{2}; = (0)t_{1} + \\frac{1}{2}(9.8)t_{1}^{2}; = 4.9t_{1}^{2}$.\n- Let's now summarize the information for the ball:The initial velocity of the ball is $u_2 = 23.52 \\, \\textrm{m/s}$. The acceleration due to gravity is $g = 9.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2$.Let $t_2$ denote the time it took for the ball to reach the ground.\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula, we can express the distance covered by the ball as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ns &= u_{2}t_{2} + \\frac{1}{2}gt_{2}^{2} \\\\\n&= (23.52)t_{2} + \\frac{1}{2}(9.8)t_{2}^{2} \\\\\n&= 4.9t_{2}^{2} + 23.52t_{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that\n\n$$\nt_1 = t_2 + 2\n$$\n\nsince the stone is in motion $2$ seconds more than the ball, and both objects hit the ground simultaneously.\n\nTherefore, equating our two expressions for $s$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4.9t_{1}^{2} &= 4.9t_{2}^{2} + 23.52t_{2} \\\\\n4.9(t_{2} + 2)^{2} &= 4.9t_{2}^{2} + 23.52t_{2} \\\\\n4.9(t_{2}^{2} + 4t_{2} + 4) &= 4.9t_{2}^{2} + 23.52t_{2} \\\\\n4.9t_{2}^{2} + 19.6t_{2} + 19.6 &= 4.9t_{2}^{2} + 23.52t_{2} \\\\\n19.6t_{2} + 19.6 &= 23.52t_{2} \\\\\n19.6 &= 3.92t_{2} \\\\\nt_{2} &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\nt_1 = 5 + 2 = 7 \\, \\textrm{s}\n$$\n\nFinally, by substituting\n\n$$\nt_1=7\\,\\textrm{s}\n$$\n\ninto the first equation for $s$, we find that the height of the cliff is\n\n$$\ns = 4.9(7)^2 = 240.1 \\, \\textrm{m}\n$$" "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\dfrac{1}{2}gt^2\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nWe need to find the height $s$ of the cliff.\n\n- Let's summarize the information for the stone:The initial velocity of the stone is $u_1 = 0\\,\\textrm{m/s}$. The acceleration due to gravity is $g = 9.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2$.Let $t_1$ denote the time it took for the stone to reach the ground.Substituting these values into the formula, we can express the distance covered by the stone (i.e., the height of the cliff) as\n$$\n\\displaystyle s|= u_{1}t_{1} + \\frac{1}{2}gt_{1}^{2}; = (0)t_{1} + \\frac{1}{2}(9.8)t_{1}^{2}; = 4.9t_{1}^{2}.\n$$\n- Let's now summarize the information for the ball:The initial velocity of the ball is $u_2 = 23.52 \\, \\textrm{m/s}$. The acceleration due to gravity is $g = 9.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2$.Let $t_2$ denote the time it took for the ball to reach the ground.\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula, we can express the distance covered by the ball as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ns &= u_{2}t_{2} + \\frac{1}{2}gt_{2}^{2} \\\\\n&= (23.52)t_{2} + \\frac{1}{2}(9.8)t_{2}^{2} \\\\\n&= 4.9t_{2}^{2} + 23.52t_{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that\n\n$$\nt_1 = t_2 + 2\n$$\n\nsince the stone is in motion $2$ seconds more than the ball, and both objects hit the ground simultaneously.\n\nTherefore, equating our two expressions for $s$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4.9t_{1}^{2} &= 4.9t_{2}^{2} + 23.52t_{2} \\\\\n4.9(t_{2} + 2)^{2} &= 4.9t_{2}^{2} + 23.52t_{2} \\\\\n4.9(t_{2}^{2} + 4t_{2} + 4) &= 4.9t_{2}^{2} + 23.52t_{2} \\\\\n4.9t_{2}^{2} + 19.6t_{2} + 19.6 &= 4.9t_{2}^{2} + 23.52t_{2} \\\\\n19.6t_{2} + 19.6 &= 23.52t_{2} \\\\\n19.6 &= 3.92t_{2} \\\\\nt_{2} &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\nt_1 = 5 + 2 = 7 \\, \\textrm{s}\n$$\n\nFinally, by substituting\n\n$$\nt_1=7\\,\\textrm{s}\n$$\n\ninto the first equation for $s$, we find that the height of the cliff is\n\n$$\ns = 4.9(7)^2 = 240.1 \\, \\textrm{m}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186179958 :question/problem "Consider the line integral\n\n$\\displaystyle \\int_(C)32xyds =-\\frac{1}{4}\\displaystyle \\int_{-8}^{4}AAAAAdt$\nwhere $C$ is the arc of the parabola $y^{2} =-16x$ traversed from the point $(-4,-8)$ to the point $(-1, 4)$. Which of the following is the missing expression under the integral on the right?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y = t$.*" "Consider the line integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_(C)32xyds =-\\frac{1}{4}\\displaystyle \\int_{-8}^{4}AAAAAdt\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the arc of the parabola $y^{2} =-16x$ traversed from the point $(-4,-8)$ to the point $(-1, 4)$. Which of the following is the missing expression under the integral on the right?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y = t$.*"] [:db/cas 17592186179961 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\displaystyle \\int_(C)(x + y)ds$, where $C$ is the ellipse $4x^{2} + 9y^{2} = 36$, traversed once in the counterclockwise direction?" "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_(C)(x + y)ds,\n$$ where $C$ is the ellipse $4x^{2} + 9y^{2} = 36$, traversed once in the counterclockwise direction?"] [:db/cas 17592186179962 :question/problem "![](images/3b/3bf746cfb21805587a65c7beeac90e75464f630bab1fbf3453de69ca3e6639eb.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the line integral ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{4y}{x}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is the left half of the ellipse ${x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}=16,$ shown above?" "![](images/3b/3bf746cfb21805587a65c7beeac90e75464f630bab1fbf3453de69ca3e6639eb.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the line integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\frac{4y}{x}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is the left half of the ellipse ${x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}=16,$ shown above?"] [:db/cas 17592186179963 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral\n${\\int }_{C}\\frac{24x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s,$\nwhere $C$ is the arc of the parabola ${y}^{2}=8x$ from $(0,0)$ to $(\\frac{9}{8},3).$" "Evaluate the line integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\frac{24x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the arc of the parabola ${y}^{2}=8x$ from $(0,0)$ to $(\\frac{9}{8},3).$"] [:db/cas 17592186179969 :question/problem "![](images/dd/ddefdc9ada8270723d9be7ab7fe3f0b2a59b73fbe69d38b674f2fa5c43800e4c.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_(C)xy^{2}ds$, where $C$ is the section of the curve $x = 2y^{3}$ traversed between the points $(0, 0)$ and $(16, 2)$, as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y = t$.*" "![](images/dd/ddefdc9ada8270723d9be7ab7fe3f0b2a59b73fbe69d38b674f2fa5c43800e4c.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_(C)xy^{2}ds,\n$$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $x = 2y^{3}$ traversed between the points $(0, 0)$ and $(16, 2)$, as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y = t$.*"] [:db/cas 17592186179972 :question/problem "![](images/00/004a94ad9a64973b8e8d3edae709c75f0b76ea1a013d86dbfa87476b85742fc7.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n$\\displaystyle \\int_(C)4xyds$,\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x = \\sqrt{y}$ from $(0, 0)$ to $(\\sqrt{2}, 2)$, as shown above." "![](images/00/004a94ad9a64973b8e8d3edae709c75f0b76ea1a013d86dbfa87476b85742fc7.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_(C)4xyds,\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x = \\sqrt{y}$ from $(0, 0)$ to $(\\sqrt{2}, 2)$, as shown above."] [:db/cas 17592186179974 :question/problem "Let $P=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\}$ be a partition of the interval $[0,3].$ If $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1},$ calculate ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}5Δ{x}_{i}.$" "Let $P=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\}$ be a partition of the interval $[0,3].$ If $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1},$ calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}5Δ{x}_{i}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186179974 :question/worked-solution "Using the constant multiple rule, we can write the summation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}5Δ{x}_{i}=5{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}$ is just the length of the interval $[0,3],$ because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i} & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})+({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})+({x}_{3}-{x}_{2})+⋯+({x}_{m}-{x}_{m-1}) \\\\  & =(\\cancel{{x}_{1}}-{x}_{0})+(\\cancel{{x}_{2}}-\\cancel{{x}_{1}})+(\\cancel{{x}_{3}}-\\cancel{{x}_{2}})+⋯+({x}_{m}-\\cancel{{x}_{m-1}}) \\\\  & =-{x}_{0}+{x}_{m} \\\\  & ={x}_{m}-{x}_{0} \\\\  & =3-0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n5{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}=5\\cdot 3=15.\n$$" "Using the constant multiple rule, we can write the summation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}5Δ{x}_{i}=5{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}\n$$ is just the length of the interval $[0,3],$ because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i} & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})+({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})+({x}_{3}-{x}_{2})+⋯+({x}_{m}-{x}_{m-1}) \\\\  & =(\\cancel{{x}_{1}}-{x}_{0})+(\\cancel{{x}_{2}}-\\cancel{{x}_{1}})+(\\cancel{{x}_{3}}-\\cancel{{x}_{2}})+⋯+({x}_{m}-\\cancel{{x}_{m-1}}) \\\\  & =-{x}_{0}+{x}_{m} \\\\  & ={x}_{m}-{x}_{0} \\\\  & =3-0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n5{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}=5\\cdot 3=15.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186179975 :question/problem "Let $P=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\}$ be a partition of the interval $[0,2].$ If $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1},$ calculate ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}({x}_{i}+{x}_{i-1})Δ{x}_{i}.$" "Let $P=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\}$ be a partition of the interval $[0,2].$ If $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1},$ calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}({x}_{i}+{x}_{i-1})Δ{x}_{i}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186179978 :question/problem "Let $P=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ be a partition of the interval $[0,4].$ If $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1},$ calculate ${\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}8({y}_{j}+{y}_{j-1})Δ{y}_{j}.$" "Let $P=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ be a partition of the interval $[0,4].$ If $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1},$ calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}8({y}_{j}+{y}_{j-1})Δ{y}_{j}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186179979 :question/problem "Consider the following partitions:\n\n- ${P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\}$ is a partition of the interval $[0,2]$\n\n- ${P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is a partition of the interval $[1,6]$\n\nCalculate ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j},$ where $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1}.$" "Consider the following partitions:\n\n- ${P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\}$ is a partition of the interval $[0,2]$\n\n- ${P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is a partition of the interval $[1,6]$\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j},\n$$ where $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186179979 :question/worked-solution "Using the product rule, we can write the summation as\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}.\n$$\n\nNote that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}$ and ${\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}$ are just the lengths of the intervals $[0,2]$ and $[1,6]$ respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i} & =2-0=2, \\\\ {\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j} & =6-1=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining our results, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}=2\\cdot 5=10.\n$$" "Using the product rule, we can write the summation as\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}.\n$$\n\nNote that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}\n$$ are just the lengths of the intervals $[0,2]$ and $[1,6]$ respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i} & =2-0=2, \\\\ {\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j} & =6-1=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining our results, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}=2\\cdot 5=10.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186179983 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\displaystyle \\int_(C)xyds$, where $C$ is the ellipse $9x^{2} + y^{2} = 9$, traversed once in the counterclockwise direction?" "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_(C)xyds,\n$$ where $C$ is the ellipse $9x^{2} + y^{2} = 9$, traversed once in the counterclockwise direction?"] [:db/cas 17592186179984 :question/problem "![](images/0b/0b0f3ae9a464812957c297c09ff3f65bfe5964bdac70298e72ec3dbde2325e72.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the line integral ${\\int }_{C}(6{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is the right half of the ellipse $6{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=6,$ shown above?" "![](images/0b/0b0f3ae9a464812957c297c09ff3f65bfe5964bdac70298e72ec3dbde2325e72.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the line integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}(6{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is the right half of the ellipse $6{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=6,$ shown above?"] [:db/cas 17592186