the point $(0,3),$ as shown above."] [:db/cas 17592186180002 :question/problem "If ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x=4$ and ${\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x=6,$ calculate ${\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x$ given that $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" "If $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x=4\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x=6,\n$$ calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ given that $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186180002 :question/worked-solution "Let $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ be a piecewise smooth path in the $xy$-plane, and let $-C$ be the curve consisting of the same points as $C$ but with the opposite orientation. \n\nFor line integrals with respect to $x,$ we have the following properties:\n\n- ${\\int }_{-C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x$\n\n- ${\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4-6 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ be a piecewise smooth path in the $xy$-plane, and let $-C$ be the curve consisting of the same points as $C$ but with the opposite orientation. \n\nFor line integrals with respect to $x,$ we have the following properties:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4-6 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186180003 :question/problem "If ${\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=12$ and ${\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=8,$ calculate ${\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y$ given that $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" "If $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=12\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=8,\n$$ calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\n$$ given that $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186180003 :question/worked-solution "Let $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ be a piecewise smooth path in the $xy$-plane, and let $-C$ be the curve consisting of the same points as $C$ but with the opposite orientation. \n\nFor line integrals with respect to $y,$ we have the following properties:\n\n- ${\\int }_{-C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=-{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y$\n\n- ${\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y$\n\nIn our case, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\ 12 & =-{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\ 12 & =-8-{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\ 20 & =-{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=-20.\n$$" "Let $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ be a piecewise smooth path in the $xy$-plane, and let $-C$ be the curve consisting of the same points as $C$ but with the opposite orientation. \n\nFor line integrals with respect to $y,$ we have the following properties:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=-{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\ 12 & =-{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\ 12 & =-8-{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\ 20 & =-{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=-20.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186180007 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}(x+y)\\,dx+(x-y)\\,dy,$ where $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=4t\\,i+(4-4t)\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,1]?$" "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}(x+y)\\,dx+(x-y)\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=4t\\,i+(4-4t)\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,1]?$"] [:db/cas 17592186180008 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx-{x}^{2}\\,dy,$ where $C$ is part of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $A(1,0)$ to the point $B(0,1).$" "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx-{x}^{2}\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is part of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $A(1,0)$ to the point $B(0,1).$"] [:db/cas 17592186180009 :question/problem "If the path $C$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{MN}$ traversed from the point $M(-1,0)$ to the point $N(2,4),$ then ${\\int }_{C}y\\,dx-x\\,dy=$" "If the path $C$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{MN}$ traversed from the point $M(-1,0)$ to the point $N(2,4),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}y\\,dx-x\\,dy=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186180010 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)={e}^{x}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,j$ and $C$ is the line segment traversed from the point $A(3,2)$ to the point $B(1,5)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=(3-2t)\\,i+(2+3t)\\,j.$*" "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $F(x,y)={e}^{x}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,j$ and $C$ is the line segment traversed from the point $A(3,2)$ to the point $B(1,5)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=(3-2t)\\,i+(2+3t)\\,j.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186180011 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=(x+y)\\,i+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi y\\,j$ and $C$ is the line segment traversed from the point $A(1,-1)$ to the point $B(-3,1).$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=(x+y)\\,i+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi y\\,j$ and $C$ is the line segment traversed from the point $A(1,-1)$ to the point $B(-3,1).$"] [:db/cas 17592186180012 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j$ and $C$ is the curve $y=2{x}^{2}-1$ traversed from the point $A(1,1)$ to the point $B(2,7)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=t\\,i+(2{t}^{2}-1)\\,j.$*" "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j$ and $C$ is the curve $y=2{x}^{2}-1$ traversed from the point $A(1,1)$ to the point $B(2,7)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=t\\,i+(2{t}^{2}-1)\\,j.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186180013 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)\\,dx+(x+y)\\,dy,$ where $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=i+t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2]?$" "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)\\,dx+(x+y)\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=i+t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2]?$"] [:db/cas 17592186180014 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}2y\\,dx+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,dy+3z\\,dz,$ where $C$ is a portion of the curve $r(t)=\\,i+t\\,j+{t}^{2}\\,k$ for $t\\in [1,3]?$" "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}2y\\,dx+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,dy+3z\\,dz,\n$$ where $C$ is a portion of the curve $r(t)=\\,i+t\\,j+{t}^{2}\\,k$ for $t\\in [1,3]?$"] [:db/cas 17592186180015 :question/problem "Evaluate ${∮}_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=y\\,i-x\\,j$ and $C$ is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, traversed once in the counterclockwise direction." "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=y\\,i-x\\,j$ and $C$ is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, traversed once in the counterclockwise direction."] [:db/cas 17592186180016 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,i+y\\,j$ and $C$ is the curve $y=2{sin}^{2}⁡x$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to the point $B(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2).$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,i+y\\,j$ and $C$ is the curve $y=2{sin}^{2}⁡x$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to the point $B(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2).$"] [:db/cas 17592186180017 :question/problem "![](images/b7/b70d2d8824cfbebbff2807239cf9b98796b43e8cb0f74e7e43ae25eb28b79086.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path along the segment $AB^(―)$ traversed from $A$ to $B$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\displaystyle \\int_{C}F \\cdot dr > 0$\n2. $\\displaystyle \\int_{C}F \\cdot dr < 0$\n3. $\\displaystyle \\int_{-C}F \\cdot dr > 0$" "![](images/b7/b70d2d8824cfbebbff2807239cf9b98796b43e8cb0f74e7e43ae25eb28b79086.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path along the segment $AB^(―)$ traversed from $A$ to $B$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{C}F \\cdot dr > 0\n$$\n2. $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{C}F \\cdot dr < 0\n$$\n3. $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-C}F \\cdot dr > 0\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186180018 :question/problem "Let $F(x,y,z)={y}^{2}\\,i-{x}^{2}\\,j+5\\,k$ and let $C$ be a path in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r?$" "Let $F(x,y,z)={y}^{2}\\,i-{x}^{2}\\,j+5\\,k$ and let $C$ be a path in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186180019 :question/worked-solution "Remember that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion  of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$ is false, while statement ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ is true. Since the force $F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/20/20121d30445c9e756f1c2c024713bc6cd6f0464cfcfc24df68bc30c766c4988e.png)\n\n \nSo, ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\n- Statement ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ is true. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/0f/0ff98536c275458d68deabda91f56bf63e7c8f59f5ea79c68292332d8cd58d4f.png)\n\nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$; ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ only." "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion  of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is false, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ is true. Since the force $F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/20/20121d30445c9e756f1c2c024713bc6cd6f0464cfcfc24df68bc30c766c4988e.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ is true. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/0f/0ff98536c275458d68deabda91f56bf63e7c8f59f5ea79c68292332d8cd58d4f.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186180020 :question/worked-solution "Remember that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$ is false while statement ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/4c/4c11b38f9e598e0cd8fc50715d2091e5bea48d188440ae8a99c84d66a6acb6b9.png)\n\n \nSo, ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\n- Statement ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/34/346007ae582fccdf4519a3d1cd50a467e19d5345238343a1311fd3348bbeb138.png)\n\n \nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$; ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$ only." "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is false while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/4c/4c11b38f9e598e0cd8fc50715d2091e5bea48d188440ae8a99c84d66a6acb6b9.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/34/346007ae582fccdf4519a3d1cd50a467e19d5345238343a1311fd3348bbeb138.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186180022 :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$ is false, while statement ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves clockwise along $C.$ \n\n![](images/db/dbb6dbdfabccf7a94c67781210238c647e6f3a2e428cb79c4debdbff5e124720.png)\n\nSo, $Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- Statement ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$ is false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves counterclockwise along $C.$\n\n![](images/1c/1c5aa77b0a89e1fcb0782c9d969d3a81bbec47b004f07bd3a53ccd10e623ba6e.png)\n\nSo, $Γ={∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$ only." "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is false, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves clockwise along $C.$ \n\n![](images/db/dbb6dbdfabccf7a94c67781210238c647e6f3a2e428cb79c4debdbff5e124720.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves counterclockwise along $C.$\n\n![](images/1c/1c5aa77b0a89e1fcb0782c9d969d3a81bbec47b004f07bd3a53ccd10e623ba6e.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle Γ={∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186180023 :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statements ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$ and ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ are false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves counterclockwise along $C.$ \n\n![](images/bc/bc0121782b16d513d7d17d3741bd07ef1716e096bb2459fbda0cb841c7fd8b43.png)\n\nSo, ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\n- Statement ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves **clockwise** along $-C.$\n\n![](images/1a/1a6b109bc1da3aa7905b05f7f7902523b45fd51a65b97bec1328037bd6c0cfe7.png)\n\nSo, ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$ only." "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statements $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ are false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves counterclockwise along $C.$ \n\n![](images/bc/bc0121782b16d513d7d17d3741bd07ef1716e096bb2459fbda0cb841c7fd8b43.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves **clockwise** along $-C.$\n\n![](images/1a/1a6b109bc1da3aa7905b05f7f7902523b45fd51a65b97bec1328037bd6c0cfe7.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186180024 :question/worked-solution "Remember that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$ is false, while statement ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/d3/d363cf00c4901fa0c29bb9521cdd2ea3c12c377dd8e38306df0d5a2837915a36.png)\n\n \nSo, ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\n- Statement ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that if we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/c5/c5799e631e7eb45ec5a9711ef1569d51b376d8c764a3226caa2676cdb968a6f5.png)\n\n \nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$; ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$ only." "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is false, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/d3/d363cf00c4901fa0c29bb9521cdd2ea3c12c377dd8e38306df0d5a2837915a36.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that if we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/c5/c5799e631e7eb45ec5a9711ef1569d51b376d8c764a3226caa2676cdb968a6f5.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186180025 :question/problem "If ${\\int }_{C}z\\,\\text{d}x+x\\,\\text{d}y+y\\,\\text{d}z$ is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ for some piecewise smooth curve $C,$ then $F(x,y,z)=$" "If $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}z\\,\\text{d}x+x\\,\\text{d}y+y\\,\\text{d}z\n$$ is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ for some piecewise smooth curve $C,$ then $F(x,y,z)=$"] [:db/cas 17592186180026 :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a conservative vector field on ${R}^{2},$ then the circulation $Γ=0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in {R}^{2},$ that is \n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$∇f$ is conservative” is true. The vector field $∇f$ is conservative by definition.\n\n- Statement “${∮}_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}$” is true, while statement “${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any piecewise smooth path $C\\in {R}^{2}$” is false. The result $Γ=0$ applies to closed curves only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$∇f$ is conservative”; “${∮}_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}$” only." "If $F$ is a conservative vector field on ${R}^{2},$ then the circulation $Γ=0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in {R}^{2},$ that is \n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$∇f$ is conservative” is true. The vector field $∇f$ is conservative by definition.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}$” is true, while statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ for any piecewise smooth path $C\\in {R}^{2}$” is false. The result $Γ=0$ applies to closed curves only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$∇f$ is conservative”; “$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186180027 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=-\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i-\\frac{2y}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "Consider the vector field $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y)=-\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i-\\frac{2y}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j.\n$$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186180027 :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a conservative vector field on ${R}^{2},$ then the circulation $Γ=0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in {R}^{2},$ that is \n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nWe notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{4xy}{(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$F$ is conservative” is true. The vector field $F$ is conservative since the partial derivatives are equal and continuous everywhere on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Statement “${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any piecewise smooth path $C\\in {R}^{2}$” is false, while statement “${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}$” is true. The result $Γ=0$ applies to closed curves only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$F$ is conservative”; “${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}$” only." "If $F$ is a conservative vector field on ${R}^{2},$ then the circulation $Γ=0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in {R}^{2},$ that is \n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nWe notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{4xy}{(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$F$ is conservative” is true. The vector field $F$ is conservative since the partial derivatives are equal and continuous everywhere on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ for any piecewise smooth path $C\\in {R}^{2}$” is false, while statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}$” is true. The result $Γ=0$ applies to closed curves only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$F$ is conservative”; “$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186180044 :question/problem "Find the value of $\\frac{1}{8} \\div \\left(-\\frac{1}{24}\\right)$." "Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{8} \\div \\left(-\\frac{1}{24}\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186180045 :question/problem "What is the value of $\\left(-\\frac{5}{8}\\right) \\div \\left(-\\frac{3}{4}\\right)$?" "What is the value of $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(-\\frac{5}{8}\\right) \\div \\left(-\\frac{3}{4}\\right)\n$$?"] [:db/cas 17592186180054 :question/problem "Find the generating function for the sequence that satisfies the recurrence relation $a_{n} - 2a_{n - 1} = 1 - 5n$ with $a_{0} = 3$.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $∑_(n = 1)^(∞)nx^{n} = (x)/((1 - x)^{2})$.*" "Find the generating function for the sequence that satisfies the recurrence relation $a_{n} - 2a_{n - 1} = 1 - 5n$ with $a_{0} = 3$.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)nx^{n} = (x)/((1 - x)^{2}).\n$$*"] [:db/cas 17592186180055 :question/problem "Find the generating function for the sequence that satisfies the recurrence relation $a_{n} - a_{n - 1} = 2n$ with $a_{0} = 1$.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $∑_(n = 1)^(∞)nx^{n} = (x)/((1 - x)^{2})$.*" "Find the generating function for the sequence that satisfies the recurrence relation $a_{n} - a_{n - 1} = 2n$ with $a_{0} = 1$.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)nx^{n} = (x)/((1 - x)^{2}).\n$$*"] [:db/cas 17592186180089 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-3)-6\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $T$ is non-invertible and the image of the square under the action of $T$ is a line segment." "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-3)-6\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $T$ is non-invertible and the image of the square under the action of $T$ is a line segment."] [:db/cas 17592186180170 :question/problem "Evaluate $lim_(x → π/3)(\\frac{π}{3} - x)\\csc (x - \\frac{π}{3})$." "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle lim_(x → π/3)(\\frac{π}{3} - x)\\csc (x - \\frac{π}{3}).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186180172 :question/problem "![](images/48/4807f92defb4202359a32ca046073cd138db396a0975164e42b96126ac0cc591.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)={e}^{x}\\,i+{e}^{-2y}\\,j$ and $C$ is the arc of a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin traversed cou