\\\\ 9\\end{array}} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} {\\color{blue}\\begin{array}{c} \\\\ 12\\end{array}} \\\\  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\cancel{2} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\cancel{3} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\cancel{5} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\cancel{4} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\cancel{7} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\cancel{0} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 2 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} - \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 1 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 4 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 4 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 6 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 2 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 5 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 4 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hline  &  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 9 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 0 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 8 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 4 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 4 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 8 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em}\\end{array}}\n$$\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n2,354,702 - 1,446,254 = 908,448\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186188206 :question/worked-solution "Suppose $a_n$ is a positive sequence defined for\n\n$$\nn \\geq 1\n$$\n\nand that\n\n$$\nL = \\dfrac{a_{n+1}}{a_n}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- if $L \\geq 1$ for all $n \\geq 1$, then $a_n$ is *increasing,* and\n- if $0 < L \\leq 1$ for all $n \\geq 1$, then $a_n$ is *decreasing.*\n\nNotice that, in this case, $a_n = 3^{n}$ is positive for all\n\n$$\nn \\geq 1\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the ratio $L$, we get\n$\\frac{a_{n + 1}}{a_{n}}|= \\frac{3^{n + 1}}{3^{n}}; = 3^{n + 1 - n}; = 3^{1}; = 3; \\ge 1$.\n- Statement II is true, whereas statement III is false. Since $L \\geq 1$ for all $n \\geq 1$, we can conclude that the sequence $a_n$ is increasing.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "Suppose $a_n$ is a positive sequence defined for\n\n$$\nn \\geq 1\n$$\n\nand that\n\n$$\nL = \\dfrac{a_{n+1}}{a_n}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- if $L \\geq 1$ for all $n \\geq 1$, then $a_n$ is *increasing,* and\n- if $0 < L \\leq 1$ for all $n \\geq 1$, then $a_n$ is *decreasing.*\n\nNotice that, in this case, $a_n = 3^{n}$ is positive for all\n\n$$\nn \\geq 1\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the ratio $L$, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{a_{n + 1}}{a_{n}}|= \\frac{3^{n + 1}}{3^{n}}; = 3^{n + 1 - n}; = 3^{1}; = 3; \\ge 1.\n$$\n- Statement II is true, whereas statement III is false. Since $L \\geq 1$ for all $n \\geq 1$, we can conclude that the sequence $a_n$ is increasing.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186188207 :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation from $\\mathbb{R}^4$ into $\\mathbb{R}^4,$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad\n\\left\\{\\;\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{Im} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = {\\mathbb{R}}^{4} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{rank} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = 4 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{Ker} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = \\{  \\mathbf{0} \\}  \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{nullity} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = 0\\end{array}} \\right.$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) + \\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}) = 4.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})=\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3\\}$ and the set $\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3 \\}$ is linearly independent, we get that $\\textrm{rank}(T)=\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T})=3.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =4-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =4-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq 4$ (or since $\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq 0$), the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ cannot be a bijection according to the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, the correct option is \"I only.\"" "Since $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation from $\\mathbb{R}^4$ into $\\mathbb{R}^4,$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $$\n\\displaystyle \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad\n\\left\\{\\;\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{Im} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = {\\mathbb{R}}^{4} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{rank} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = 4 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{Ker} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = \\{  \\mathbf{0} \\}  \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{nullity} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = 0\\end{array}} \\right.\n$$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) + \\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}) = 4.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})=\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3\\}$ and the set $\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3 \\}$ is linearly independent, we get that $\\textrm{rank}(T)=\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T})=3.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =4-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =4-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq 4$ (or since $\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq 0$), the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ cannot be a bijection according to the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, the correct option is \"I only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186188214 :question/problem "The weights, in kilograms, of five randomly selected 2-year-old children visiting a pediatrician were measured. The results are shown below.\n\n$$\n12.5, \\quad 11.7,\\quad 13.2,\\quad 12.8, \\quad 12.1\n$$\n\nCalculate the value of the statistic $\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^2$ for this particular sample." "The weights, in kilograms, of five randomly selected 2-year-old children visiting a pediatrician were measured. The results are shown below.\n\n$$\n12.5, \\quad 11.7,\\quad 13.2,\\quad 12.8, \\quad 12.1\n$$\n\nCalculate the value of the statistic $$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^2\n$$ for this particular sample."] [:db/cas 17592186188215 :question/problem "A sample $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{8}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population standard deviation $\\sigma.$ If $\\mu$ and $\\sigma$ are both unknown, which of the following are statistics?\n\nI. $\\displaystyle \\min(X_1, X_2, \\dots, X_8)$\n\nII. $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{4} X_{2i}^2$\n\nIII. $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{8} \\left( \\dfrac{X_i}{\\sigma} - 1 \\right)^2$" "A sample $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{8}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population standard deviation $\\sigma.$ If $\\mu$ and $\\sigma$ are both unknown, which of the following are statistics?\n\nI. $\\displaystyle \\min(X_1, X_2, \\dots, X_8)$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{4} X_{2i}^2\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{8} \\left( \\dfrac{X_i}{\\sigma} - 1 \\right)^2\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186188545 :question/problem "For what values of $p$ will both $∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\left(\\frac{3p}{4}\\right)^{n}$ and $∑_(n = 1)^(∞)((-1)^{n + 1})/(n^{6 - 2p})$ converge?" "For what values of $p$ will both $$\n\\displaystyle ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\left(\\frac{3p}{4}\\right)^{n}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)((-1)^{n + 1})/(n^{6 - 2p})\n$$ converge?"] [:db/cas 17592186188547 :question/problem "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables  $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{X}(x)=\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}$ for $x\\in [0,1]$\n\nII. $f(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables  $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle {f}_{X}(x)=\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\n$$ for $x\\in [0,1]$\n\nII. $f(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent"] [:db/cas 17592186188548 :question/problem "A sample $X_{1}, X_{2}, …, X_{10}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $μ$ and population variance $σ^{2}$. If $μ$ and $σ$ are both unknown, which of the following are statistics?\n\n1. $\\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(10)X_{i}$\n2. $∑_(i = 1)^(10)(X_{i} - μ)^{2}$\n3. $X_{1} + 2X_{2} + X_{3}$" "A sample $X_{1}, X_{2}, …, X_{10}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $μ$ and population variance $σ^{2}$. If $μ$ and $σ$ are both unknown, which of the following are statistics?\n\n1. $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(10)X_{i}\n$$\n2. $$\n\\displaystyle ∑_(i = 1)^(10)(X_{i} - μ)^{2}\n$$\n3. $X_{1} + 2X_{2} + X_{3}$"] [:db/cas 17592186188565 :question/problem "A sample $X_{1}, X_{2}, …, X_{10}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $μ$ and population variance $σ^{2}$. If $μ$ is known and $σ$ is unknown, which of the following are statistics?\n\n1. $\\frac{1}{10}∑_(i = 1)^(10)X_{i}$\n2. $∑_(i = 1)^(10)(X_{i} - μ)^{2}$\n3. $∑_(i = 1)^(10)\\left(\\frac{X_{i} - μ}{σ}\\right)^{2}$" "A sample $X_{1}, X_{2}, …, X_{10}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $μ$ and population variance $σ^{2}$. If $μ$ is known and $σ$ is unknown, which of the following are statistics?\n\n1. $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{10}∑_(i = 1)^(10)X_{i}\n$$\n2. $$\n\\displaystyle ∑_(i = 1)^(10)(X_{i} - μ)^{2}\n$$\n3. $$\n\\displaystyle ∑_(i = 1)^(10)\\left(\\frac{X_{i} - μ}{σ}\\right)^{2}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186188634 :question/problem "${f}_{X | Y} ( x \\hspace{0.167em} | \\hspace{0.167em} 3 ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3}} , & x = 12 \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{3}} , & x = 18 \\\\ 0 , & \\text{otherwise}\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nSuppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Find the conditional variance $Var\\begin{bmatrix}X & Y = 3\\end{bmatrix}$ given that the conditional probability mass function $f_{X\\mid Y}(x \\mid 3)$ is shown above and the corresponding conditional expected value is $μ_{X \\mid 3} = 16$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." "$$\n\\displaystyle {f}_{X | Y} ( x \\hspace{0.167em} | \\hspace{0.167em} 3 ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3}} , & x = 12 \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{3}} , & x = 18 \\\\ 0 , & \\text{otherwise}\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nSuppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Find the conditional variance $Var\\begin{bmatrix}X & Y = 3\\end{bmatrix}$ given that the conditional probability mass function $f_{X\\mid Y}(x \\mid 3)$ is shown above and the corresponding conditional expected value is $μ_{X \\mid 3} = 16$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186188655 :question/problem "${f}_{X | Y} ( x \\hspace{0.167em} | \\hspace{0.167em} 2 ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{x}{4}} , & \\hspace{1em} 1 \\le  x \\le  3 \\\\ 0 , & \\hspace{1em} \\text{otherwise}\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables.\nFind the conditional variance $Var\\begin{bmatrix}X & Y = 2\\end{bmatrix}$ given that the conditional probability density function $f_{X\\mid Y}(x \\mid 2)$ is shown above and the corresponding expected value is $μ_{X \\mid 2} = \\frac{13}{6}$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." "$$\n\\displaystyle {f}_{X | Y} ( x \\hspace{0.167em} | \\hspace{0.167em} 2 ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{x}{4}} , & \\hspace{1em} 1 \\le  x \\le  3 \\\\ 0 , & \\hspace{1em} \\text{otherwise}\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables.\nFind the conditional variance $Var\\begin{bmatrix}X & Y = 2\\end{bmatrix}$ given that the conditional probability density function $f_{X\\mid Y}(x \\mid 2)$ is shown above and the corresponding expected value is $μ_{X \\mid 2} = \\frac{13}{6}$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186188721 :question/problem "${f}_{Y | X} ( y \\hspace{0.167em} | \\hspace{0.167em} 2 ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{2}} {y}^{2} , & \\hspace{1em} - 1 \\le  y \\le  1 \\\\ 0 , & \\hspace{1em} \\text{otherwise}\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables.\nFind the conditional variance $Var\\begin{bmatrix}Y & X = 2\\end{bmatrix}$ given that the conditional probability density function $f_{Y\\mid X}(y \\mid 2)$ is shown above and the corresponding expected value is $μ_{Y \\mid 2} = 0$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." "$$\n\\displaystyle {f}_{Y | X} ( y \\hspace{0.167em} | \\hspace{0.167em} 2 ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{2}} {y}^{2} , & \\hspace{1em} - 1 \\le  y \\le  1 \\\\ 0 , & \\hspace{1em} \\text{otherwise}\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables.\nFind the conditional variance $Var\\begin{bmatrix}Y & X = 2\\end{bmatrix}$ given that the conditional probability density function $f_{Y\\mid X}(y \\mid 2)$ is shown above and the corresponding expected value is $μ_{Y \\mid 2} = 0$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186188745 :question/problem "Suppose the following random sample was drawn from the uniform distribution $U[0, 1]$.\n\n$0.224, 0.389, 0.701$\n\nUse these numbers to generate three random numbers that follow a probability distribution with the CDF $F(x)$, given by\n\n$F ( x ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}0 , & \\hspace{1em} x < 2 , \\\\ 1 - {\\displaystyle \\frac{16}{( 2 + x {)}^{2}}} , & \\hspace{1em} x \\ge  2.\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nRound your answers to $3$ decimal places.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." "Suppose the following random sample was drawn from the uniform distribution $U[0, 1]$.\n\n$0.224, 0.389, 0.701$\n\nUse these numbers to generate three random numbers that follow a probability distribution with the CDF $F(x)$, given by\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle F ( x ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}0 , & \\hspace{1em} x < 2 , \\\\ 1 - {\\displaystyle \\frac{16}{( 2 + x {)}^{2}}} , & \\hspace{1em} x \\ge  2.\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nRound your answers to $3$ decimal places.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186188751 :question/problem "Suppose the following random sample was drawn from the uniform distribution $U[0, 1]$.\n\n$0.103, 0.534, 0.847$\n\nUse these numbers to generate three random numbers that follow a probability distribution with the CDF $F(x)$, given by\n\n$F(x) = \\frac{\\arctan x}{π} + \\frac{1}{2}, x ∈ (- ∞, ∞)$.\n\nRound your answers to $3$ decimal places.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." "Suppose the following random sample was drawn from the uniform distribution $U[0, 1]$.\n\n$0.103, 0.534, 0.847$\n\nUse these numbers to generate three random numbers that follow a probability distribution with the CDF $F(x)$, given by\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle F(x) = \\frac{\\arctan x}{π} + \\frac{1}{2}, x ∈ (- ∞, ∞).\n$$\n\nRound your answers to $3$ decimal places.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186188753 :question/problem "Suppose the following random sample was drawn from the uniform distribution $U[0, 1]$.\n\n$0.231, 0.412, 0.602$\nUse these numbers to generate three random numbers that follow a probability distribution with the PDF $f(x)$, given by\n\n$f ( x ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{ {x}^{2}}{72}} , \\hspace{1em} & 0 \\le  x \\le  6 , \\\\ 0 , \\hspace{1em} & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nRound your answers to $3$ decimal places.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." "Suppose the following random sample was drawn from the uniform distribution $U[0, 1]$.\n\n$0.231, 0.412, 0.602$\nUse these numbers to generate three random numbers that follow a probability distribution with the PDF $f(x)$, given by\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle f ( x ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{ {x}^{2}}{72}} , \\hspace{1em} & 0 \\le  x \\le  6 , \\\\ 0 , \\hspace{1em} & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nRound your answers to $3$ decimal places.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186188767 :question/problem "Suppose the following random sample was drawn from the uniform distribution $U[0, 1]$.\n$0.241, 0.532, 0.824$\nUse these numbers to generate three random numbers that follow a probability distribution with the PDF $f(x)$, given by\n$f ( x ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}\\sin   x , \\hspace{1em} & 0 \\le  x \\le  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\pi }{2}} , \\\\ 0 , \\hspace{1em} & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{array} \\right.$\nRound your answers to $3$ decimal places.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." "Suppose the following random sample was drawn from the uniform distribution $U[0, 1]$.\n$0.241, 0.532, 0.824$\nUse these numbers to generate three random numbers that follow a probability distribution with the PDF $f(x)$, given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle f ( x ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}\\sin   x , \\hspace{1em} & 0 \\le  x \\le  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\pi }{2}} , \\\\ 0 , \\hspace{1em} & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\nRound your answers to $3$ decimal places.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186188788 :question/problem "$Σ = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & 1 \\\\ a & 5 & 3 \\\\ b & c & 6\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe covariance matrix $Σ$ for some random variables $X_{1}, X_{2}$, and $X_{3}$ is given above. Find $a + b + c$." "$$\n\\displaystyle Σ = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & 1 \\\\ a & 5 & 3 \\\\ b & c & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe covariance matrix $Σ$ for some random variables $X_{1}, X_{2}$, and $X_{3}$ is given above. Find $a + b + c$."] [:db/cas 17592186188828 :question/problem "![](images/51/51611d844d5334e9c0ec443d892b51eca73b4790163d1fb5fdbc998d6e194e2d.png)\n\nWhat number is missing from the statement below?\n${\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{10}} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\boxed{\\phantom{\\hspace{0.167em} 0 \\hspace{0.167em}}}}{100}}$" "![](images/51/51611d844d5334e9c0ec443d892b51eca73b4790163d1fb5fdbc998d6e194e2d.png)\n\nWhat number is missing from the statement below?\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{10}} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\boxed{\\phantom{\\hspace{0.167em} 0 \\hspace{0.167em}}}}{100}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186188830 :question/problem "What is the missing number in the following equality?\n\n${\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{10}} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\hspace{0.167em} \\boxed{\\phantom{0}}}{100}}$" "What is the missing number in the following equality?\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{10}} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\hspace{0.167em} \\boxed{\\phantom{0}}}{100}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186188844 :question/problem "$Σ = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ a & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe covariance matrix $Σ$ for some random variables $X_{1}$ and $X_{2}$ is given above. Find $a$." "$$\n\\displaystyle Σ = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ a & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe covariance matrix $Σ$ for some random variables $X_{1}$ and $X_{2}$ is given above. Find $a$."] [:db/cas 17592186188904 :question/problem "Let $X_{1}, X_{2}, …, X_{57}∼U[2, 4]$ be an I.I.D. sample of size $57$ from the continuous uniform distribution on the interval $[2, 4]$. Suppose we define the random variable $X$ as\n$X = ∑_(i = 1)^(57)X_{i}$.\n\nWhat is the approximate distribution of $X$?\n\nThe approximate distribution is $X∼N($\n{{field-1}} $$,\n{{field-2}}\n$)$." "Let $X_{1}, X_{2}, …, X_{57}∼U[2, 4]$ be an I.I.D. sample of size $57$ from the continuous uniform distribution on the interval $[2, 4]$. Suppose we define the random variable $X$ as\n$$\n\\displaystyle X = ∑_(i = 1)^(57)X_{i}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the approximate distribution of $X$?\n\nThe approximate distribution is $X∼N($\n{{field-1}} $$,\n{{field-2}}\n$)$."] [:db/cas 17592186188907 :question/problem "Let $X_{i}∼Geom\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)$ for $i = 1, 2, …, 60$ be independent geometric random variables. Suppose we define the random variable $X$ as\n$X = ∑_(i = 1)^(60)X_{i}$.\nWhat is the approximate distribution of $X$?The approximate distribution is $X∼N($\n{{field-1}} $$,\n{{field-2}}\n$)$." "Let $X_{i}∼Geom\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)$ for $i = 1, 2, …, 60$ be independent geometric random variables. Suppose we define the random variable $X$ as\n$$\n\\displaystyle X = ∑_(i = 1)^(60)X_{i}.\n$$\nWhat is the approximate distribution of $X$?The approximate distribution is $X∼N($\n{{field-1}} $$,\n{{field-2}}\n$)$."] [:db/cas 17592186188908 :question/problem "Let $X_{i}∼Po(3)$ for $i = 1, 2, …, 120$ be independent Poisson random variables. Suppose we define the random variable $X$ as\n$X = ∑_(i = 1)^(120)X_{i}$.\nWhat is the approximate distribution of $X$?The approximate distribution is $X∼N($\n{{field-1}} $$,\n{{field-2}}\n$)$." "Let $X_{i}∼Po(3)$ for $i = 1, 2, …, 120$ be independent Poisson random variables. Suppose we define the random variable $X$ as\n$$\n\\displaystyle X = ∑_(i = 1)^(120)X_{i}.\n$$\nWhat is the approximate distribution of $X$?The approximate distribution is $X∼N($\n{{field-1}} $$,\n{{field-2}}\n$)$."] [:db/cas 17592186188909 :question/problem "Let $X_{i}∼B(15, 0.6)$ for $i = 1, 2, …, 60$ be independent binomial random variables. Suppose we define the random variable $X$ as\n$X = ∑_(i = 1)^(60)X_{i}$.\nWhat is the approximate distribution of $X$?The approximate distribution is $X∼N($\n{{field-1}} $$,\n{{field-2}}\n$)$." "Let $X_{i}∼B(15, 0.6)$ for $i = 1, 2, …, 60$ be independent binomial random variables. Suppose we define the random variable $X$ as\n$$\n\\displaystyle X = ∑_(i = 1)^(60)X_{i}.\n$$\nWhat is the approximate distribution of $X$?The approximate distribution is $X∼N($\n{{field-1}} $$,\n{{field-2}}\n$)$."] [:db/cas 17592186188911 :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{5}$ into ${R}^{5},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=5 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=5.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(T)=1$ is false. Since $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ and the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent, we get that $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=3.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{nullity}(T)=2$ is true. According to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =5-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =5-3 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$T$ is a bijection” is false. Since $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 5$ (or since $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$), the linear transformation $T$ cannot be a bijection according to the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, the correct option is $\\text{nullity}(T)=2$ only." "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{5}$ into ${R}^{5},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=5 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=5.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(T)=1$ is false. Since $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ and the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent, we get that $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=3.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{nullity}(T)=2$ is true. According to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =5-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =5-3 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$T$ is a bijection” is false. Since $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 5$ (or since $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$), the linear transformation $T$ cannot be a bijection according to the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, the correct option is $\\text{nullity}(T)=2$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186188925 :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “If $\\begin{gathered}\\text{Im}(T)=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}\\,[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\\\\\,3x+2y-z=0\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$” is true. Solving $3x+2y-z=0$ for $z,$ we get $z=3x+2y.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ 3x+2y\\end{bmatrix}=x\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{{v}_{1}}+y\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=2.$ Finally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “If ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are two different solutions to $Tx=[1,-2,1]^{T}$ then $T$ is invertible” is false. If ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are two different solutions to $Tx=[1,-2,1]^{T},$ then \n\n$$\nT{v}_{1}=T{v}_{2}\\,⟹\\,T({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}-{v}_{2}$ is a non-zero vector. This means that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Null}(T)\\ne {0}$ and, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $T$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement “If ${v}_{1},$ ${v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the columns of $T$ and ${v}_{3}={v}_{1}+2{v}_{2}$, then $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$” is false. If the columns of $T$ are such that ${v}_{3}={v}_{1}+2{v}_{2},$ then the set of vectors ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly dependent and therefore $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$ Now, according to the matrix invertible theorem, we obtain that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “If $\\begin{gathered}\\text{Im}(T)=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}\\,[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\\\\\,3x+2y-z=0\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$” only." "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “If $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}\\text{Im}(T)=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}\\,[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\\\\\,3x+2y-z=0\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}\n$$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$” is true. Solving $3x+2y-z=0$ for $z,$ we get $z=3x+2y.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ 3x+2y\\end{bmatrix}=x\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{{v}_{1}}+y\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=2.$ Finally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “If ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are two different solutions to $Tx=[1,-2,1]^{T}$ then $T$ is invertible” is false. If ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are two different solutions to $Tx=[1,-2,1]^{T},$ then \n\n$$\nT{v}_{1}=T{v}_{2}\\,⟹\\,T({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}-{v}_{2}$ is a non-zero vector. This means that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Null}(T)\\ne {0}$ and, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $T$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement “If ${v}_{1},$ ${v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the columns of $T$ and ${v}_{3}={v}_{1}+2{v}_{2}$, then $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$” is false. If the columns of $T$ are such that ${v}_{3}={v}_{1}+2{v}_{2},$ then the set of vectors ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly dependent and therefore $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$ Now, according to the matrix invertible theorem, we obtain that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “If $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}\\text{Im}(T)=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}\\,[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\\\\\,3x+2y-z=0\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}\n$$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186188926 :question/problem "For which values of $p$ do both of the series $∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{1}{n^{2p - 1}}$ and $∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{1}{n^{5 - p}}$ converge?" "For which values of $p$ do both of the series $$\n\\displaystyle ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{1}{n^{2p - 1}}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{1}{n^{5 - p}}\n$$ converge?"] [:db/cas 17592186188928 :question/problem "For what values of $p$ will both $∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\left(\\frac{p}{4}\\right)^{n}$ and $∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\left(\\frac{4}{n}\\right)^{p}$ converge?" "For what values of $p$ will both $$\n\\displaystyle ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\left(\\frac{p}{4}\\right)^{n}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\left(\\frac{4}{n}\\right)^{p}\n$$ converge?"] [:db/cas 17592186188929 :question/problem "For which values of $p$ is the following sum convergent?\n\n$∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{1}{n^{2p - 3}} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{1}{n^{6 - p}}$" "For which values of $p$ is the following sum convergent?\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{1}{n^{2p - 3}} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{1}{n^{6 - p}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186188930 :question/problem "For what values of $p$ will both $∑_(n = 1)^(∞)5\\left(\\frac{p}{3}\\right)^{n}$ and $∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{1}{n^{2p}}$ converge?" "For what values of $p$ will both $$\n\\displaystyle ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)5\\left(\\frac{p}{3}\\right)^{n}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{1}{n^{2p}}\n$$ converge?"] [:db/cas 17592186188931 :question/problem "For what values of $p$ will both $∑_(n = 1)^(∞)((-1)^{n + 1})/(n^{2p + 1})$ and $∑_(n = 1)^(∞)p^{2n + 1}$ converge?" "For what values of $p$ will both $$\n\\displaystyle ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)((-1)^{n + 1})/(n^{2p + 1})\n$$ and $∑_(n = 1)^(∞)p^{2n + 1}$ converge?"] [:db/cas 17592186188984 :question/problem "![](images/51/51eb14498e739c0761f03e96709988430d339f462131dc68ec8bc9de4f6e52fb.png)\n\nConsider the polar curve $r = 1 - \\cos θ$, shown above. Given that\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{\\cos θ + a\\cos 2θ}{b\\sin 2θ - \\sin θ}$\n\nfind the value of $a + b$." "![](images/51/51eb14498e739c0761f03e96709988430d339f462131dc68ec8bc9de4f6e52fb.png)\n\nConsider the polar curve $r = 1 - \\cos θ$, shown above. Given that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{\\cos θ + a\\cos 2θ}{b\\sin 2θ - \\sin θ}\n$$\n\nfind the value of $a + b$."] [:db/cas 17592186189372 :tutorial/content "Let's remind ourselves of some convergence tests for infinite series:\n\n- The $p$-series test states that\n$\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\dfrac 1 {n^p}$\nis convergent for $p > 1$.\n- The geometric series\n$\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} a r^n$\nis convergent for $\\mid r \\mid < 1$.\n\nWe often wish to determine some parameter values that cause two series to converge simultaneously.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the following series:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{n^{3p+2}}, \\qquad \\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{n^{p-1}}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find the values of $p$ for which *both* series converge, The idea is to examine the convergence behavior of each series individually and then find the intersection of the solutions.\n\nNotice that both series are $p$-series. Therefore, we will use the $p$-series test on both series:\n\n- For the first series to converge, we require\n$3p+2 > 1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad p\\in \\left(-\\dfrac13,\\infty\\right)$.\n- For the second series to converge, we require\n$p-1> 1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad p\\in (2, \\infty)$.\n\nFinding the intersection of the two solutions gives\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\dfrac13,\\infty\\right) \\cap (2, \\infty) = (2, \\infty)\n$$\n\nTherefore, both series converge for $p \\in (2, \\infty)$." "Let's remind ourselves of some convergence tests for infinite series:\n\n- The $p$-series test states that\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\dfrac 1 {n^p}\n$$\nis convergent for $p > 1$.\n- The geometric series\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} a r^n\n$$\nis convergent for $\\mid r \\mid < 1$.\n\nWe often wish to determine some parameter values that cause two series to converge simultaneously.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the following series:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{n^{3p+2}}, \\qquad \\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{n^{p-1}}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find the values of $p$ for which *both* series converge, The idea is to examine the convergence behavior of each series individually and then find the intersection of the solutions.\n\nNotice that both series are $p$-series. Therefore, we will use the $p$-series test on both series:\n\n- For the first series to converge, we require\n$3p+2 > 1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad p\\in \\left(-\\dfrac13,\\infty\\right)$.\n- For the second series to converge, we require\n$p-1> 1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad p\\in (2, \\infty)$.\n\nFinding the intersection of the two solutions gives\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\dfrac13,\\infty\\right) \\cap (2, \\infty) = (2, \\infty)\n$$\n\nTherefore, both series converge for $p \\in (2, \\infty)$."] [:db/cas 17592186189404 :tutorial/content "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, where the conditional expected value of $X$ given $Y=y$ is given by $\\mu_{X|y}.$ In other words,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] = \\mu_{X|y}.\n$$\n\nThe **conditional variance of $X$ given $Y=y$** is defined as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y] = \\textrm{E}\\big[(X - \\mu_{X|y} \\big)^2 \\,|\\, Y=y].\n$$\n\nAs with the discrete case, this definition can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}\\big[X^2 \\,| \\, Y=y \\big] = \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} x^2 \\, f_{X|Y}(x \\,|\\, y) \\: \\textrm{d}x,\n$$\n\n \n\nand $f_{X|Y}(x \\,|\\, y)$ is the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y.$ Note that these formulas are analogous to those for $\\textrm{Var}[X]$ for continuous $X.$\n\nWhen we select a particular value of $y,$ the conditional variance of $X$ given $Y=y$ returns a real number. However, if $y$ is not specified, then $\\textrm {Var}[X | Y = y]$ gives us a *function* of $y.$\n\nWe have an analogous definition for the **conditional variance of $Y$ given  $X=x{:}$**\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=x] & =\\text{E}[(Y-{\\mu }_{Y|x})^{2}\\,|\\,X=x] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=x]-{\\mu }_{Y|x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\mu_{Y|x} = \\textrm{E}\\big[Y \\,| \\, X=x \\big]$ is the conditional expected value of $Y$ given  $X=x,$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}\\big[Y^2 \\,| \\, X=x \\big] = \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} y^2 \\, f_{Y|X}(y \\,|\\, x) \\: \\textrm{d}y,$ and\n\n- $f_{Y|X}(y \\,|\\, x)$ is the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x.$" "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, where the conditional expected value of $X$ given $Y=y$ is given by $\\mu_{X|y}.$ In other words,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] = \\mu_{X|y}.\n$$\n\nThe **conditional variance of $X$ given $Y=y$** is defined as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y] = \\textrm{E}\\big[(X - \\mu_{X|y} \\big)^2 \\,|\\, Y=y].\n$$\n\nAs with the discrete case, this definition can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}\\big[X^2 \\,| \\, Y=y \\big] = \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} x^2 \\, f_{X|Y}(x \\,|\\, y) \\: \\textrm{d}x,\n$$\n\n \n\nand $f_{X|Y}(x \\,|\\, y)$ is the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y.$ Note that these formulas are analogous to those for $\\textrm{Var}[X]$ for continuous $X.$\n\nWhen we select a particular value of $y,$ the conditional variance of $X$ given $Y=y$ returns a real number. However, if $y$ is not specified, then $\\textrm {Var}[X | Y = y]$ gives us a *function* of $y.$\n\nWe have an analogous definition for the **conditional variance of $Y$ given  $X=x{:}$**\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=x] & =\\text{E}[(Y-{\\mu }_{Y|x})^{2}\\,|\\,X=x] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=x]-{\\mu }_{Y|x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\mu_{Y|x} = \\textrm{E}\\big[Y \\,| \\, X=x \\big]$ is the conditional expected value of $Y$ given  $X=x,$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}\\big[Y^2 \\,| \\, X=x \\big] = \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} y^2 \\, f_{Y|X}(y \\,|\\, x) \\: \\textrm{d}y,\n$$ and\n\n- $f_{Y|X}(y \\,|\\, x)$ is the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x.$"] [:db/cas 17592186189405 :tutorial/content "If $X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_n$ is an I.I.D. random sample of size $n$ from a population with mean $\\mu$ and variance $\\sigma^2$, then by the central limit theorem (CLT), the sample mean is normally distributed with mean $\\mu$ and variance\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\overline{X}\\sim N\\!\\left(\\mu,\\frac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\right)\n$$\n\nThis approximation is valid provided that $n$ is sufficiently large (typically\n\n$$\nn\\geq 30\n$$\n\n) and the underlying population is *infinite.*\n\nPopulations are rarely infinite in real-world situations. In a previous lesson, we saw that if the population is finite and a sample is conducted *without* replacement, the elements of a sample are not independent! So, does this mean we cannot reliably apply the CLT to model real-world situations?\n\nTo answer this question, suppose the sample is drawn *without* replacement from a finite population of size $N$. We have two cases:\n\n- **Case 1**: If the population size is *large compared to the sample size*, the sample elements will be *approximately* independent. This means that the approximation for the sampling distribution of $\\overline{X}$ given by the CLT remains valid.\n- **Case 2**: If the population size is *not* large compared to the sample size, individual sample elements are *not* independent! This means that the approximation for the sampling distribution of $\\overline{X}$ given by the CLT is no longer valid!\n\nSo, it may seem that everything is lost in the second case. However, it turns out that, to account for the dependency of the sample elements, we simply adjust the variance of the sample mean distribution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overline{X}\\sim N\\!\\left(\\mu,\\frac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\cdot \\frac{N-n}{N-1}\\right)\n$$\n\nThis allows us to model the distribution of $\\overline X$ even when $N$ is not large compared to $n$.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The so-called **finite population correction factor** is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{N-n}{N-1}\n$$\n\n- It's appropriate to apply the correction factor whenever $n > 5\\%\\cdot N$ (i.e., the sample includes more than $5\\%$ of the population).\n- If $N$ is much larger than $n$, then\n$\\dfrac{N-n}{N-1} \\approx \\dfrac{N}{N} = 1$\nand we recover the approximation given by the CLT.\n- For $N > n > 1$, we have\n$\\dfrac{N-n}{N-1} < 1$.\nThis means the correction factor *reduces* the variance of $\\overline X$ compared to the CLT approximation. This makes intuitive sense. If we sample from a small population without replacement, the pool of remaining elements becomes smaller with each element sampled, reducing their variability.\n- Finally, if $N=n >1$, then the finite population correction factor equals zero, which means the variance of $\\overline X$ equals zero. Again, this makes intuitive sense. If the sample contains *every* element from the population, then the sample mean equals the population mean, i.e., $\\overline X = \\mu$, and there is no variation.\n\nFinally, suppose that a sample is conducted from a finite population *with replacement* (in other words, we're free to select the same element from the population more than once). In this case, the sample data are independent, and a finite population correction factor is not required. Sampling with replacement effectively makes the population infinite, so a correction to the variance is unnecessary." "If $X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_n$ is an I.I.D. random sample of size $n$ from a population with mean $\\mu$ and variance $\\sigma^2$, then by the central limit theorem (CLT), the sample mean is normally distributed with mean $\\mu$ and variance\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\overline{X}\\sim N\\!\\left(\\mu,\\frac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\right)\n$$\n\nThis approximation is valid provided that $n$ is sufficiently large (typically\n\n$$\nn\\geq 30\n$$\n\n) and the underlying population is *infinite.*\n\nPopulations are rarely infinite in real-world situations. In a previous lesson, we saw that if the population is finite and a sample is conducted *without* replacement, the elements of a sample are not independent! So, does this mean we cannot reliably apply the CLT to model real-world situations?\n\nTo answer this question, suppose the sample is drawn *without* replacement from a finite population of size $N$. We have two cases:\n\n- **Case 1**: If the population size is *large compared to the sample size*, the sample elements will be *approximately* independent. This means that the approximation for the sampling distribution of $\\overline{X}$ given by the CLT remains valid.\n- **Case 2**: If the population size is *not* large compared to the sample size, individual sample elements are *not* independent! This means that the approximation for the sampling distribution of $\\overline{X}$ given by the CLT is no longer valid!\n\nSo, it may seem that everything is lost in the second case. However, it turns out that, to account for the dependency of the sample elements, we simply adjust the variance of the sample mean distribution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overline{X}\\sim N\\!\\left(\\mu,\\frac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\cdot \\frac{N-n}{N-1}\\right)\n$$\n\nThis allows us to model the distribution of $\\overline X$ even when $N$ is not large compared to $n$.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The so-called **finite population correction factor** is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{N-n}{N-1}\n$$\n\n- It's appropriate to apply the correction factor whenever $n > 5\\%\\cdot N$ (i.e., the sample includes more than $5\\%$ of the population).\n- If $N$ is much larger than $n$, then\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{N-n}{N-1} \\approx \\dfrac{N}{N} = 1\n$$\nand we recover the approximation given by the CLT.\n- For $N > n > 1$, we have\n$\\dfrac{N-n}{N-1} < 1$.\nThis means the correction factor *reduces* the variance of $\\overline X$ compared to the CLT approximation. This makes intuitive sense. If we sample from a small population without replacement, the pool of remaining elements becomes smaller with each element sampled, reducing their variability.\n- Finally, if $N=n >1$, then the finite population correction factor equals zero, which means the variance of $\\overline X$ equals zero. Again, this makes intuitive sense. If the sample contains *every* element from the population, then the sample mean equals the population mean, i.e., $\\overline X = \\mu$, and there is no variation.\n\nFinally, suppose that a sample is conducted from a finite population *with replacement* (in other words, we're free to select the same element from the population more than once). In this case, the sample data are independent, and a finite population correction factor is not required. Sampling with replacement effectively makes the population infinite, so a correction to the variance is unnecessary."] [:db/cas 17592186189406 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we'll learn how to construct confidence intervals for population proportions when the population we're sampling is finite. First, let's quickly recap some important results.\n\nSuppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a sufficiently large population where a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Then, we have the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\sim N\\left(p, \\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion. Now, if we $z$-score this result, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p} - p}{\\sqrt{\\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n}}} \\sim N(0,1)\n$$\n\nReplacing our expression for the variance with its sample-based estimate, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p} - p}{\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}}\\phantom{\\mid}} \\sim N(0,1)\n$$\n\nFor this result to be valid, the following conditions must be satisfied:\n\n- More than five members of the sample have the characteristic $(n \\widehat{\\,p} > 5)$, and\n- More than five members of the sample do *not* have the characteristic $(n (1-\\widehat{\\,p}) > 5)$.\n\nThen, for a given value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, a $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $z_{\\alpha/2}$ is the $z$-score satisfying $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) =\\dfrac\\alpha2$, and $Z\\sim N(0,1)$,\n- $E = z_{\\alpha/2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}}$ is the margin of error.\n\nThese results assume that the sample elements are independent (i.e., the population we're drawing from is infinite). However, if the population size $N$ is finite and not large compared to the sample size, the independence assumption is no longer valid.\n\nIn such cases, we can apply the finite population correction factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p} - p}{\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}\\cdot \\dfrac{N-n}{N-1}}\\phantom{\\mid}} \\sim N(0,1)\n$$\n\nThen, the expression for our confidence interval is given by\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n} \\cdot \\dfrac{N-n}{N-1}}\n$$" "In this lesson, we'll learn how to construct confidence intervals for population proportions when the population we're sampling is finite. First, let's quickly recap some important results.\n\nSuppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a sufficiently large population where a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Then, we have the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\sim N\\left(p, \\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion. Now, if we $z$-score this result, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p} - p}{\\sqrt{\\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n}}} \\sim N(0,1)\n$$\n\nReplacing our expression for the variance with its sample-based estimate, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p} - p}{\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}}\\phantom{\\mid}} \\sim N(0,1)\n$$\n\nFor this result to be valid, the following conditions must be satisfied:\n\n- More than five members of the sample have the characteristic $(n \\widehat{\\,p} > 5)$, and\n- More than five members of the sample do *not* have the characteristic $(n (1-\\widehat{\\,p}) > 5)$.\n\nThen, for a given value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, a $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $z_{\\alpha/2}$ is the $z$-score satisfying $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) =\\dfrac\\alpha2$, and $Z\\sim N(0,1)$,\n- $$\n\\displaystyle E = z_{\\alpha/2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}}\n$$ is the margin of error.\n\nThese results assume that the sample elements are independent (i.e., the population we're drawing from is infinite). However, if the population size $N$ is finite and not large compared to the sample size, the independence assumption is no longer valid.\n\nIn such cases, we can apply the finite population correction factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p} - p}{\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}\\cdot \\dfrac{N-n}{N-1}}\\phantom{\\mid}} \\sim N(0,1)\n$$\n\nThen, the expression for our confidence interval is given by\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n} \\cdot \\dfrac{N-n}{N-1}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186192387 :question/problem "Let $\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x=1.6+0.7  x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $(x_1,y_1), \\: \\ldots, \\: (x_{8},y_{8})$ of $n=8$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overline{x}=2.4,$ the mean square error of the model is $1.25,$ and $S_{xx} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2 = 42,$ find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 1.943) = 0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $6$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for $\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] = \\sqrt{ \\textrm{MSE} \\cdot \\bigg( \\dfrac{1}{n} + \\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}} \\bigg) }\n$$\n\n*" "Let $\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x=1.6+0.7  x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $(x_1,y_1), \\: \\ldots, \\: (x_{8},y_{8})$ of $n=8$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overline{x}=2.4,$ the mean square error of the model is $1.25,$ and $$\n\\displaystyle S_{xx} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2 = 42,\n$$ find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 1.943) = 0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $6$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for $\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] = \\sqrt{ \\textrm{MSE} \\cdot \\bigg( \\dfrac{1}{n} + \\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}} \\bigg) }\n$$\n\n*"] [:db/cas 17592186192389 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x; \\theta),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = P(X_1=x_1, X_2=x_2, \\ldots , X_n = x_n) = \\displaystyle\\prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i;\\,\\theta).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta$ and variance $\\sigma^2 = 1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x; \\theta) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sigma \\sqrt{2\\pi}} e^{-\\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\frac{x-\\theta}{\\sigma} \\right)^2} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} e^{-(x-\\theta)^2/2}, \\qquad x \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-\\sum ({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(\\sum {x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(C-2S\\theta +n{\\theta }^{2})/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\displaystyle S = \\sum\\limits_ {i=1}^n x_i$ and $\\displaystyle C = \\sum\\limits_ {i=1}^n x_i^2.$\n\nFor our sample, we have that $n=4,$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =0.4+(-0.3)+(-2.2)+(-0.4) \\\\  & =-2.5 \\\\ C & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(0.4)^{2}+(-0.3)^{2}+(-2.2)^{2}+(-0.4)^{2} \\\\  & =5.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =(2\\pi )^{-4/2}\\,{e}^{-(5.25-2(-2.5)\\theta +4{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4{\\pi }^{2}}\\,{e}^{-2.625\\,-\\,2.5\\theta \\,-\\,2{\\theta }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x; \\theta),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = P(X_1=x_1, X_2=x_2, \\ldots , X_n = x_n) = \\displaystyle\\prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i;\\,\\theta).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta$ and variance $\\sigma^2 = 1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x; \\theta) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sigma \\sqrt{2\\pi}} e^{-\\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\frac{x-\\theta}{\\sigma} \\right)^2} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} e^{-(x-\\theta)^2/2}, \\qquad x \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-\\sum ({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(\\sum {x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(C-2S\\theta +n{\\theta }^{2})/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle S = \\sum\\limits_ {i=1}^n x_i\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle C = \\sum\\limits_ {i=1}^n x_i^2.\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that $n=4,$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =0.4+(-0.3)+(-2.2)+(-0.4) \\\\  & =-2.5 \\\\ C & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(0.4)^{2}+(-0.3)^{2}+(-2.2)^{2}+(-0.4)^{2} \\\\  & =5.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =(2\\pi )^{-4/2}\\,{e}^{-(5.25-2(-2.5)\\theta +4{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4{\\pi }^{2}}\\,{e}^{-2.625\\,-\\,2.5\\theta \\,-\\,2{\\theta }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186192390 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x; \\theta),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given a sample of $n=10$ independent Bernoulli random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta.$ Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta) = \\theta^x(1-\\theta)^{1-x}, \\qquad x=0, 1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(1-{x}_{i})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(1-{x}_{i}) \\\\  & =S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\displaystyle S = \\sum_ {i=1}^n x_i$ is the number of successes in the sample.\n\nFinally, since there are $7$ successes in the sample, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =7\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(10-7)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x; \\theta),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given a sample of $n=10$ independent Bernoulli random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta.$ Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta) = \\theta^x(1-\\theta)^{1-x}, \\qquad x=0, 1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(1-{x}_{i})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(1-{x}_{i}) \\\\  & =S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle S = \\sum_ {i=1}^n x_i\n$$ is the number of successes in the sample.\n\nFinally, since there are $7$ successes in the sample, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =7\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(10-7)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186192391 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x; \\theta),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=8$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta$ and variance $\\sigma^2 = 1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x, \\theta) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} e^{-(x-\\theta)^2/2}, \\qquad x \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x,\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}\\right)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{(x-\\theta )^{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{n}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{n}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that $\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{8} x_i = 11.8,$ $\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{8} x_i^2 = 25.2,$ and $n=8.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =-\\frac{8}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}(25.2-2\\theta \\cdot 11.8+8{\\theta }^{2}) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-12.6+11.8\\theta -4{\\theta }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x; \\theta),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=8$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta$ and variance $\\sigma^2 = 1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x, \\theta) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} e^{-(x-\\theta)^2/2}, \\qquad x \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x,\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}\\right)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{(x-\\theta )^{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{n}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{n}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that $$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{8} x_i = 11.8,\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{8} x_i^2 = 25.2,\n$$ and $n=8.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =-\\frac{8}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}(25.2-2\\theta \\cdot 11.8+8{\\theta }^{2}) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-12.6+11.8\\theta -4{\\theta }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186192393 :question/problem "Use a calculator to compute $\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{1} e^{-x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x$. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." "Use a calculator to compute $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{1} e^{-x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186192393 :question/worked-solution "We bring up the definite integral option on most calculators in one of the following ways:\n\n- Press the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,2\\text{nd}\\,}$ (or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{shift}\\,}$) button, then the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\displaystyle\\color{gray}\\,\\displaystyle \\int_{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}}^{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}} \\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{\\big \\mid 0\\big \\mid}}\\,}$ button.\n- Press the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{math}\\,}$ (or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{calc}\\,}$) button and choose the integral option. Note that this might be called $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{fnInt}\\,}$ or similar.\n\nEvaluating our integral using a calculator, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{1} e^{-x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x \\approx 0.75\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." "We bring up the definite integral option on most calculators in one of the following ways:\n\n- Press the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,2\\text{nd}\\,}$ (or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{shift}\\,}$) button, then the $$\n\\displaystyle \\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\displaystyle\\color{gray}\\,\\displaystyle \\int_{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}}^{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}} \\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{\\big \\mid 0\\big \\mid}}\\,}\n$$ button.\n- Press the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{math}\\,}$ (or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{calc}\\,}$) button and choose the integral option. Note that this might be called $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{fnInt}\\,}$ or similar.\n\nEvaluating our integral using a calculator, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{1} e^{-x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x \\approx 0.75\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186192394 :question/problem "The integral\n$\\mathcal{A} = \\displaystyle \\int_{a}^{1.312} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x$\nrepresents the (signed) area of the finite region bounded by the curve $f(x) = x^4 - 2 -\\sin{x}$ and the $x$-axis. Find the lower limit $a$ to $3$ decimal places, and use your answer to estimate the area $\\mathcal{A}$." "The integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\mathcal{A} = \\displaystyle \\int_{a}^{1.312} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\nrepresents the (signed) area of the finite region bounded by the curve $f(x) = x^4 - 2 -\\sin{x}$ and the $x$-axis. Find the lower limit $a$ to $3$ decimal places, and use your answer to estimate the area $\\mathcal{A}$."] [:db/cas 17592186192394 :question/worked-solution "First, let's plot our region using a graphing calculator.\n\nTo get a better view, we might need to either\n\n- zoom in or out, or\n- adjust the window ranges.\n\nWe can adjust the window settings using the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{window}\\,}$ button (or equivalent).\n\nIn our case, the appropriate window ranges could be the following:\n\n- for the horizontal axis, $x \\in [-2.5,2.5]$\n- for the vertical axis, $y \\in [-3,1]$\n\nThis gives us the following graph:\n\n![](images/b2/b2675f8f7be370b26a070c686a72b17861e598ff33cfb1d7d0ae6f13eaab9d72.png)\n\nAccording to the graph, the lower limit corresponds to the root of the function $f(x)$ that lies in the interval $x \\in [-2,0]$.\n\nUsing a graphing calculator, we find that this root, rounded to $3$ decimal places, is\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\na \\approx -1.034\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral with the lower limit $a=-1.034{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\int_{-1.034}^{1.312} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nWe bring up the integral option on most calculators in one of the following ways:\n\n- Press the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,2\\text{nd}\\,}$ (or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{shift}\\,}$) button, then the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\displaystyle\\color{gray}\\,\\displaystyle \\int_{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}}^{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}} \\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{\\big \\mid 0\\big \\mid}}\\,}$ button.\n- Press the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{math}\\,}$ (or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{calc}\\,}$) button and choose the integral option. Note that this might be called $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{fnInt}\\,}$ or similar.\n\nEvaluating our integral using a calculator, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\int_{-1.034}^{1.312} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x \\approx -3.934\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "First, let's plot our region using a graphing calculator.\n\nTo get a better view, we might need to either\n\n- zoom in or out, or\n- adjust the window ranges.\n\nWe can adjust the window settings using the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{window}\\,}$ button (or equivalent).\n\nIn our case, the appropriate window ranges could be the following:\n\n- for the horizontal axis, $x \\in [-2.5,2.5]$\n- for the vertical axis, $y \\in [-3,1]$\n\nThis gives us the following graph:\n\n![](images/b2/b2675f8f7be370b26a070c686a72b17861e598ff33cfb1d7d0ae6f13eaab9d72.png)\n\nAccording to the graph, the lower limit corresponds to the root of the function $f(x)$ that lies in the interval $x \\in [-2,0]$.\n\nUsing a graphing calculator, we find that this root, rounded to $3$ decimal places, is\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\na \\approx -1.034\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral with the lower limit $a=-1.034{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\int_{-1.034}^{1.312} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nWe bring up the integral option on most calculators in one of the following ways:\n\n- Press the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,2\\text{nd}\\,}$ (or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{shift}\\,}$) button, then the $$\n\\displaystyle \\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\displaystyle\\color{gray}\\,\\displaystyle \\int_{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}}^{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}} \\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{\\big \\mid 0\\big \\mid}}\\,}\n$$ button.\n- Press the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{math}\\,}$ (or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{calc}\\,}$) button and choose the integral option. Note that this might be called $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{fnInt}\\,}$ or similar.\n\nEvaluating our integral using a calculator, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\int_{-1.034}^{1.312} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x \\approx -3.934\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186192409 :question/problem "The table below gives the values of a positive, continuous function $f(x)$ and its derivative $f'(x)$ over the closed interval $[1,4]$.\nUsing a right Riemann sum with $3$ equal subintervals, estimate the arc length of the curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=1$ and $x=4$.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $2$ | $1$ | $4$ | $2$ |\n| $f'(x)$ | $-1$ | $3\\sqrt{11}$ | $0$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ |\n\n*Hint: The arc length of a smooth curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ is given by*\n\n$L = \\displaystyle \\int_a^b \\sqrt{1+[f'(x)]^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x$." "The table below gives the values of a positive, continuous function $f(x)$ and its derivative $f'(x)$ over the closed interval $[1,4]$.\nUsing a right Riemann sum with $3$ equal subintervals, estimate the arc length of the curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=1$ and $x=4$.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $2$ | $1$ | $4$ | $2$ |\n| $f'(x)$ | $-1$ | $3\\sqrt{11}$ | $0$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ |\n\n*Hint: The arc length of a smooth curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ is given by*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle L = \\displaystyle \\int_a^b \\sqrt{1+[f'(x)]^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186192417 :question/problem "Approximate $\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{\\infty} \\sqrt{2x}e^{-x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x$ to three decimal places." "Approximate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{\\infty} \\sqrt{2x}e^{-x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$ to three decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186192417 :question/worked-solution "We bring up the integral option on most calculators in one of the following ways:\n\n- Press the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,2\\text{nd}\\,}$ (or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{shift}\\,}$) button, then the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\displaystyle\\color{gray}\\,\\displaystyle \\int_{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}}^{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}} \\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{\\big \\mid 0\\big \\mid}}\\,}$ button.\n- Press the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{math}\\,}$ (or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{calc}\\,}$) button and choose the integral option. Note that this might be called $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{fnInt}\\,}$ or similar.\n\nTo calculate our improper integral, we insert *increasingly large* numbers in place of $\\infty$ until successive approximations are the same, as follows:\n\n- Evaluating the integral with upper limit $9$ using a calculator, we get\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{9} \\sqrt{2x}e^{-x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x \\approx \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{0.225}$,\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n- Evaluating the integral with upper limit $99$ using a calculator, we obtain\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{99} \\sqrt{2x}e^{-x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x \\approx \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{0.225}$,\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nWe can stop now since the last two approximations were $0.225$ to $3$ decimal places.\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_{1}^{\\infty} \\sqrt{2x}e^{-x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x \\approx 0.225\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "We bring up the integral option on most calculators in one of the following ways:\n\n- Press the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,2\\text{nd}\\,}$ (or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{shift}\\,}$) button, then the $$\n\\displaystyle \\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\displaystyle\\color{gray}\\,\\displaystyle \\int_{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}}^{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}} \\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{\\big \\mid 0\\big \\mid}}\\,}\n$$ button.\n- Press the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{math}\\,}$ (or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{calc}\\,}$) button and choose the integral option. Note that this might be called $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{fnInt}\\,}$ or similar.\n\nTo calculate our improper integral, we insert *increasingly large* numbers in place of $\\infty$ until successive approximations are the same, as follows:\n\n- Evaluating the integral with upper limit $9$ using a calculator, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{9} \\sqrt{2x}e^{-x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x \\approx \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{0.225},\n$$\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n- Evaluating the integral with upper limit $99$ using a calculator, we obtain\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{99} \\sqrt{2x}e^{-x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x \\approx \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{0.225},\n$$\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nWe can stop now since the last two approximations were $0.225$ to $3$ decimal places.\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_{1}^{\\infty} \\sqrt{2x}e^{-x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x \\approx 0.225\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186192418 :question/problem "Use a calculator to approximate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{\\ln(x^2+1)}{x^4 + 2} \\, \\textrm{d}x$ rounded to $2$ decimal places." "Use a calculator to approximate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{\\ln(x^2+1)}{x^4 + 2} \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$ rounded to $2$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186192418 :question/worked-solution "We bring up the integral option on most calculators in one of the following ways:\n\n- Press the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,2\\text{nd}\\,}$ (or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{shift}\\,}$) button, then the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\displaystyle\\color{gray}\\,\\displaystyle \\int_{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}}^{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}} \\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{\\big \\mid 0\\big \\mid}}\\,}$ button.\n- Press the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{math}\\,}$ (or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{calc}\\,}$) button and choose the integral option. Note that this might be called $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{fnInt}\\,}$ or similar.\n\nTo calculate our improper integral, we insert decreasingly small numbers in place of $-\\infty$ and increasingly large numbers in place of $\\infty$ until successive approximations are the same, as follows:\n\n- Evaluating the integral with lower limit $-9$ and upper limit $9$ using a calculator, we get\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{-9}^{9} \\dfrac{\\ln(x^2+1)}{x^4 + 2} \\, \\textrm{d}x \\approx \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{0.73}$,\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n- Evaluating the integral with lower limit $-99$ and upper limit $99$ using a calculator, we obtain\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{-99}^{99} \\dfrac{\\ln(x^2+1)}{x^4 + 2} \\, \\textrm{d}x \\approx \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{0.73}$,\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nWe can stop now since the last two approximations were $0.73$, to $2$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{\\ln(x^2+1)}{x^4 + 2} \\, \\textrm{d}x \\approx 0.73\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." "We bring up the integral option on most calculators in one of the following ways:\n\n- Press the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,2\\text{nd}\\,}$ (or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{shift}\\,}$) button, then the $$\n\\displaystyle \\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\displaystyle\\color{gray}\\,\\displaystyle \\int_{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}}^{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}} \\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{\\big \\mid 0\\big \\mid}}\\,}\n$$ button.\n- Press the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{math}\\,}$ (or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{calc}\\,}$) button and choose the integral option. Note that this might be called $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{fnInt}\\,}$ or similar.\n\nTo calculate our improper integral, we insert decreasingly small numbers in place of $-\\infty$ and increasingly large numbers in place of $\\infty$ until successive approximations are the same, as follows:\n\n- Evaluating the integral with lower limit $-9$ and upper limit $9$ using a calculator, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-9}^{9} \\dfrac{\\ln(x^2+1)}{x^4 + 2} \\, \\textrm{d}x \\approx \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{0.73},\n$$\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n- Evaluating the integral with lower limit $-99$ and upper limit $99$ using a calculator, we obtain\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-99}^{99} \\dfrac{\\ln(x^2+1)}{x^4 + 2} \\, \\textrm{d}x \\approx \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{0.73},\n$$\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nWe can stop now since the last two approximations were $0.73$, to $2$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{\\ln(x^2+1)}{x^4 + 2} \\, \\textrm{d}x \\approx 0.73\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186192420 :question/problem "The table below gives the values of a positive, continuous function $f(x)$ over the closed interval $[1,9]$.\nUsing a left Riemann sum with $4$ equal subintervals, estimate the volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by the curve $y=f(x)$, the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x=1$ and $x=9$ is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ | $9$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $3$ | $1$ | $0$ | $5$ |\n\n*Hint: The volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by a curve $y=f(x)$, the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x=a$ and $x=b$ is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis is given by*\n\n$V = \\pi \\displaystyle \\int_a^b [f(x)]^2 \\,\\textrm{d}x$." "The table below gives the values of a positive, continuous function $f(x)$ over the closed interval $[1,9]$.\nUsing a left Riemann sum with $4$ equal subintervals, estimate the volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by the curve $y=f(x)$, the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x=1$ and $x=9$ is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ | $9$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $3$ | $1$ | $0$ | $5$ |\n\n*Hint: The volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by a curve $y=f(x)$, the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x=a$ and $x=b$ is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis is given by*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle V = \\pi \\displaystyle \\int_a^b [f(x)]^2 \\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186192421 :question/problem "Evaluate\n$\\displaystyle{\\sum_{i=1}^{100} \\dfrac14}$." "Evaluate\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\sum_{i=1}^{100} \\dfrac14}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186192422 :question/problem "Given that\n$\\displaystyle{\\sum_{i=1}^{10} a_i} = 5$,\nfind the value of\n$\\displaystyle{\\sum_{i=1}^{10} (-2a_i)}$." "Given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\sum_{i=1}^{10} a_i} = 5,\n$$\nfind the value of\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\sum_{i=1}^{10} (-2a_i)}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186192423 :question/problem "Given that\n$\\displaystyle{\\sum_{i=1}^{20} s_i} = -7$\nand\n$\\displaystyle{\\sum_{i=1}^{20} t_i} = 9$,\nfind the value of\n$\\displaystyle{\\sum_{i=1}^{20} (10s_i+12t_i+5)}$." "Given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\sum_{i=1}^{20} s_i} = -7\n$$\nand\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\sum_{i=1}^{20} t_i} = 9,\n$$\nfind the value of\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\sum_{i=1}^{20} (10s_i+12t_i+5)}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186192423 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following properties of sums:\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{n} (s_i+t_i) = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} s_i + \\sum_{i=1}^{n} t_i$\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{n} c s_i = c \\cdot \\sum_{i=1}^{n} s_i$\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{n} 1 = n$\n\nBy applying each property in turn, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(i = 1)^(20)(10s_{i} + 12t_{i} + 5) &= ∑_(i = 1)^(20)10s_{i} + ∑_(i = 1)^(20)12t_{i} + ∑_(i = 1)^(20)5 \\\\\n&= 10 \\cdot ∑_(i = 1)^(20)s_{i} + 12 \\cdot ∑_(i = 1)^(20)t_{i} + 5 \\cdot ∑_(i = 1)^(20)1 \\\\\n&= 10 \\cdot (-7) + 12 \\cdot 9 + 5 \\cdot 20 \\\\\n&= 138\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall the following properties of sums:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{n} (s_i+t_i) = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} s_i + \\sum_{i=1}^{n} t_i\n$$\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{n} c s_i = c \\cdot \\sum_{i=1}^{n} s_i\n$$\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{n} 1 = n\n$$\n\nBy applying each property in turn, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(i = 1)^(20)(10s_{i} + 12t_{i} + 5) &= ∑_(i = 1)^(20)10s_{i} + ∑_(i = 1)^(20)12t_{i} + ∑_(i = 1)^(20)5 \\\\\n&= 10 \\cdot ∑_(i = 1)^(20)s_{i} + 12 \\cdot ∑_(i = 1)^(20)t_{i} + 5 \\cdot ∑_(i = 1)^(20)1 \\\\\n&= 10 \\cdot (-7) + 12 \\cdot 9 + 5 \\cdot 20 \\\\\n&= 138\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186192427 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{2\\sqrt{3}}^\\infty \\dfrac{6}{x^2+4} \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{2\\sqrt{3}}^\\infty \\dfrac{6}{x^2+4} \\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186192427 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{k^2 + x^2} \\, \\textrm dx = \\dfrac{1}{k} \\arctan \\left( \\dfrac{x}{k} \\right) + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)]}_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{a} \\\\  & =3{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})) \\\\  & =3{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{2}\\right)-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =3{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{2}\\right)]-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\left[\\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)\\right] = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\left[\\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)\\right] = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n![](images/04/042ad2450306552d9d5a7e4219d15547ad7104601b7ce6350a64193981de0de7.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{2}\\right)]-\\pi  \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{k^2 + x^2} \\, \\textrm dx = \\dfrac{1}{k} \\arctan \\left( \\dfrac{x}{k} \\right) + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)]}_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{a} \\\\  & =3{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})) \\\\  & =3{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{2}\\right)-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =3{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{2}\\right)]-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\left[\\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)\\right] = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\left[\\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)\\right] = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/04/042ad2450306552d9d5a7e4219d15547ad7104601b7ce6350a64193981de0de7.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{2}\\right)]-\\pi  \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186192428 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1/4}^\\infty \\dfrac {2}{1+16x^2}  \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-1/4}^\\infty \\dfrac {2}{1+16x^2}  \\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186192428 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{1+(kx)^2} \\, \\textrm dx = \\dfrac{1}{k} \\arctan \\left(kx \\right) + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1/4}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{1+16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-1/4}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-1/4}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+(4x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4x)]}_{-1/4}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4a)]-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4a)]+\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\arctan{(4x)},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\left[\\arctan{(4x)}\\right] = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\left[\\arctan{(4x)}\\right] = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n![](images/9a/9a7272f8bc8d0eba3e5bfc38e843cd02db3f3eb7b51e77b95b2efd61034890b6.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1/4}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{1+16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4a)]+\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{1+(kx)^2} \\, \\textrm dx = \\dfrac{1}{k} \\arctan \\left(kx \\right) + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1/4}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{1+16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-1/4}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-1/4}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+(4x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4x)]}_{-1/4}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4a)]-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4a)]+\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\arctan{(4x)},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\left[\\arctan{(4x)}\\right] = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\left[\\arctan{(4x)}\\right] = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/9a/9a7272f8bc8d0eba3e5bfc38e843cd02db3f3eb7b51e77b95b2efd61034890b6.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1/4}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{1+16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4a)]+\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186192429 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_1^\\infty  x^3\\cdot 2^{-x^4}   \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_1^\\infty  x^3\\cdot 2^{-x^4}   \\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186192429 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=x^4.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= 4x^3\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac 14 \\textrm{d}u =x^3\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty$ when $x\\to \\infty,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x^4$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot {2}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot {2}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{2}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{2}^{-u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{2}^{-u}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{a}}-\\frac{1}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\dfrac{1}{2^x},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{2^{x}} = \\infty$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{1}{2^{x}} = 0$\n\n![](images/19/1969145d74662468ec23d458ac1ad0194d51cbf6b026718b7232466e9357efdf.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot {2}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{a}}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we substitute $u=x^4.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= 4x^3\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac 14 \\textrm{d}u =x^3\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty$ when $x\\to \\infty,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x^4$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot {2}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot {2}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{2}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{2}^{-u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{2}^{-u}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{a}}-\\frac{1}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\dfrac{1}{2^x},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{2^{x}} = \\infty\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{1}{2^{x}} = 0\n$$\n\n![](images/19/1969145d74662468ec23d458ac1ad0194d51cbf6b026718b7232466e9357efdf.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot {2}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{a}}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186192430 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^\\infty   e^{-3x}  \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^\\infty   e^{-3x}  \\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186192430 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int e^{px + q}\\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac1p e^{px+q} + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-1}^{a}{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}\\,]}_{-1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{e}^{-3x}]}_{-1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-3a}-{e}^{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=e^{-3x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} e^{-3x} = \\infty$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} e^{-3x} =0$\n\n![](images/bc/bc4ffc41105491f2bd6b37b2482afc64946d8f67f6db83dfa322c400ae4bc2b6.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-3x} & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-3a}-{e}^{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (0-{e}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int e^{px + q}\\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac1p e^{px+q} + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-1}^{a}{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}\\,]}_{-1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{e}^{-3x}]}_{-1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-3a}-{e}^{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=e^{-3x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} e^{-3x} = \\infty\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} e^{-3x} =0\n$$\n\n![](images/bc/bc4ffc41105491f2bd6b37b2482afc64946d8f67f6db83dfa322c400ae4bc2b6.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-3x} & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-3a}-{e}^{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (0-{e}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186192452 :question/worked-solution "We have the following two cases:\n\n- If $\\mid 2x-3|=2x-3$, we obtain\n$\\mid 2x - 3 \\mid |=-x + 1; 2x - 3|=-x + 1; 2x|=-x + 4; 3x|= 4; x|= \\frac{4}{3}$.\nThis *potential* solution is valid only if it solves the original equation. So, let's plug it back in:\n$\\mid 2x - 3 \\mid |=-x + 1; \\mid 2 \\cdot \\frac{4}{3} - 3 \\mid |= - \\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right) + 1; \\mid \\frac{8}{3} - 3 \\mid |=-\\frac{4}{3} + 1; \\mid \\frac{8}{3} - \\frac{9}{3} \\mid |=-\\frac{4}{3} + \\frac{3}{3}; \\mid - \\frac{1}{3} \\mid |=-\\frac{1}{3}; \\frac{1}{3}|=-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot$\nSince $x=-\\dfrac 43$ does *not* satisfy the original equation, it's *not* a valid solution.\n- If $\\mid 2x-3|=-(2x-3)$, we obtain\n$\\mid 2x - 3 \\mid |=-x + 1; - (2x - 3)|=-x + 1; - 2x + 3|=-x + 1; 3|= x + 1; 2|= x; x|= 2$.\nThis *potential* solution is valid only if it solves the original equation. So, let's plug it back in:\n$\\mid 2x - 3 \\mid |=-x + 1; \\mid 2(2) - 3 \\mid |= - (2) + 1; \\mid 4 - 3 \\mid |=-2 + 1; \\mid 1 \\mid |=-1; 1|=-1\\cdot$\nSince $x=2$ does *not* satisfy the original equation, it's *not* a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, our equation has no solutions." "We have the following two cases:\n\n- If $\\mid 2x-3|=2x-3$, we obtain\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\mid 2x - 3 \\mid |=-x + 1; 2x - 3|=-x + 1; 2x|=-x + 4; 3x|= 4; x|= \\frac{4}{3}.\n$$\nThis *potential* solution is valid only if it solves the original equation. So, let's plug it back in:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\mid 2x - 3 \\mid |=-x + 1; \\mid 2 \\cdot \\frac{4}{3} - 3 \\mid |= - \\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right) + 1; \\mid \\frac{8}{3} - 3 \\mid |=-\\frac{4}{3} + 1; \\mid \\frac{8}{3} - \\frac{9}{3} \\mid |=-\\frac{4}{3} + \\frac{3}{3}; \\mid - \\frac{1}{3} \\mid |=-\\frac{1}{3}; \\frac{1}{3}|=-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot\n$$\nSince $x=-\\dfrac 43$ does *not* satisfy the original equation, it's *not* a valid solution.\n- If $\\mid 2x-3|=-(2x-3)$, we obtain\n$\\mid 2x - 3 \\mid |=-x + 1; - (2x - 3)|=-x + 1; - 2x + 3|=-x + 1; 3|= x + 1; 2|= x; x|= 2$.\nThis *potential* solution is valid only if it solves the original equation. So, let's plug it back in:\n$\\mid 2x - 3 \\mid |=-x + 1; \\mid 2(2) - 3 \\mid |= - (2) + 1; \\mid 4 - 3 \\mid |=-2 + 1; \\mid 1 \\mid |=-1; 1|=-1\\cdot$\nSince $x=2$ does *not* satisfy the original equation, it's *not* a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, our equation has no solutions."] [:db/cas 17592186192455 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^0 \\dfrac{4}{1+x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^0 \\dfrac{4}{1+x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186192455 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{1+x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\arctan(x) + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a \\to -\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)]_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)] \\\\  & =4\\cdot (0-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]) \\\\  & =-4{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y = \\arctan{x},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\left[\\arctan{x}\\right] = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\left[\\arctan{x}\\right] = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n![](images/27/27ec65761e5119be666e69b67b2fa2fcb7b4f3665ddc41faddd6c136d5c955b1.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-4{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)] \\\\  & =-4\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{1+x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\arctan(x) + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a \\to -\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)]_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)] \\\\  & =4\\cdot (0-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]) \\\\  & =-4{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y = \\arctan{x},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\left[\\arctan{x}\\right] = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\left[\\arctan{x}\\right] = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/27/27ec65761e5119be666e69b67b2fa2fcb7b4f3665ddc41faddd6c136d5c955b1.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-4{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)] \\\\  & =-4\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186192456 :question/problem "Use the substitution $u = x^2$ to evaluate $\\displaystyle{\\int}_{1}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{2x}{1 + x^4}   \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" "Use the substitution $u = x^2$ to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle{\\int}_{1}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{2x}{1 + x^4}   \\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186192456 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u = x^2.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2x\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}u =2x \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u \\to \\infty$ when $x \\to \\infty,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u = x^2$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(u)]_{1}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)] \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\arctan(x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\left[ \\arctan(x) \\right] = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\left[ \\arctan(x) \\right] = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n![](images/46/46e6cf2950c99831a7445a862fb36a6a7b8f9233a82e49c7cf5da4a383ec4756.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we substitute $u = x^2.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2x\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}u =2x \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u \\to \\infty$ when $x \\to \\infty,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u = x^2$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(u)]_{1}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)] \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\arctan(x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\left[ \\arctan(x) \\right] = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\left[ \\arctan(x) \\right] = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/46/46e6cf2950c99831a7445a862fb36a6a7b8f9233a82e49c7cf5da4a383ec4756.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186192465 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{0}  3\\cdot 2^{2x+1}   \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{0}  3\\cdot 2^{2x+1}   \\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186192465 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int b^{\\,px + q}\\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac1{p\\ln(b)} b^{\\,px+q} + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to-\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {2}^{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{2}^{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{2}^{2x+1}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot {2}^{1}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{2}^{2x}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({2}^{0}-{2}^{2a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{2a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=2^{2x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} 2^{2x} = 0$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} 2^{2x} = \\infty$\n\n![](images/00/009f467677932d0ac17e1632c31f4d3aa5271ffe332730d9670e69862b243bef.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {2}^{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{2a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int b^{\\,px + q}\\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac1{p\\ln(b)} b^{\\,px+q} + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to-\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {2}^{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{2}^{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{2}^{2x+1}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot {2}^{1}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{2}^{2x}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({2}^{0}-{2}^{2a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{2a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=2^{2x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} 2^{2x} = 0\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} 2^{2x} = \\infty\n$$\n\n![](images/00/009f467677932d0ac17e1632c31f4d3aa5271ffe332730d9670e69862b243bef.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {2}^{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{2a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186192466 :question/worked-solution "The $x$-coordinate of $A$ corresponds to a solution of the equation\n\n$$\n-\\mid x-2 \\mid = -2x+6\n$$\n\nThe absolute value graph\n\n$$\ny=-\\mid x-2 \\mid\n$$\n\nhas two branches:\n\n- where $-\\mid x-2 \\mid = -(x-2)$, which has a negative slope, and\n- where $-\\mid x-2|=x-2$, which has a positive slope.\n\nNow, let's consider each branch in turn:\n\n- No points of intersection lie on the branch with a positive slope, so we can ignore this branch.\n- The point $A$ lies on the branch with a negative slope. So, if $-\\mid x-2 \\mid = -(x-2)$, we obtain\n$- (x - 2)|=-2x + 6; - x + 2|=-2x + 6; x + 2|= 6; x|= 4$.\nThis *potential* solution is valid only if it solves the original equation. So, let's plug it back in:\n$-\\begin{vmatrix}x - 2 & |=-2x + 6 \\\\ - & 4 - 2\\end{vmatrix}|=-2(4) + 6; -\\begin{vmatrix}2 & |=-8 + 6 \\\\ - & 2\\end{vmatrix}|=-2; - 2|=-2✓$\nSince $x=4$ satisfies the original equation, the $x$-coordinate of $A$ is $x=4$.\n\nTherefore, the $x$-coordinate of $A$ is $4$." "The $x$-coordinate of $A$ corresponds to a solution of the equation\n\n$$\n-\\mid x-2 \\mid = -2x+6\n$$\n\nThe absolute value graph\n\n$$\ny=-\\mid x-2 \\mid\n$$\n\nhas two branches:\n\n- where $-\\mid x-2 \\mid = -(x-2)$, which has a negative slope, and\n- where $-\\mid x-2|=x-2$, which has a positive slope.\n\nNow, let's consider each branch in turn:\n\n- No points of intersection lie on the branch with a positive slope, so we can ignore this branch.\n- The point $A$ lies on the branch with a negative slope. So, if $-\\mid x-2 \\mid = -(x-2)$, we obtain\n$- (x - 2)|=-2x + 6; - x + 2|=-2x + 6; x + 2|= 6; x|= 4$.\nThis *potential* solution is valid only if it solves the original equation. So, let's plug it back in:\n$$\n\\displaystyle -\\begin{vmatrix}x - 2 & |=-2x + 6 \\\\ - & 4 - 2\\end{vmatrix}|=-2(4) + 6; -\\begin{vmatrix}2 & |=-8 + 6 \\\\ - & 2\\end{vmatrix}|=-2; - 2|=-2✓\n$$\nSince $x=4$ satisfies the original equation, the $x$-coordinate of $A$ is $x=4$.\n\nTherefore, the $x$-coordinate of $A$ is $4$."] [:db/cas 17592186192474 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ defined on a set $S$ to be a valid probability density function, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x \\in S$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{S} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 1$\n\nIn our case, for the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{k}{(4x+1)^3}$ on the interval $x \\geq 0,$ the second condition states that\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{k}{(4x+1)^3} \\, \\textrm dx = 1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{k}{(4x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-{[\\frac{k}{8(4x+1)^{2}}]}_{0}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result back into the first equation and solving for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{k}{8} = 1 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad k = 8.\n$$" "For a function $f(x)$ defined on a set $S$ to be a valid probability density function, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x \\in S$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{S} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 1\n$$\n\nIn our case, for the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{k}{(4x+1)^3}$ on the interval $x \\geq 0,$ the second condition states that\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{k}{(4x+1)^3} \\, \\textrm dx = 1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{k}{(4x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-{[\\frac{k}{8(4x+1)^{2}}]}_{0}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result back into the first equation and solving for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{k}{8} = 1 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad k = 8.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186192478 :question/problem "Given that $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n$ is convergent, what can be said about the convergence behavior of the series $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=150}^\\infty a_n$?" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$ is convergent, what can be said about the convergence behavior of the series $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=150}^\\infty a_n\n$$?"] [:db/cas 17592186192478 :question/worked-solution "Removing the first $k-1$ terms from a convergent series results in a convergent series.\n\nMore precisely, assume that\n\n$$\nk\\geq 1\n$$\n\nis an integer. Then, we have the following:\n\n> *If $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n$ is convergent, then $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=k}^\\infty a_n$ is also convergent.*\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=150}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\nis convergent." "Removing the first $k-1$ terms from a convergent series results in a convergent series.\n\nMore precisely, assume that\n\n$$\nk\\geq 1\n$$\n\nis an integer. Then, we have the following:\n\n> *If $$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$ is convergent, then $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=k}^\\infty a_n\n$$ is also convergent.*\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=150}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\nis convergent."] [:db/cas 17592186192479 :question/problem "Given that $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n$ is convergent and $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n$ is convergent, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} b_n$ is convergent\n2. $\\displaystyle 4 \\cdot \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n$ is convergent\n3. $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=10}^\\infty b_n$ is convergent" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$ is convergent and $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$ is convergent, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} b_n\n$$ is convergent\n2. $$\n\\displaystyle 4 \\cdot \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$ is convergent\n3. $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=10}^\\infty b_n\n$$ is convergent"] [:db/cas 17592186192479 :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the properties of convergent series:\n\n- If $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n$ is convergent, then $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty K\\cdot a_n = K\\cdot \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n$ is also convergent.\n- If $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n$ is convergent and $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n$ is convergent, then $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n$ is convergent.\n- If $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n$ is convergent, then $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=k}^\\infty a_n$ is also convergent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. The sum of two convergent series is convergent.\n- Statement II is true. Any constant multiple of a convergent series gives a convergent series.\n- Statement III is true. Removing a finite number of terms from a convergent series gives a convergent series.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, III.\"" "Let's recall the properties of convergent series:\n\n- If $$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$ is convergent, then $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty K\\cdot a_n = K\\cdot \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$ is also convergent.\n- If $$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$ is convergent and $$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$ is convergent, then $$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$ is convergent.\n- If $$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$ is convergent, then $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=k}^\\infty a_n\n$$ is also convergent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. The sum of two convergent series is convergent.\n- Statement II is true. Any constant multiple of a convergent series gives a convergent series.\n- Statement III is true. Removing a finite number of terms from a convergent series gives a convergent series.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, III.\""] [:db/cas 17592186192491 :question/worked-solution "Let's compare each of the given triangles to $\\triangle{ABC}{:}$\n\n- First, let's analyze whether $\\triangle{ABC} \\sim \\triangle{AKL}$ is true:![](images/16/168175df0fcfb7baf7a1eb838e6d3cd4086916ea7f6ecdc1b6a8d52a96bebce8.png)\nThere is no similarity criterion we can apply to these triangles. Therefore, we **cannot** conclude that $\\triangle ABC \\sim \\triangle AKL$.\n\n- Next, let's analyze whether $\\triangle{ABC} \\sim \\triangle{BLC}$ is true:![](images/40/40dc5d0817a4a7a0848da00d4b54b171fafc736f5f39fa95c2e778dd6ee4148b.png)\nNote that\n$\\angle{C} \\cong \\angle{C}, \\qquad \\dfrac{6}{4} = \\dfrac{9}{6} = \\dfrac{3}{2}$.\nTherefore, $\\triangle{ABC} \\sim \\triangle{BLC}$ must be true by the SAS criterion.\n\n- Finally, let's analyze whether $\\triangle{ABC} \\sim \\triangle{BKL}{:}$![](images/ce/ce1739f0cdc28b5aead24f901282cb0e8e465176b9816a8241231ac10f0289f2.png)\nNote that\n$\\angle{A} \\cong \\angle{LBK}, \\qquad \\angle{C} \\cong \\angle{KLB}$.\nTherefore, $\\triangle{ABC} \\sim \\triangle{BKL}$ must be true by the AA criterion.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "Let's compare each of the given triangles to $\\triangle{ABC}{:}$\n\n- First, let's analyze whether $\\triangle{ABC} \\sim \\triangle{AKL}$ is true:![](images/16/168175df0fcfb7baf7a1eb838e6d3cd4086916ea7f6ecdc1b6a8d52a96bebce8.png)\nThere is no similarity criterion we can apply to these triangles. Therefore, we **cannot** conclude that $\\triangle ABC \\sim \\triangle AKL$.\n\n- Next, let's analyze whether $\\triangle{ABC} \\sim \\triangle{BLC}$ is true:![](images/40/40dc5d0817a4a7a0848da00d4b54b171fafc736f5f39fa95c2e778dd6ee4148b.png)\nNote that\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\angle{C} \\cong \\angle{C}, \\qquad \\dfrac{6}{4} = \\dfrac{9}{6} = \\dfrac{3}{2}.\n$$\nTherefore, $\\triangle{ABC} \\sim \\triangle{BLC}$ must be true by the SAS criterion.\n\n- Finally, let's analyze whether $\\triangle{ABC} \\sim \\triangle{BKL}{:}$![](images/ce/ce1739f0cdc28b5aead24f901282cb0e8e465176b9816a8241231ac10f0289f2.png)\nNote that\n$\\angle{A} \\cong \\angle{LBK}, \\qquad \\angle{C} \\cong \\angle{KLB}$.\nTherefore, $\\triangle{ABC} \\sim \\triangle{BKL}$ must be true by the AA criterion.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186192497 :question/worked-solution "To convert $168$ inches into yards, we convert $168$ inches into feet, and then convert the result from feet to yards.\n\n- To convert from $168$ inches into feet, we start with the unit conversion\n$1\\, \\textrm{ft} =12 \\, \\textrm{in}$.\nNow, we divide both sides of the above equation by $12$ and obtain the following:\n$\\dfrac{1}{12}\\, \\textrm{ft} =1 \\, \\textrm{in}$\nWe want to determine how many feet are in $168$ inches. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation $168{:}$\n$168 \\cdot \\frac{1}{12}ft|= 168 \\cdot 1in; \\frac{168}{12}ft|= 168in$\nNow, we simplify this fraction by interpreting $\\dfrac{168}{12}$ as the division $168 \\div 12$. We can calculate this using long division:\n$1$$4$$12$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$4$$8$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$8$$\\phantom{0}$$0$\nTherefore, $168\\,\\textrm{in}$ equals $14\\,\\textrm{ft}$.\n\n- Next, to convert from $14$ feet into yards, we start with the unit conversion\n$1\\, \\textrm{yd} =3 \\, \\textrm{ft}$.\nNow, we divide both sides of the above equation by $3$ and obtain the following:\n$\\dfrac{1}{3}\\, \\textrm{yd} =1 \\, \\textrm{ft}$\nWe want to determine how many yards are in $14$ feet. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation $17{:}$\n$14 \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}yd|= 14 \\cdot 1ft; \\frac{14}{3}yd|= 14ft; 4\\frac{2}{3}yd|= 14ft$\nTherefore, $14\\,\\textrm{ft}$ equals $4\\,\\dfrac{2}{3}\\,\\textrm{yd}$.\n\nIn conclusion, $168\\,\\textrm{in}$ equals\n\n$$\n4\\,\\dfrac{2}{3}\\,\\textrm{yd}\n$$" "To convert $168$ inches into yards, we convert $168$ inches into feet, and then convert the result from feet to yards.\n\n- To convert from $168$ inches into feet, we start with the unit conversion\n$1\\, \\textrm{ft} =12 \\, \\textrm{in}$.\nNow, we divide both sides of the above equation by $12$ and obtain the following:\n$\\dfrac{1}{12}\\, \\textrm{ft} =1 \\, \\textrm{in}$\nWe want to determine how many feet are in $168$ inches. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation $168{:}$\n$$\n\\displaystyle 168 \\cdot \\frac{1}{12}ft|= 168 \\cdot 1in; \\frac{168}{12}ft|= 168in\n$$\nNow, we simplify this fraction by interpreting $\\dfrac{168}{12}$ as the division $168 \\div 12$. We can calculate this using long division:\n$1$$4$$12$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$4$$8$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$8$$\\phantom{0}$$0$\nTherefore, $168\\,\\textrm{in}$ equals $14\\,\\textrm{ft}$.\n\n- Next, to convert from $14$ feet into yards, we start with the unit conversion\n$1\\, \\textrm{yd} =3 \\, \\textrm{ft}$.\nNow, we divide both sides of the above equation by $3$ and obtain the following:\n$\\dfrac{1}{3}\\, \\textrm{yd} =1 \\, \\textrm{ft}$\nWe want to determine how many yards are in $14$ feet. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation $17{:}$\n$$\n\\displaystyle 14 \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}yd|= 14 \\cdot 1ft; \\frac{14}{3}yd|= 14ft; 4\\frac{2}{3}yd|= 14ft\n$$\nTherefore, $14\\,\\textrm{ft}$ equals $4\\,\\dfrac{2}{3}\\,\\textrm{yd}$.\n\nIn conclusion, $168\\,\\textrm{in}$ equals\n\n$$\n4\\,\\dfrac{2}{3}\\,\\textrm{yd}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186192502 :question/problem "Given that $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n$ is divergent, what can be said about the convergence of $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=15}^\\infty a_n$? Assume that $a_n$ is well-defined for $n\\geq 1$." "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$ is divergent, what can be said about the convergence of $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=15}^\\infty a_n\n$$? Assume that $a_n$ is well-defined for $n\\geq 1$."] [:db/cas 17592186192502 :question/worked-solution "Removing the first $k-1$ terms from a divergent series results in a divergent series.\n\nMore precisely, assume $a_n$ is well-defined for\n\n$$\nn\\geq 1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nk\\geq 1\n$$\n\nis an integer. Then, we have the following:\n\n> *If $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n$ is divergent, then $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=k}^\\infty a_n$ is also divergent.*\n\nSo, since\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\nis divergent, we have that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=15}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\nis also divergent." "Removing the first $k-1$ terms from a divergent series results in a divergent series.\n\nMore precisely, assume $a_n$ is well-defined for\n\n$$\nn\\geq 1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nk\\geq 1\n$$\n\nis an integer. Then, we have the following:\n\n> *If $$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$ is divergent, then $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=k}^\\infty a_n\n$$ is also divergent.*\n\nSo, since\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\nis divergent, we have that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=15}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\nis also divergent."] [:db/cas 17592186192503 :question/problem "Given that $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n$ is convergent and $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n$ is divergent, which of the following statements are true? Assume that $b_n$ is well-defined for $n\\geqslant 1$.\n\n1. $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=9}^\\infty a_n$ is convergent\n2. $\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{10^{100}} \\cdot \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n$ is convergent\n3. $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n + \\sum_{n=1}^{40} b_n$ is convergent" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$ is convergent and $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$ is divergent, which of the following statements are true? Assume that $b_n$ is well-defined for $n\\geqslant 1$.\n\n1. $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=9}^\\infty a_n\n$$ is convergent\n2. $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{10^{100}} \\cdot \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$ is convergent\n3. $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n + \\sum_{n=1}^{40} b_n\n$$ is convergent"] [:db/cas 17592186192503 :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the properties of divergent series:\n\n- If $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n$ is divergent, then $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty K\\cdot a_n$ is also divergent for $K\\neq 0$.\n- If $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n$ is convergent and $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n$ is divergent, then $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n$ is divergent.\n- If $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n$ is divergent, then $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=k}^\\infty a_n$ is also divergent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Removing a finite number of terms from a convergent series gives a convergent series.\n- Statement II is false. Any non-zero multiple of a divergent series gives a divergent series.\n- Statement III is true. Note that $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^{40} b_n$ is a **finite series**. Therefore, $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n + \\sum_{n=1}^{40} b_n$ is convergent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "Let's recall the properties of divergent series:\n\n- If $$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$ is divergent, then $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty K\\cdot a_n\n$$ is also divergent for $K\\neq 0$.\n- If $$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$ is convergent and $$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$ is divergent, then $$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$ is divergent.\n- If $$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$ is divergent, then $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=k}^\\infty a_n\n$$ is also divergent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Removing a finite number of terms from a convergent series gives a convergent series.\n- Statement II is false. Any non-zero multiple of a divergent series gives a divergent series.\n- Statement III is true. Note that $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^{40} b_n\n$$ is a **finite series**. Therefore, $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n + \\sum_{n=1}^{40} b_n\n$$ is convergent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186192507 :question/problem "Approximate $\\left.\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{0} \\dfrac{\\mid x\\right|^3}{2^{-x} + x} \\, \\textrm{d}x$ to $2$ decimal places." "Approximate $$\n\\displaystyle \\left.\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{0} \\dfrac{\\mid x\\right|^3}{2^{-x} + x} \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$ to $2$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186192507 :question/worked-solution "We bring up the integral option on most calculators in one of the following ways:\n\n- Press the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,2\\text{nd}\\,}$ (or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{shift}\\,}$) button, then the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\displaystyle\\color{gray}\\,\\displaystyle \\int_{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}}^{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}} \\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{\\big \\mid 0\\big \\mid}}\\,}$ button.\n- Press the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{math}\\,}$ (or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{calc}\\,}$) button and choose the integral option. Note that this might be called $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{fnInt}\\,}$ or similar.\n- To access the absolute value function, you can locate this function on your calculator (on some models, you can access this by typing $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{math}\\,}$ and then selecting $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{abs}\\,}$ under the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{num}\\,}$ submenu). Or, you can recall that $\\mid x \\mid = \\sqrt{x^2}$ and rewrite the integral as\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{0} \\dfrac{(\\sqrt{x^2})^3}{2^{-x} + x} \\, \\textrm{d}x$.\n\nTo calculate our improper integral, we insert *decreasingly small* numbers in place of $-\\infty$ until successive approximations are the same, as follows:\n\n- Evaluating the integral with lower limit $-10$ using a calculator, we have\n$\\left.\\displaystyle \\int_{-10}^{0} \\dfrac{\\mid x\\right|^3}{2^{-x} + x} \\, \\textrm{d}x \\approx 30.954$,\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n- Evaluating the integral with lower limit $-100$ using a calculator, we get\n$\\left.\\displaystyle \\int_{-100}^{0} \\dfrac{\\mid x\\right|^3}{2^{-x} + x} \\, \\textrm{d}x \\approx \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{33.183}$,\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n- Evaluating the integral with lower limit $-200$ using a calculator, we obtain\n$\\left.\\displaystyle \\int_{-200}^{0} \\dfrac{\\mid x\\right|^3}{2^{-x} + x} \\, \\textrm{d}x \\approx \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{33.183}$,\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nWe can stop now since the last two approximations were $33.18$ to $2$ decimal places.\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\left.\\int_{-\\infty}^{0} \\dfrac{\\mid x\\right|^3}{2^{-x} + x} \\, \\textrm{d}x \\approx 33.18\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." "We bring up the integral option on most calculators in one of the following ways:\n\n- Press the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,2\\text{nd}\\,}$ (or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{shift}\\,}$) button, then the $\\displaystyle \\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\displaystyle\\color{gray}\\,\\displaystyle \\int_{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}}^{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}} \\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{\\big \\mid 0\\big \\mid}}\\,}$ button.\n- Press the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{math}\\,}$ (or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{calc}\\,}$) button and choose the integral option. Note that this might be called $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{fnInt}\\,}$ or similar.\n- To access the absolute value function, you can locate this function on your calculator (on some models, you can access this by typing $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{math}\\,}$ and then selecting $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{abs}\\,}$ under the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{num}\\,}$ submenu). Or, you can recall that $\\mid x \\mid = \\sqrt{x^2}$ and rewrite the integral as\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{0} \\dfrac{(\\sqrt{x^2})^3}{2^{-x} + x} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo calculate our improper integral, we insert *decreasingly small* numbers in place of $-\\infty$ until successive approximations are the same, as follows:\n\n- Evaluating the integral with lower limit $-10$ using a calculator, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left.\\displaystyle \\int_{-10}^{0} \\dfrac{\\mid x\\right|^3}{2^{-x} + x} \\, \\textrm{d}x \\approx 30.954,\n$$\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n- Evaluating the integral with lower limit $-100$ using a calculator, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left.\\displaystyle \\int_{-100}^{0} \\dfrac{\\mid x\\right|^3}{2^{-x} + x} \\, \\textrm{d}x \\approx \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{33.183},\n$$\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n- Evaluating the integral with lower limit $-200$ using a calculator, we obtain\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left.\\displaystyle \\int_{-200}^{0} \\dfrac{\\mid x\\right|^3}{2^{-x} + x} \\, \\textrm{d}x \\approx \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{33.183},\n$$\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nWe can stop now since the last two approximations were $33.18$ to $2$ decimal places.\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\left.\\int_{-\\infty}^{0} \\dfrac{\\mid x\\right|^3}{2^{-x} + x} \\, \\textrm{d}x \\approx 33.18\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186192519 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x; \\theta),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i},\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i},\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n = 3$ independent binomial random variables with an unknown probability of success $\\theta$ and $N = 5$ as the number of trials. Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,\\theta ) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{N}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{N-x},\\,x=0,1,…,N \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{5-x},\\,x=0,1,…,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{5-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-\\theta )^{5-{x}_{i}})) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}})+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(5-{x}_{i})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(5-{x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}})+S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(5n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\displaystyle S = \\sum_ {i=1}^n x_i.$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{1}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{2}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{3}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for $l(\\theta)$ becomes\n\n$$\nl(\\theta) =  \\ln\\left( \\prod_{i=1}^n {5 \\choose x_i}\\right) + S\\ln \\theta + (5n - S) \\ln(1-\\theta).\n$$\n\nNow, using the sample data with $n=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}}) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{4})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 5\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =125,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nS = 4 + 1 + 1 = 6.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above values into our expression for $l(\\theta)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(125)+6\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(5\\cdot 3-6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡125+6\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x; \\theta),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i},\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i},\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n = 3$ independent binomial random variables with an unknown probability of success $\\theta$ and $N = 5$ as the number of trials. Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,\\theta ) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{N}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{N-x},\\,x=0,1,…,N \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{5-x},\\,x=0,1,…,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{5-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-\\theta )^{5-{x}_{i}})) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}})+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(5-{x}_{i})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(5-{x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}})+S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(5n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle S = \\sum_ {i=1}^n x_i.\n$$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{1}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{2}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{3}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for $l(\\theta)$ becomes\n\n$$\nl(\\theta) =  \\ln\\left( \\prod_{i=1}^n {5 \\choose x_i}\\right) + S\\ln \\theta + (5n - S) \\ln(1-\\theta).\n$$\n\nNow, using the sample data with $n=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}}) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{4})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 5\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =125,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nS = 4 + 1 + 1 = 6.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above values into our expression for $l(\\theta)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(125)+6\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(5\\cdot 3-6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡125+6\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186192521 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x; \\theta),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = P(X_1=x_1, X_2=x_2, \\ldots , X_n = x_n) = \\displaystyle\\prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i;\\,\\theta).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=6$ independent exponential random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x; \\,\\theta) = \\theta e^{-\\theta x}, \\qquad x\\geq 0.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\theta {e}^{-\\theta {x}_{i}} \\\\  & =(\\theta {e}^{-\\theta {x}_{1}})\\cdot (\\theta {e}^{-\\theta {x}_{2}})⋯(\\theta {e}^{-\\theta {x}_{n}}) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\,{e}^{-\\theta ({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+⋯+{x}_{n})} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\,{e}^{-\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\,{e}^{-S\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\displaystyle S = \\sum\\limits_ {i=1}^n x_i.$ \n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =1.5+2.0+1.1+0.4+3.3+2.7 \\\\  & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=6.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\theta }^{6}\\,{e}^{-11\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x; \\theta),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = P(X_1=x_1, X_2=x_2, \\ldots , X_n = x_n) = \\displaystyle\\prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i;\\,\\theta).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=6$ independent exponential random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x; \\,\\theta) = \\theta e^{-\\theta x}, \\qquad x\\geq 0.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\theta {e}^{-\\theta {x}_{i}} \\\\  & =(\\theta {e}^{-\\theta {x}_{1}})\\cdot (\\theta {e}^{-\\theta {x}_{2}})⋯(\\theta {e}^{-\\theta {x}_{n}}) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\,{e}^{-\\theta ({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+⋯+{x}_{n})} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\,{e}^{-\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\,{e}^{-S\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle S = \\sum\\limits_ {i=1}^n x_i.\n$$ \n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =1.5+2.0+1.1+0.4+3.3+2.7 \\\\  & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=6.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\theta }^{6}\\,{e}^{-11\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186192529 :question/problem "$$\nf(x) = {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{( x - 2 {)}^{2}}} , \\hspace{1em} & x \\ge  3 , \\\\ 0 , \\hspace{1em} & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x \\geq 3$\n\nII. $\\displaystyle \\int_{3}^{\\infty} f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x = 1$\n\nIII. $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" "$$\nf(x) = {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{( x - 2 {)}^{2}}} , \\hspace{1em} & x \\ge  3 , \\\\ 0 , \\hspace{1em} & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x \\geq 3$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{3}^{\\infty} f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x = 1\n$$\n\nIII. $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function"] [:db/cas 17592186192529 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x \\in S$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{S} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for $x \\geq 3,$ we have $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{(x-2)^2} \\geq 0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }(x-2)^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x-2}\\,{|}_{3}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-(0-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, the first and second conditions are satisfied. So, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x \\in S$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{S} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 1\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for $x \\geq 3,$ we have $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{(x-2)^2} \\geq 0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }(x-2)^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x-2}\\,{|}_{3}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-(0-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, the first and second conditions are satisfied. So, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""] [:db/cas 17592186192531 :question/worked-solution "To find the total number of hours Tom spent studying, we need to calculate the sum\n\n$$\n3\\,\\dfrac{1}{8} + 2\\,\\dfrac{3}{8}\n$$\n\nTo add two mixed numbers, we add\n\n- whole numbers to whole numbers, and\n- fractions to fractions.\n\nWith that in mind, let's add our mixed numbers.\n\n- Adding the whole numbers, we get\n$3 + 2 = {\\color{red}5}$.\n- Adding the fractions, we get\n$\\dfrac{1}{8} + \\dfrac{3}{8} = \\dfrac{1+3}{8} = \\dfrac{4}{8}$.\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $4{:}$\n$\\dfrac{4}{8} = \\dfrac{4 \\div 4}{8 \\div 4} = {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{1}{2}}$.\n\nFinally, we combine our results:\n\n$$\n3\\,\\dfrac{1}{8} + 2\\,\\dfrac{3}{8} = {\\color{red}5} + {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{1}{2}} = 5\\,\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Tom spent\n\n$$\n5\\,\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nhours studying." "To find the total number of hours Tom spent studying, we need to calculate the sum\n\n$$\n3\\,\\dfrac{1}{8} + 2\\,\\dfrac{3}{8}\n$$\n\nTo add two mixed numbers, we add\n\n- whole numbers to whole numbers, and\n- fractions to fractions.\n\nWith that in mind, let's add our mixed numbers.\n\n- Adding the whole numbers, we get\n$3 + 2 = {\\color{red}5}$.\n- Adding the fractions, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{8} + \\dfrac{3}{8} = \\dfrac{1+3}{8} = \\dfrac{4}{8}.\n$$\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $4{:}$\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{4}{8} = \\dfrac{4 \\div 4}{8 \\div 4} = {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{1}{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we combine our results:\n\n$$\n3\\,\\dfrac{1}{8} + 2\\,\\dfrac{3}{8} = {\\color{red}5} + {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{1}{2}} = 5\\,\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Tom spent\n\n$$\n5\\,\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nhours studying."] [:db/cas 17592186192532 :question/worked-solution "To find the weight of the tomatoes Natalie picked on August $18$, we need to calculate the sum\n\n$$\n10\\,\\dfrac{7}{8} + 3\\,\\dfrac{5}{8}\n$$\n\nTo add two mixed numbers, we add\n\n- whole numbers to whole numbers, and\n- fractions to fractions.\n\nWith that in mind, let's add our mixed numbers.\n\n- Adding the whole numbers, we get\n$10 + 3 = {\\color{red}13}$.\n- Adding the fractions, we get\n$\\dfrac{7}{8} + \\dfrac{5}{8} = \\dfrac{7+5}{8} = {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{12}{8}}$.\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $4{:}$\n$\\dfrac{12}{8} = \\dfrac{12\\div 4}{8 \\div 4} = \\dfrac{3}{2}$.\nThen, we convert the fraction $\\dfrac{3}{2}$ to a mixed number, as follows:\n$\\dfrac{3}{2} = 1\\,\\textrm{R}1 = {\\color{blue}1\\,\\dfrac{1}{2}}$\n\nFinally, we combine our results:\n\n$$\n10\\,\\dfrac{7}{8} + 3\\,\\dfrac{5}{8} = {\\color{red}13} + {\\color{blue}1\\,\\dfrac{1}{2}} =14\\,\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on August $18$, Natalie collected\n\n$$\n14\\,\\dfrac{1}{2}\\,\\textrm{kg}\n$$\n\nof tomatoes from her garden." "To find the weight of the tomatoes Natalie picked on August $18$, we need to calculate the sum\n\n$$\n10\\,\\dfrac{7}{8} + 3\\,\\dfrac{5}{8}\n$$\n\nTo add two mixed numbers, we add\n\n- whole numbers to whole numbers, and\n- fractions to fractions.\n\nWith that in mind, let's add our mixed numbers.\n\n- Adding the whole numbers, we get\n$10 + 3 = {\\color{red}13}$.\n- Adding the fractions, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{7}{8} + \\dfrac{5}{8} = \\dfrac{7+5}{8} = {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{12}{8}}.\n$$\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $4{:}$\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{12}{8} = \\dfrac{12\\div 4}{8 \\div 4} = \\dfrac{3}{2}.\n$$\nThen, we convert the fraction $\\dfrac{3}{2}$ to a mixed number, as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{3}{2} = 1\\,\\textrm{R}1 = {\\color{blue}1\\,\\dfrac{1}{2}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we combine our results:\n\n$$\n10\\,\\dfrac{7}{8} + 3\\,\\dfrac{5}{8} = {\\color{red}13} + {\\color{blue}1\\,\\dfrac{1}{2}} =14\\,\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on August $18$, Natalie collected\n\n$$\n14\\,\\dfrac{1}{2}\\,\\textrm{kg}\n$$\n\nof tomatoes from her garden."] [:db/cas 17592186192562 :question/problem "What fraction gives a true statement when inserted into the blank space?\n\n$\\dfrac{2}{10} + \\dfrac{4}{10} + \\bbox[2pt, white, border: 1px solid black]{\\phantom{\\dfrac{a}{a}}}= \\dfrac{9}{10}$" "What fraction gives a true statement when inserted into the blank space?\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{2}{10} + \\dfrac{4}{10} + \\bbox[2pt, white, border: 1px solid black]{\\phantom{\\dfrac{a}{a}}}= \\dfrac{9}{10}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186192563 :question/problem "Which of the following are decompositions of the fraction $\\dfrac{7}{10}$?\n\n1. $\\dfrac{1}{10}+\\dfrac{1}{10}+\\dfrac{2}{10}+\\dfrac{3}{10}$\n2. $\\dfrac{2}{10}+\\dfrac{3}{10}+\\dfrac{4}{10}$\n3. $\\dfrac{4}{11}+\\dfrac{3}{11}$" "Which of the following are decompositions of the fraction $\\dfrac{7}{10}$?\n\n1. $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{10}+\\dfrac{1}{10}+\\dfrac{2}{10}+\\dfrac{3}{10}\n$$\n2. $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{2}{10}+\\dfrac{3}{10}+\\dfrac{4}{10}\n$$\n3. $\\dfrac{4}{11}+\\dfrac{3}{11}$"] [:db/cas 17592186192563 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each expression in turn.\n\n- I is a decomposition of $\\dfrac{7}{10}$. Indeed, each fraction in the expression has a denominator of $10$, and the sum of numerators is $7$.\n$\\dfrac{1}{10}+\\dfrac{1}{10}+\\dfrac{2}{10}+\\dfrac{3}{10} = \\dfrac{1+1+2+3}{10} = \\dfrac{7}{10}$\n- II is *not* a decomposition of $\\dfrac{7}{10}$. Indeed, each fraction in the expression has a denominator of $10$, but the sum of numerators is $9$.\n$\\dfrac{2}{10}+\\dfrac{3}{10}+\\dfrac{4}{10} = \\dfrac{2+3+4}{10} = \\dfrac{9}{10}$\n- III is *not* a decomposition of $\\dfrac{7}{10}$. Notice that each fraction in the expression has a denominator of $11$. As a result, this cannot be a decomposition of a fraction with a denominator of $10$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "Let's examine each expression in turn.\n\n- I is a decomposition of $\\dfrac{7}{10}$. Indeed, each fraction in the expression has a denominator of $10$, and the sum of numerators is $7$.\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{10}+\\dfrac{1}{10}+\\dfrac{2}{10}+\\dfrac{3}{10} = \\dfrac{1+1+2+3}{10} = \\dfrac{7}{10}\n$$\n- II is *not* a decomposition of $\\dfrac{7}{10}$. Indeed, each fraction in the expression has a denominator of $10$, but the sum of numerators is $9$.\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{2}{10}+\\dfrac{3}{10}+\\dfrac{4}{10} = \\dfrac{2+3+4}{10} = \\dfrac{9}{10}\n$$\n- III is *not* a decomposition of $\\dfrac{7}{10}$. Notice that each fraction in the expression has a denominator of $11$. As a result, this cannot be a decomposition of a fraction with a denominator of $10$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186192564 :question/problem "$\\bbox[2pt, white, border: 1px solid black]{\\phantom{\\dfrac{a}{a}}} + \\dfrac{2}{4} + \\dfrac{6}{4} = 3 \\, \\dfrac{1}{4}$\n\nWhich of the following fractions gives a true statement when inserted into the blank space?\n\n1. $\\dfrac{5}{4}$\n2. $\\dfrac{3}{4}$\n3. $\\dfrac{4}{6}$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\bbox[2pt, white, border: 1px solid black]{\\phantom{\\dfrac{a}{a}}} + \\dfrac{2}{4} + \\dfrac{6}{4} = 3 \\, \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following fractions gives a true statement when inserted into the blank space?\n\n1. $\\dfrac{5}{4}$\n2. $\\dfrac{3}{4}$\n3. $\\dfrac{4}{6}$"] [:db/cas 17592186192595 :question/problem "A research firm conducted a survey comparing two random samples of $10$ customers from two different gas stations in Miami. The time, in minutes per week, that customers of stations $A$ and $B$ spend at the stations is denoted by $x_i$ and $y_i$, respectively. The results are summarized below.\n$\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\! \\sum_{i=1}^{10} x_i = 72, \\qquad \\sum_{i=1}^{10} x_i^2 = 548, \\qquad \\sum_{i=1}^{10} y_i = 100, \\qquad \\sum_{i=1}^{10} y_i^2 = 1020$\n\nGiven that the samples from each station are independent and the population variance $\\sigma^2$ in each station may be assumed to be the same, find a pooled estimate for $\\sigma^2$, rounded to one decimal place." "A research firm conducted a survey comparing two random samples of $10$ customers from two different gas stations in Miami. The time, in minutes per week, that customers of stations $A$ and $B$ spend at the stations is denoted by $x_i$ and $y_i$, respectively. The results are summarized below.\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\! \\sum_{i=1}^{10} x_i = 72, \\qquad \\sum_{i=1}^{10} x_i^2 = 548, \\qquad \\sum_{i=1}^{10} y_i = 100, \\qquad \\sum_{i=1}^{10} y_i^2 = 1020\n$$\n\nGiven that the samples from each station are independent and the population variance $\\sigma^2$ in each station may be assumed to be the same, find a pooled estimate for $\\sigma^2$, rounded to one decimal place."] [:db/cas 17592186192596 :question/problem "A health technician selects random samples of newborn babies from two different hospitals. In kilograms, the babies' weights from the first and second samples are denoted as $x_i$ and $y_i$, respectively, where the sizes of each sample are $n_1 = 9$ and $n_2 = 7$. The results are summarized below.\n$\\sum_{i=1}^{9} x_i = 29.90, \\qquad \\sum_{i=1}^{9} x_i^2 = 100.99, \\qquad \\sum_{i=1}^{7} y_i = 24.7, \\qquad \\sum_{i=1}^{7} y_i^2 = 89.59$\n\nGiven that the samples from each hospital are independent and the population variance $\\sigma^2$ in each hospital may be assumed to be the same, find a pooled estimate for $\\sigma^2$, rounded to one decimal place." "A health technician selects random samples of newborn babies from two different hospitals. In kilograms, the babies' weights from the first and second samples are denoted as $x_i$ and $y_i$, respectively, where the sizes of each sample are $n_1 = 9$ and $n_2 = 7$. The results are summarized below.\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{9} x_i = 29.90, \\qquad \\sum_{i=1}^{9} x_i^2 = 100.99, \\qquad \\sum_{i=1}^{7} y_i = 24.7, \\qquad \\sum_{i=1}^{7} y_i^2 = 89.59\n$$\n\nGiven that the samples from each hospital are independent and the population variance $\\sigma^2$ in each hospital may be assumed to be the same, find a pooled estimate for $\\sigma^2$, rounded to one decimal place."] [:db/cas 17592186192598 :question/problem "Consider two independent random samples from normal populations. The summary statistics of these samples are given in the table below, where $n$ is the size of each sample, $\\overline{x}$ is the sample mean, and $s^2$ is the sample variance.\n\n|  | Sample 1 | Sample 2 |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $n$ | $24$ | $21$ |\n| $\\overline{x}$ | $7$ | $3.4$ |\n| $s^2$ | $3^2$ | $4^2$ |\n\nFind a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the difference $\\mu_1-\\mu_2$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution $T_k$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores $t_{k,p}$ such that $P(T > t_{k,p}) = p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p$.*\n\n| $k \\setminus p$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.001$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $33$ | $\\,1.692\\,$ | $\\,2.035\\,$ | $\\,3.356\\,$ |\n| $34$ | $\\,1.691\\,$ | $\\,2.032\\,$ | $\\,3.348\\,$ |\n| $35$ | $\\,1.690\\,$ | $\\,2.030\\,$ | $\\,3.340\\,$ |\n| $36$ | $\\,1.688\\,$ | $\\,2.028\\,$ | $\\,3.333\\,$ |\n| $37$ | $\\,1.687\\,$ | $\\,2.026\\,$ | $\\,3.326\\,$ |\n\n*Using the usual notation, the number of degrees of freedom can be approximated as the greatest integer less than or equal to*\n\n$\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{s_1^2}{n_1} + \\dfrac{s_2^2}{n_2} \\right)^2}{\\left(\\dfrac{s_1^4}{n_1^2(n_1-1)} + \\dfrac{s_2^4}{n_2^2(n_2-1)}\\right)}$." "Consider two independent random samples from normal populations. The summary statistics of these samples are given in the table below, where $n$ is the size of each sample, $\\overline{x}$ is the sample mean, and $s^2$ is the sample variance.\n\n|  | Sample 1 | Sample 2 |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $n$ | $24$ | $21$ |\n| $\\overline{x}$ | $7$ | $3.4$ |\n| $s^2$ | $3^2$ | $4^2$ |\n\nFind a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the difference $\\mu_1-\\mu_2$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution $T_k$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores $t_{k,p}$ such that $P(T > t_{k,p}) = p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p$.*\n\n| $k \\setminus p$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.001$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $33$ | $\\,1.692\\,$ | $\\,2.035\\,$ | $\\,3.356\\,$ |\n| $34$ | $\\,1.691\\,$ | $\\,2.032\\,$ | $\\,3.348\\,$ |\n| $35$ | $\\,1.690\\,$ | $\\,2.030\\,$ | $\\,3.340\\,$ |\n| $36$ | $\\,1.688\\,$ | $\\,2.028\\,$ | $\\,3.333\\,$ |\n| $37$ | $\\,1.687\\,$ | $\\,2.026\\,$ | $\\,3.326\\,$ |\n\n*Using the usual notation, the number of degrees of freedom can be approximated as the greatest integer less than or equal to*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{s_1^2}{n_1} + \\dfrac{s_2^2}{n_2} \\right)^2}{\\left(\\dfrac{s_1^4}{n_1^2(n_1-1)} + \\dfrac{s_2^4}{n_2^2(n_2-1)}\\right)}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186192598 :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n- both populations are normally distributed.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the difference $\\mu_1-\\mu_2$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n(\\overline{x}_1-\\overline{x}_2) \\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $E=t_{r, \\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{s_1^2}{n_1} + \\dfrac{s_2^2}{n_2}}$ is the margin of error,\n- $\\overline{x}_1$ and $\\overline{x}_2$ are the sample means, $\\mu_1$ and $\\mu_2$ are the population means, $s_1^2$ and $s_2^2$ are the sample variances, $n_1$ and $n_2$ are the sample sizes,\n- $P(T > t_{r, \\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $r$ degrees of freedom.\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n$\\overline{x}_1-\\overline{x}_2 = 7 - 3. 4 = 3.6$\n- **Step 2.** Approximate the degrees of freedom.\n$((\\frac{s_{1}^{2}}{n_{1}} + \\frac{s_{2}^{2}}{n_{2}})^{2})/(((s_{1}^{4})/(n_{1}^{2}(n_{1} - 1)) + (s_{2}^{4})/(n_{2}^{2}(n_{2} - 1)))) = ((\\frac{3^{2}}{24} + \\frac{4^{2}}{21})^{2})/(((3^{4})/(24^{2}(24 - 1)) + (4^{4})/(21^{2}(21 - 1)))) \\approx 36.78$\nTherefore, we have $r=36$.\n- **Step 3.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval, we have\n$\\alpha=1-0.95=0.05 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.025$.\nFrom the table we can see that $P(T > 2.028) = 0.025$. As a result,\n$t_{r, \\alpha/2} =2.028$.![](images/be/beea42e515e91b05e094adb7096730ee51d3796ed0d2fc9998c73747691af243.png)\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n$E = 2.028 \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{3^2}{24} + \\dfrac{4^2}{21}} \\approx 2.162$\n- **Step 4.** Construct the $95\\%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n$3.6 ± 2.162$" "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n- both populations are normally distributed.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the difference $\\mu_1-\\mu_2$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n(\\overline{x}_1-\\overline{x}_2) \\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle E=t_{r, \\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{s_1^2}{n_1} + \\dfrac{s_2^2}{n_2}}\n$$ is the margin of error,\n- $\\overline{x}_1$ and $\\overline{x}_2$ are the sample means, $\\mu_1$ and $\\mu_2$ are the population means, $s_1^2$ and $s_2^2$ are the sample variances, $n_1$ and $n_2$ are the sample sizes,\n- $P(T > t_{r, \\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $r$ degrees of freedom.\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n$\\overline{x}_1-\\overline{x}_2 = 7 - 3. 4 = 3.6$\n- **Step 2.** Approximate the degrees of freedom.\n$$\n\\displaystyle ((\\frac{s_{1}^{2}}{n_{1}} + \\frac{s_{2}^{2}}{n_{2}})^{2})/(((s_{1}^{4})/(n_{1}^{2}(n_{1} - 1)) + (s_{2}^{4})/(n_{2}^{2}(n_{2} - 1)))) = ((\\frac{3^{2}}{24} + \\frac{4^{2}}{21})^{2})/(((3^{4})/(24^{2}(24 - 1)) + (4^{4})/(21^{2}(21 - 1)))) \\approx 36.78\n$$\nTherefore, we have $r=36$.\n- **Step 3.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\alpha=1-0.95=0.05 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.025.\n$$\nFrom the table we can see that $P(T > 2.028) = 0.025$. As a result,\n$t_{r, \\alpha/2} =2.028$.![](images/be/beea42e515e91b05e094adb7096730ee51d3796ed0d2fc9998c73747691af243.png)\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n$$\n\\displaystyle E = 2.028 \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{3^2}{24} + \\dfrac{4^2}{21}} \\approx 2.162\n$$\n- **Step 4.** Construct the $95\\%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n$3.6 ± 2.162$"] [:db/cas 17592186192599 :question/problem "The length of matchsticks, in millimeters, produced by machines X and Y are known to be normally distributed with means $\\mu_1$ and $\\mu_2$. Engineers sampled matchsticks from both machines. The summary statistics of these samples are given in the table below, where $n$ is the number of matchsticks in each sample, in millimeters, $\\overline{x}$ is the sample mean length, and $s^2$ is the sample variance.\n\n|  | Machine X | Machine Y |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $n$ | $152$ | $164$ |\n| $\\overline{x}$ | $53$ | $52$ |\n| $s^2$ | $1.5^2$ | $1^2$ |\n\nAssuming that the samples are independent, which statements are true?\n\n1. The end-points of the $99\\%$ confidence interval for the difference between the mean lengths are approximately $1 \\pm 0.375$.\n2. All values contained within the $99\\%$ confidence interval are positive.\n3. The confidence interval data suggests that the mean lengths of matchsticks produced by the machines are not equal.\n\n*Hint: Using the usual notation, the number of degrees of freedom can be approximated as follows:*\n\n$\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{s_1^2}{n_1} + \\dfrac{s_2^2}{n_2} \\right)^2}{\\left(\\dfrac{s_1^4}{n_1^2(n_1-1)} + \\dfrac{s_2^4}{n_2^2(n_2-1)}\\right)}$\n\n*You may use the fact that $P(T > 2.595) = 0.005$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $260$ degrees of freedom.*" "The length of matchsticks, in millimeters, produced by machines X and Y are known to be normally distributed with means $\\mu_1$ and $\\mu_2$. Engineers sampled matchsticks from both machines. The summary statistics of these samples are given in the table below, where $n$ is the number of matchsticks in each sample, in millimeters, $\\overline{x}$ is the sample mean length, and $s^2$ is the sample variance.\n\n|  | Machine X | Machine Y |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $n$ | $152$ | $164$ |\n| $\\overline{x}$ | $53$ | $52$ |\n| $s^2$ | $1.5^2$ | $1^2$ |\n\nAssuming that the samples are independent, which statements are true?\n\n1. The end-points of the $99\\%$ confidence interval for the difference between the mean lengths are approximately $1 \\pm 0.375$.\n2. All values contained within the $99\\%$ confidence interval are positive.\n3. The confidence interval data suggests that the mean lengths of matchsticks produced by the machines are not equal.\n\n*Hint: Using the usual notation, the number of degrees of freedom can be approximated as follows:*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{s_1^2}{n_1} + \\dfrac{s_2^2}{n_2} \\right)^2}{\\left(\\dfrac{s_1^4}{n_1^2(n_1-1)} + \\dfrac{s_2^4}{n_2^2(n_2-1)}\\right)}\n$$\n\n*You may use the fact that $P(T > 2.595) = 0.005$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $260$ degrees of freedom.*"] [:db/cas 17592186192599 :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n- both populations are normally distributed.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the difference $\\mu_1-\\mu_2$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n(\\overline{x}_1-\\overline{x}_2) \\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $E=t_{r, \\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{s_1^2}{n_1} + \\dfrac{s_2^2}{n_2}}$ is the margin of error,\n- $\\overline{x}_1$ and $\\overline{x}_2$ are the sample means, $\\mu_1$ and $\\mu_2$ are the population means, $s_1^2$ and $s_2^2$ are the sample variances, $n_1$ and $n_2$ are the sample sizes,\n- $P(T > t_{r, \\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $r$ degrees of freedom.\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n$\\overline{x}_1-\\overline{x}_2 = 53-52 = 1$\n- **Step 2.** Approximate the degrees of freedom.\n$((\\frac{s_{1}^{2}}{n_{1}} + \\frac{s_{2}^{2}}{n_{2}})^{2})/(((s_{1}^{4})/(n_{1}^{2}(n_{1} - 1)) + (s_{2}^{4})/(n_{2}^{2}(n_{2} - 1)))) = ((\\frac{1.5^{2}}{152} + \\frac{1^{2}}{164})^{2})/(((1.5^{4})/(152^{2}(152 - 1)) + (1^{4})/(164^{2}(164 - 1)))) \\approx 260.1$\nTherefore, we have $r=260$.\n- **Step 3.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99\\%$ confidence interval, we have\n$\\alpha=1-0.99=0.01 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.005$.\nWe are given that $P(T > 2.595) = 0.005$. As a result,\n$t_{r, \\alpha/2} =2.595$.![](images/e0/e05e9db7dc98c81ea409797333234c91df41276fa89e6cff5a2425c23d8cc8ae.png)\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n$E = 2.595\\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1.5^2}{152} + \\dfrac{1^2}{164}} \\approx 0.375$\n- **Step 4.** Construct the $99\\%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n$1 ± 0.375$\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The endpoints of the confidence interval are approximately $1 \\pm 0.375$.\n- Statement II is true. The confidence interval contains only positive values.\n$(1 - 0.375, \\, 1 + 0.375) = (0.625,\\, 1.375)$\n- Statement III is true. Since the confidence interval does not contain zero, there is sufficient evidence to suggest that means are not equal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II and III.\"" "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n- both populations are normally distributed.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the difference $\\mu_1-\\mu_2$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n(\\overline{x}_1-\\overline{x}_2) \\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle E=t_{r, \\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{s_1^2}{n_1} + \\dfrac{s_2^2}{n_2}}\n$$ is the margin of error,\n- $\\overline{x}_1$ and $\\overline{x}_2$ are the sample means, $\\mu_1$ and $\\mu_2$ are the population means, $s_1^2$ and $s_2^2$ are the sample variances, $n_1$ and $n_2$ are the sample sizes,\n- $P(T > t_{r, \\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $r$ degrees of freedom.\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n$\\overline{x}_1-\\overline{x}_2 = 53-52 = 1$\n- **Step 2.** Approximate the degrees of freedom.\n$$\n\\displaystyle ((\\frac{s_{1}^{2}}{n_{1}} + \\frac{s_{2}^{2}}{n_{2}})^{2})/(((s_{1}^{4})/(n_{1}^{2}(n_{1} - 1)) + (s_{2}^{4})/(n_{2}^{2}(n_{2} - 1)))) = ((\\frac{1.5^{2}}{152} + \\frac{1^{2}}{164})^{2})/(((1.5^{4})/(152^{2}(152 - 1)) + (1^{4})/(164^{2}(164 - 1)))) \\approx 260.1\n$$\nTherefore, we have $r=260$.\n- **Step 3.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99\\%$ confidence interval, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\alpha=1-0.99=0.01 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.005.\n$$\nWe are given that $P(T > 2.595) = 0.005$. As a result,\n$t_{r, \\alpha/2} =2.595$.![](images/e0/e05e9db7dc98c81ea409797333234c91df41276fa89e6cff5a2425c23d8cc8ae.png)\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n$$\n\\displaystyle E = 2.595\\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1.5^2}{152} + \\dfrac{1^2}{164}} \\approx 0.375\n$$\n- **Step 4.** Construct the $99\\%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n$1 ± 0.375$\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The endpoints of the confidence interval are approximately $1 \\pm 0.375$.\n- Statement II is true. The confidence interval contains only positive values.\n$(1 - 0.375, \\, 1 + 0.375) = (0.625,\\, 1.375)$\n- Statement III is true. Since the confidence interval does not contain zero, there is sufficient evidence to suggest that means are not equal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II and III.\""] [:db/cas 17592186192602 :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the differences $D_i$. However, the sample size\n\n$$\nn = 60\\geq 30\n$$\n\nis *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\overline{D} \\sim N\\left(\\mu, \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\overline{D}$ is the sample mean,\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean of $D_i$,\n- $\\sigma^2$ is the population variance of $D_i$, and\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the mean difference $\\mu$ between $X$ and $Y$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overline{d} \\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\overline{d}=\\overline{x}-\\overline{y}\\,$ is a point estimate for $\\mu$,\n- $E=z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}$ is the margin of error,\n- $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $Z\\sim N(0,1)$.\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find a point estimate for $\\mu{:}$We compute our point estimate as follows:\n$\\overline{d} = \\overline{x} - \\overline{y} = 11.8-9.6= 2.2$\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval, we have\n$\\alpha=1-0.95=0.05 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.025$.\nWe are given that $P(Z > 1.96) = 0.025$. As a result,\n$z_{\\alpha/2} =1.96$.\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n$E = 1.96 \\cdot \\dfrac{4.53}{\\sqrt{60}} = 1.146$\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean of $D{:}$Our $95\\%$ confidence interval is\n$2.2 ± 1.146$." "We do not know the distribution of the differences $D_i$. However, the sample size\n\n$$\nn = 60\\geq 30\n$$\n\nis *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\overline{D} \\sim N\\left(\\mu, \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\overline{D}$ is the sample mean,\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean of $D_i$,\n- $\\sigma^2$ is the population variance of $D_i$, and\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the mean difference $\\mu$ between $X$ and $Y$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overline{d} \\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\overline{d}=\\overline{x}-\\overline{y}\\,$ is a point estimate for $\\mu$,\n- $E=z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}$ is the margin of error,\n- $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $Z\\sim N(0,1)$.\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find a point estimate for $\\mu{:}$We compute our point estimate as follows:\n$\\overline{d} = \\overline{x} - \\overline{y} = 11.8-9.6= 2.2$\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\alpha=1-0.95=0.05 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.025.\n$$\nWe are given that $P(Z > 1.96) = 0.025$. As a result,\n$z_{\\alpha/2} =1.96$.\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n$E = 1.96 \\cdot \\dfrac{4.53}{\\sqrt{60}} = 1.146$\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean of $D{:}$Our $95\\%$ confidence interval is\n$2.2 ± 1.146$."] [:db/cas 17592186192603 :question/worked-solution "Let us call\n\n$$\nD_i = X_i-Y_i\n$$\n\nthe random variable that represents the difference in the reaction times of a randomly selected reaction.\n\nWe do not know the distribution of the differences $D_i$. However, the sample size\n\n$$\nn = 45\\geq 30\n$$\n\nis *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\overline{D} \\sim N\\left(\\mu, \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\overline{D}$ is the sample mean,\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean of $D_i$,\n- $\\sigma^2$ is the population variance of $D_i$, and\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the mean difference $\\mu$ between $X$ and $Y$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overline{d} \\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\overline{d}=\\overline{x}-\\overline{y}$ is a point estimate for $\\mu$,\n- $E=z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}$ is the margin of error,\n- $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $Z\\sim N(0,1)$.\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find a point estimate for $\\mu{:}$\n$\\overline{d} = \\overline{x} - \\overline{y} = 36-30 = 6$\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval, we have\n$\\alpha=1-0.95=0.05 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.025$.\nWe are given that $P(Z > 1.96) = 0.025$. As a result,\n$z_{\\alpha/2} =1.96$.\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n$E = 1.96 \\cdot \\dfrac{22}{\\sqrt{45}} \\approx 6.43$\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean of $D{:}$\n$6 ± 6.43$\n\nNote that the confidence interval contains zero.\n\n$$\n(6-6.43, \\, 6+6.43) = (-0.43,\\,12.43)\n$$\n\nSince the confidence interval contains zero, the data suggests that the average reaction time with the catalyst is approximately the same as without the catalyst." "Let us call\n\n$$\nD_i = X_i-Y_i\n$$\n\nthe random variable that represents the difference in the reaction times of a randomly selected reaction.\n\nWe do not know the distribution of the differences $D_i$. However, the sample size\n\n$$\nn = 45\\geq 30\n$$\n\nis *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\overline{D} \\sim N\\left(\\mu, \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\overline{D}$ is the sample mean,\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean of $D_i$,\n- $\\sigma^2$ is the population variance of $D_i$, and\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the mean difference $\\mu$ between $X$ and $Y$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overline{d} \\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\overline{d}=\\overline{x}-\\overline{y}$ is a point estimate for $\\mu$,\n- $E=z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}$ is the margin of error,\n- $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $Z\\sim N(0,1)$.\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find a point estimate for $\\mu{:}$\n$\\overline{d} = \\overline{x} - \\overline{y} = 36-30 = 6$\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\alpha=1-0.95=0.05 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.025.\n$$\nWe are given that $P(Z > 1.96) = 0.025$. As a result,\n$z_{\\alpha/2} =1.96$.\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n$E = 1.96 \\cdot \\dfrac{22}{\\sqrt{45}} \\approx 6.43$\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean of $D{:}$\n$6 ± 6.43$\n\nNote that the confidence interval contains zero.\n\n$$\n(6-6.43, \\, 6+6.43) = (-0.43,\\,12.43)\n$$\n\nSince the confidence interval contains zero, the data suggests that the average reaction time with the catalyst is approximately the same as without the catalyst."] [:db/cas 17592186192604 :question/worked-solution "To construct a confidence interval, we start with a random variable that depends on the samples and has a known distribution under certain conditions.\n\n> Consider the random variable\n> $Z|= ((X^(―) - Y^(―)) - μ)/\\left(\\frac{σ}{\\sqrt{n}}\\right); = ((X^(―) - Y^(―)) - μ)/(\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{27}}\\right)); = ((X^(―) - Y^(―)) - μ)/(\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right))$,\n> where $\\overline{X}$ and $\\overline{Y}$ are the sample means of the given samples.\n\nIt is known that if the samples are paired and the differences between each pair are normally distributed with a population mean $\\mu$ and known population variance, $Z$ has a standard normal distribution.\n\n> We have that\n> $Z \\sim {\\color{black}N(0,1)}$.\n\nWe wish to find an interval that we're $95\\%$ confident that the random variable $Z$ lies within. This interval is indicated in the diagram below:\n\n![](images/ce/ce18d66a53578e13b52bc7d6b6fd44ab6320ed98795be5621ae892727e7ef8eb.png)\n\nSince we're computing a $95\\%$ confidence interval, the area bounded by each \"tail\" that we're excluding from our confidence interval must be\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1 - 0.95}{2} = 0.025\n$$\n\nWe can find the endpoints of such interval using a table of values for the normal distribution.\n\n> Using a table of values for the normal distribution, we require a value $c$ such that\n> $P(Z > c) = {\\color{black}0.025}$.\n> In other words,\n> $P(-c < Z < c) = 0.95$.\n> Let's call $c = z_{0.025}$.\n> Now, we find the appropriate confidence interval by solving the inequality inside the parentheses of the last probability statement for the difference mean $\\mu$.\n> $\\begin{bmatrix}-z_{0.025} & < Z < z_{0.025} \\\\ -z_{0.025} & < ((x^(―) - y^(―)) - μ)/(\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)) < z_{0.025}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTo solve this inequality, we first multiply by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nto get rid of the denominator:\n\n> We solve the inequality as follows:\n> $-\\frac{z_{0.025}}{3} \\mid < (x^(―) - y^(―)) - μ < \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3}$\n\nThen, we multiply all sides by $-1$:\n\n> $\\frac{z_{0.025}}{3} \\mid > μ - (x^(―) - y^(―)) > - \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3}$\n\nNext, we add $\\overline{x} - \\overline{y}$:\n\n> $\\frac{z_{0.025}}{3} + x^(―) - y^(―) \\mid > μ > - \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3} + x^(―) - y^(―)$\n\nNext, we rewrite our inequality so that the smaller terms are on the left-hand side.\n\n> $x^(―) - y^(―) - \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3} \\mid < μ < x^(―) - y^(―) + \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3}$\n\nNow, we write our result more compactly.\n\n> Finally, we can write our interval as follows:\n> $(x^(―) - y^(―)) ± z_{0.025} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}$\n\nThe full proof is given below:\n\n> Consider the random variable\n> $Z|= ((X^(―) - Y^(―)) - μ)/\\left(\\frac{σ}{\\sqrt{n}}\\right); = ((X^(―) - Y^(―)) - μ)/(\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{27}}\\right)); = ((X^(―) - Y^(―)) - μ)/(\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right))$,\n> where $\\overline{X}$ and $\\overline{Y}$ are the sample means of the given samples.\n> We have that\n> $Z \\sim {\\color{black}N(0,1)}$.\n> Using a table of values for the normal distribution, we require a value $c$ such that\n> $P(Z > c) = {\\color{black}0.025}$.\n> In other words,\n> $P(-c < Z < c) = 0.95$.\n> Let's call $c = z_{0.025}$.\n> Now, we find the appropriate confidence interval by solving the inequality inside the parentheses of the last probability statement for the difference mean $\\mu$.\n> $\\begin{bmatrix}-z_{0.025} & < Z < z_{0.025} \\\\ -z_{0.025} & < ((x^(―) - y^(―)) - μ)/(\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)) < z_{0.025}\\end{bmatrix}$\n> We solve the inequality as follows:\n> $-\\frac{z_{0.025}}{3} \\mid < (x^(―) - y^(―)) - μ < \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3}$\n> $\\frac{z_{0.025}}{3} \\mid > μ - (x^(―) - y^(―)) > - \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3}$\n> $\\frac{z_{0.025}}{3} + x^(―) - y^(―) \\mid > μ > - \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3} + x^(―) - y^(―)$\n> $x^(―) - y^(―) - \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3} \\mid < μ < x^(―) - y^(―) + \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3}$\n> Finally, we can write our interval as follows:\n> $(x^(―) - y^(―)) ± z_{0.025} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}$" "To construct a confidence interval, we start with a random variable that depends on the samples and has a known distribution under certain conditions.\n\n> Consider the random variable\n> $$\n\\displaystyle Z|= ((X^(―) - Y^(―)) - μ)/\\left(\\frac{σ}{\\sqrt{n}}\\right); = ((X^(―) - Y^(―)) - μ)/(\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{27}}\\right)); = ((X^(―) - Y^(―)) - μ)/(\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)),\n$$\n> where $\\overline{X}$ and $\\overline{Y}$ are the sample means of the given samples.\n\nIt is known that if the samples are paired and the differences between each pair are normally distributed with a population mean $\\mu$ and known population variance, $Z$ has a standard normal distribution.\n\n> We have that\n> $Z \\sim {\\color{black}N(0,1)}$.\n\nWe wish to find an interval that we're $95\\%$ confident that the random variable $Z$ lies within. This interval is indicated in the diagram below:\n\n![](images/ce/ce18d66a53578e13b52bc7d6b6fd44ab6320ed98795be5621ae892727e7ef8eb.png)\n\nSince we're computing a $95\\%$ confidence interval, the area bounded by each \"tail\" that we're excluding from our confidence interval must be\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1 - 0.95}{2} = 0.025\n$$\n\nWe can find the endpoints of such interval using a table of values for the normal distribution.\n\n> Using a table of values for the normal distribution, we require a value $c$ such that\n> $P(Z > c) = {\\color{black}0.025}$.\n> In other words,\n> $P(-c < Z < c) = 0.95$.\n> Let's call $c = z_{0.025}$.\n> Now, we find the appropriate confidence interval by solving the inequality inside the parentheses of the last probability statement for the difference mean $\\mu$.\n> $\\begin{bmatrix}-z_{0.025} & < Z < z_{0.025} \\\\ -z_{0.025} & < ((x^(―) - y^(―)) - μ)/(\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)) < z_{0.025}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTo solve this inequality, we first multiply by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nto get rid of the denominator:\n\n> We solve the inequality as follows:\n> $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{z_{0.025}}{3} \\mid < (x^(―) - y^(―)) - μ < \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3}\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply all sides by $-1$:\n\n> $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3} \\mid > μ - (x^(―) - y^(―)) > - \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3}\n$$\n\nNext, we add $\\overline{x} - \\overline{y}$:\n\n> $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3} + x^(―) - y^(―) \\mid > μ > - \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3} + x^(―) - y^(―)\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite our inequality so that the smaller terms are on the left-hand side.\n\n> $$\n\\displaystyle x^(―) - y^(―) - \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3} \\mid < μ < x^(―) - y^(―) + \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we write our result more compactly.\n\n> Finally, we can write our interval as follows:\n> $(x^(―) - y^(―)) ± z_{0.025} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}$\n\nThe full proof is given below:\n\n> Consider the random variable\n> $$\n\\displaystyle Z|= ((X^(―) - Y^(―)) - μ)/\\left(\\frac{σ}{\\sqrt{n}}\\right); = ((X^(―) - Y^(―)) - μ)/(\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{27}}\\right)); = ((X^(―) - Y^(―)) - μ)/(\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)),\n$$\n> where $\\overline{X}$ and $\\overline{Y}$ are the sample means of the given samples.\n> We have that\n> $Z \\sim {\\color{black}N(0,1)}$.\n> Using a table of values for the normal distribution, we require a value $c$ such that\n> $P(Z > c) = {\\color{black}0.025}$.\n> In other words,\n> $P(-c < Z < c) = 0.95$.\n> Let's call $c = z_{0.025}$.\n> Now, we find the appropriate confidence interval by solving the inequality inside the parentheses of the last probability statement for the difference mean $\\mu$.\n> $\\begin{bmatrix}-z_{0.025} & < Z < z_{0.025} \\\\ -z_{0.025} & < ((x^(―) - y^(―)) - μ)/(\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)) < z_{0.025}\\end{bmatrix}$\n> We solve the inequality as follows:\n> $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{z_{0.025}}{3} \\mid < (x^(―) - y^(―)) - μ < \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3}\n$$\n> $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3} \\mid > μ - (x^(―) - y^(―)) > - \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3}\n$$\n> $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3} + x^(―) - y^(―) \\mid > μ > - \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3} + x^(―) - y^(―)\n$$\n> $$\n\\displaystyle x^(―) - y^(―) - \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3} \\mid < μ < x^(―) - y^(―) + \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3}\n$$\n> Finally, we can write our interval as follows:\n> $(x^(―) - y^(―)) ± z_{0.025} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}$"] [:db/cas 17592186192936 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{∞}\\frac{2}{4x^{2} + 1}dx$." "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{∞}\\frac{2}{4x^{2} + 1}dx.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186192937 :question/problem "What is $\\displaystyle \\int_{- ∞}^{3}\\frac{3}{9 + z^{2}}dz$?" "What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{- ∞}^{3}\\frac{3}{9 + z^{2}}dz\n$$?"] [:db/cas 17592186192938 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\sqrt{3}/6}\\frac{1}{1+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t=$" "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\sqrt{3}/6}\\frac{1}{1+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186192939 :question/problem "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186192944 :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The second set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The third set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\\\\\,1\\le x\\le 4,\\\\\\,1\\le y\\le 4\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$; $\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\\\\\,0\\le y\\le x+1,\\\\\\,-1\\le x\\le 1\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ only." "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The second set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The third set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\\\\\,1\\le x\\le 4,\\\\\\,1\\le y\\le 4\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered};\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\\\\\,0\\le y\\le x+1,\\\\\\,-1\\le x\\le 1\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186192964 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are ${\\lambda }_{1}=10$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=5.$\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=10:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-10I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-10I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are ${\\lambda }_{1}=10$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=5.$\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=10:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-10I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-10I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186192965 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=10,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=5,\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=3.\n$$\n\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=10,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=5,\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=3.\n$$\n\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 