igenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 11\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-2I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186192968 :question/problem "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below.\n$C = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\nFind the first principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $6$, $3$, and $0$." "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below.\n$$\n\\displaystyle C = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nFind the first principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $6$, $3$, and $0$."] [:db/cas 17592186192979 :question/problem "Let $\\widehat{\\,y}={\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=3.5+5x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $({x}_{1},{y}_{1}),\\,…,\\,({x}_{20},{y}_{20})$ of $n=20$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=2,$ the mean square error of the model is $8,$ and ${S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}=24,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.101)=0.025,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $18$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for ${\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\n*" "Let $\\widehat{\\,y}={\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=3.5+5x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $({x}_{1},{y}_{1}),\\,…,\\,({x}_{20},{y}_{20})$ of $n=20$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=2,$ the mean square error of the model is $8,$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}=24,\n$$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.101)=0.025,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $18$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for ${\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\n*"] [:db/cas 17592186192980 :question/problem "| **Age ($x$)** | **$3$** | **$5$** | **$7$** | **$9$** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **Height ($y$)** | $95$ | $106$ | $110$ | $125$ |\n\nThe table above gives the ages, in years, and the corresponding heights, in centimeters, of four children. The corresponding linear regression line fitted using this sample is $\\widehat{y} = 80.8 + 4.7x$.\nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $x^(―) = 6$, and $MSE = 10.1$, find a $95 \\mathrm{\\% }$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 4.303) = 0.025$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the $y$-intecept is*\n$SE[\\widehat{β}_{0}] = \\sqrt{MSE \\cdot (\\frac{1}{n} + (x^(―)^{2})/(S_{xx}))}$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." "| **Age ($x$)** | **$3$** | **$5$** | **$7$** | **$9$** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **Height ($y$)** | $95$ | $106$ | $110$ | $125$ |\n\nThe table above gives the ages, in years, and the corresponding heights, in centimeters, of four children. The corresponding linear regression line fitted using this sample is $\\widehat{y} = 80.8 + 4.7x$.\nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $x^(―) = 6$, and $MSE = 10.1$, find a $95 \\mathrm{\\% }$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 4.303) = 0.025$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the $y$-intecept is*\n$$\n\\displaystyle SE[\\widehat{β}_{0}] = \\sqrt{MSE \\cdot (\\frac{1}{n} + (x^(―)^{2})/(S_{xx}))}.\n$$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186192982 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i},\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i},\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent binomial random variables with an unknown probability of success $\\theta $ and $N=5$ as the number of trials. Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,\\theta ) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{N}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{N-x},\\,x=0,1,…,N \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{5-x},\\,x=0,1,…,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{5-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-\\theta )^{5-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}})+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(5-{x}_{i})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(5-{x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}})+S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(5n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{1}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{2}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{3}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{4}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for $l(\\theta )$ becomes\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}}))+S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(5n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\n$$\n\nNow, using the sample data with $n=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}}) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{5})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{3})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 10\\cdot 10\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =500,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nS=5+3+2+1=11.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above values into our expression for $l(\\theta )$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(500)+11\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(5\\cdot 4-11)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(500)+11\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=500+11+9=520.\n$$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i},\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i},\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent binomial random variables with an unknown probability of success $\\theta $ and $N=5$ as the number of trials. Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,\\theta ) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{N}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{N-x},\\,x=0,1,…,N \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{5-x},\\,x=0,1,…,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{5-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-\\theta )^{5-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}})+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(5-{x}_{i})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(5-{x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}})+S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(5n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.\n$$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{1}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{2}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{3}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{4}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for $l(\\theta )$ becomes\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}}))+S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(5n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\n$$\n\nNow, using the sample data with $n=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}}) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{5})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{3})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 10\\cdot 10\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =500,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nS=5+3+2+1=11.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above values into our expression for $l(\\theta )$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(500)+11\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(5\\cdot 4-11)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(500)+11\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=500+11+9=520.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186192984 :question/problem "Suppose $X_{1}, X_{2}, …, X_{12}$ is an I.I.D random sample with $X_{i}∼N(θ, 1^{2})$, where $θ$ is an unknown mean. For a particular sample $x_{1}, x_{2}, …, x_{12}$, you're given that\n$∑_(i = 1)^(12)x_{i} = 30.2, ∑_(i = 1)^(12)x_{i}^{2} = 90.6$.\nGiven that the log-likelihood function of the sample is\n$l(θ) =-6\\ln (2π) + a + bθ + cθ^{2}$,\nwhat is the value of $a + b + c$?\n\n*Hint: The probability density function for a normal random variable with mean $θ$ and standard deviation $1$ is given by*\n$f(x) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2π}}e^{- (x - θ)^{2}/2}, x ∈ (- ∞, ∞)$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." "Suppose $X_{1}, X_{2}, …, X_{12}$ is an I.I.D random sample with $X_{i}∼N(θ, 1^{2})$, where $θ$ is an unknown mean. For a particular sample $x_{1}, x_{2}, …, x_{12}$, you're given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle ∑_(i = 1)^(12)x_{i} = 30.2, ∑_(i = 1)^(12)x_{i}^{2} = 90.6.\n$$\nGiven that the log-likelihood function of the sample is\n$l(θ) =-6\\ln (2π) + a + bθ + cθ^{2}$,\nwhat is the value of $a + b + c$?\n\n*Hint: The probability density function for a normal random variable with mean $θ$ and standard deviation $1$ is given by*\n$f(x) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2π}}e^{- (x - θ)^{2}/2}, x ∈ (- ∞, ∞)$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186192985 :question/problem "Suppose $X_{1}, X_{2}, …, X_{7}$ is an I.I.D random sample with $X_{i}∼Exp(θ)$ and unknown rate parameter $θ$. For a particular sample $x_{1}, x_{2}, …, x_{7}$, you're given that\n$∑_(i = 1)^(7)x_{i} = 18.2$.\n\nFind the likelihood function of the sample.\n\n*Hint: The probability density function for an exponential random variable is given by*\n$f(x; θ) = θe^{- θx}, x \\ge 0$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." "Suppose $X_{1}, X_{2}, …, X_{7}$ is an I.I.D random sample with $X_{i}∼Exp(θ)$ and unknown rate parameter $θ$. For a particular sample $x_{1}, x_{2}, …, x_{7}$, you're given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle ∑_(i = 1)^(7)x_{i} = 18.2.\n$$\n\nFind the likelihood function of the sample.\n\n*Hint: The probability density function for an exponential random variable is given by*\n$f(x; θ) = θe^{- θx}, x \\ge 0$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186192986 :question/problem "Let $X_{1}, X_{2}, …, X_{15}$ be I.I.D. random variables with probability density function\n$f(x; θ) = θx^{- θ - 1}, x \\ge 1$,\nwhere $θ$ is an unknown parameter. For a particular sample $x_{1}, x_{2}, …, x_{15}$, you're given that\n$∏_(i = 1)^(15)x_{i} = 40.6$.\nFind the likelihood function of the sample.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." "Let $X_{1}, X_{2}, …, X_{15}$ be I.I.D. random variables with probability density function\n$f(x; θ) = θx^{- θ - 1}, x \\ge 1$,\nwhere $θ$ is an unknown parameter. For a particular sample $x_{1}, x_{2}, …, x_{15}$, you're given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle ∏_(i = 1)^(15)x_{i} = 40.6.\n$$\nFind the likelihood function of the sample.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186192988 :question/problem "Use a calculator to compute $\\displaystyle \\int_{1.5}^{e}e^{-x^{2}}\\cos 2xdx$. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n> A graphing calculator is required to answer this question." "Use a calculator to compute $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{1.5}^{e}e^{-x^{2}}\\cos 2xdx.\n$$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n> A graphing calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186192989 :question/problem "$\\displaystyle \\int_{0.2}^{0.5}(\\tan (3x))/(x)dx \\approx$\n\n> A graphing calculator is required to answer this question." "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{0.2}^{0.5}(\\tan (3x))/(x)dx \\approx\n$$\n\n> A graphing calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186193006 :question/problem "The function $f(x)$, defined as\n$f(x) = 1 - \\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{15 - kx}}, x < \\frac{15}{k}$\nhas an inverse function given by\n\n$f^{-1}(x) = 5 - (25)/(12(x - 1)^{2}), x < 1$.\n\nWhat is the value of $k$?" "The function $f(x)$, defined as\n$$\n\\displaystyle f(x) = 1 - \\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{15 - kx}}, x < \\frac{15}{k}\n$$\nhas an inverse function given by\n\n$f^{-1}(x) = 5 - (25)/(12(x - 1)^{2}), x < 1$.\n\nWhat is the value of $k$?"] [:db/cas 17592186193022 :question/problem "The table below gives the values of a positive, continuous function $f(x)$ over the closed interval $[1, 9]$.\nUsing a right Riemann sum with $4$ equal subintervals, estimate the volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by the curve $y = f(x)$, the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x = 1$ and $x = 9$ is rotated $2π$ radians about the $x$-axis.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ | $9$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $2$ | $0$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n\n*Hint: The volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by a curve $y = f(x)$, the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x = a$ and $x = b$ is rotated $2π$ radians about the $x$-axis is given by*\n\n$V = π\\displaystyle \\int_{a}^{b}[f(x)]^{2}dx$.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." "The table below gives the values of a positive, continuous function $f(x)$ over the closed interval $[1, 9]$.\nUsing a right Riemann sum with $4$ equal subintervals, estimate the volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by the curve $y = f(x)$, the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x = 1$ and $x = 9$ is rotated $2π$ radians about the $x$-axis.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ | $9$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $2$ | $0$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n\n*Hint: The volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by a curve $y = f(x)$, the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x = a$ and $x = b$ is rotated $2π$ radians about the $x$-axis is given by*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle V = π\\displaystyle \\int_{a}^{b}[f(x)]^{2}dx.\n$$\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186193025 :question/problem "The table below gives the values of a continuous function $f(x)$ and its derivative $f^{′}(x)$ over the closed interval $[0, 8]$.\nUsing a left Riemann sum with $4$ equal subintervals, estimate the arc length of the curve $y = f(x)$ between $x = 0$ and $x = 8$.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $0$ | $3$ |\n| $f^{′}(x)$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $0$ | $-\\sqrt{3}$ | $2\\sqrt{2}$ | $0$ |\n\n*Hint: The arc length of a smooth curve $y = f(x)$ between $x = a$ and $x = b$ is given by*\n\n$L = \\displaystyle \\int_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1 + [f^{′}(x)]^{2}}dx$.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." "The table below gives the values of a continuous function $f(x)$ and its derivative $f^{′}(x)$ over the closed interval $[0, 8]$.\nUsing a left Riemann sum with $4$ equal subintervals, estimate the arc length of the curve $y = f(x)$ between $x = 0$ and $x = 8$.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $0$ | $3$ |\n| $f^{′}(x)$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $0$ | $-\\sqrt{3}$ | $2\\sqrt{2}$ | $0$ |\n\n*Hint: The arc length of a smooth curve $y = f(x)$ between $x = a$ and $x = b$ is given by*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle L = \\displaystyle \\int_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1 + [f^{′}(x)]^{2}}dx.\n$$\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186193029 :question/problem "What fraction gives a true statement when inserted into the blank space?\n\n${\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{9}} + \\bbox[2pt,white,border:1px solid black]{\\phantom{ {\\displaystyle \\frac{a}{a}}}} + {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{9}} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{8}{9}}$" "What fraction gives a true statement when inserted into the blank space?\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{9}} + \\bbox[2pt,white,border:1px solid black]{\\phantom{ {\\displaystyle \\frac{a}{a}}}} + {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{9}} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{8}{9}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186193030 :question/problem "Which of the following are decompositions of the fraction $\\frac{8}{11}$?\n\n1. $\\frac{4}{11} + \\frac{3}{11} + \\frac{1}{11}$\n2. $\\frac{4}{10} + \\frac{4}{10}$\n3. $\\frac{1}{11} + \\frac{2}{11} + \\frac{2}{11} + \\frac{3}{11}$" "Which of the following are decompositions of the fraction $\\frac{8}{11}$?\n\n1. $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{4}{11} + \\frac{3}{11} + \\frac{1}{11}\n$$\n2. $\\frac{4}{10} + \\frac{4}{10}$\n3. $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{11} + \\frac{2}{11} + \\frac{2}{11} + \\frac{3}{11}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186193031 :question/prob