ribution with $30$ degrees of freedom.*\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186193269 :question/problem "A restaurant owner believes that the mean number of customers per day has changed since he changed the menu. The number of customers per day after and before the menu change are known to be normally distributed with means $μ_{1}$ and $μ_{2}$. The table below shows the summary statistics of several randomly selected days before and after the menu change, where $n$ is the number of days in each sample, $x^(―)$ is the mean number of customers per day, and $s^{2}$ is the sample variance.\n\n|  | After Menu Change | Before Menu Change |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $n$ | $7$ | $7$ |\n| $x^(―)$ | $190$ | $150$ |\n| $s^{2}$ | $20^{2}$ | $30^{2}$ |\n\nAssuming that the samples are independent, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The end-points of the $95 \\mathrm{\\% }$ confidence interval for the difference between the mean number of customers are approximately $40 ± 30.364$.\n2. All values within the $95 \\mathrm{\\% }$ confidence interval are larger than $10$.\n3. The confidence interval data suggests that the mean number of customers per day has increased by at least $10$ since the menu change.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution $T_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores $t_{k, p}$ such that $P(T > t_{k, p}) = p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p$.*\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.001$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $9$ | $1.833$ | $2.262$ | $4.297$ |\n| $10$ | $1.812$ | $2.228$ | $4.144$ |\n| $11$ | $1.796$ | $2.201$ | $4.025$ |\n| $12$ | $1.782$ | $2.179$ | $3.930$ |\n| $13$ | $1.771$ | $2.160$ | $3.852$ |\n\n*Using the usual notation, the number of degrees of freedom can be approximated as the greatest integer less than or equal to*\n\n$((\\frac{s_{1}^{2}}{n_{1}} + \\frac{s_{2}^{2}}{n_{2}})^{2})/(((s_{1}^{4})/(n_{1}^{2}(n_{1} - 1)) + (s_{2}^{4})/(n_{2}^{2}(n_{2} - 1))))$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." "A restaurant owner believes that the mean number of customers per day has changed since he changed the menu. The number of customers per day after and before the menu change are known to be normally distributed with means $μ_{1}$ and $μ_{2}$. The table below shows the summary statistics of several randomly selected days before and after the menu change, where $n$ is the number of days in each sample, $x^(―)$ is the mean number of customers per day, and $s^{2}$ is the sample variance.\n\n|  | After Menu Change | Before Menu Change |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $n$ | $7$ | $7$ |\n| $x^(―)$ | $190$ | $150$ |\n| $s^{2}$ | $20^{2}$ | $30^{2}$ |\n\nAssuming that the samples are independent, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The end-points of the $95 \\mathrm{\\% }$ confidence interval for the difference between the mean number of customers are approximately $40 ± 30.364$.\n2. All values within the $95 \\mathrm{\\% }$ confidence interval are larger than $10$.\n3. The confidence interval data suggests that the mean number of customers per day has increased by at least $10$ since the menu change.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution $T_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores $t_{k, p}$ such that $P(T > t_{k, p}) = p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p$.*\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.001$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $9$ | $1.833$ | $2.262$ | $4.297$ |\n| $10$ | $1.812$ | $2.228$ | $4.144$ |\n| $11$ | $1.796$ | $2.201$ | $4.025$ |\n| $12$ | $1.782$ | $2.179$ | $3.930$ |\n| $13$ | $1.771$ | $2.160$ | $3.852$ |\n\n*Using the usual notation, the number of degrees of freedom can be approximated as the greatest integer less than or equal to*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle ((\\frac{s_{1}^{2}}{n_{1}} + \\frac{s_{2}^{2}}{n_{2}})^{2})/(((s_{1}^{4})/(n_{1}^{2}(n_{1} - 1)) + (s_{2}^{4})/(n_{2}^{2}(n_{2} - 1)))).\n$$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186193284 :question/problem "If $∑_(i = 1)^(51)a_{i} = 2$ and $∑_(i = 1)^(51)b_{i} =-5$, then$∑_(i = 1)^(51)(a_{i} + b_{i}) =$\n{{field-1}}." "If $∑_(i = 1)^(51)a_{i} = 2$ and $∑_(i = 1)^(51)b_{i} =-5$, then$$\n\\displaystyle ∑_(i = 1)^(51)(a_{i} + b_{i}) =\n$$\n{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186193322 :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The second is *closed* since it contains all its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The third is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(0,2).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(0,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\\\\\text{ }0\\le x\\le 2,\\\\\\,1<y<4\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ only." "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The second is *closed* since it contains all its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The third is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(0,2).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(0,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\\\\\text{ }0\\le x\\le 2,\\\\\\,1<y<4\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186193323 :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(0,1).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(1,0).$\n\n- The second set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The third set is *closed* since it contains all its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\\\\\,0\\le x<2,\\\\\\,0<y<2\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ only." "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(0,1).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(1,0).$\n\n- The second set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The third set is *closed* since it contains all its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\\\\\,0\\le x<2,\\\\\\,0<y<2\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186193341 :question/problem "Given that $∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n}$ is convergent and $∑_(n = 1)^(∞)b_{n}$ is divergent, which of the following statements are true? Assume that $b_{n}$ is well-defined for $n⩾1$.\n\n1. $∑_(n = 12)^(∞)b_{n}$ is convergent\n2. $∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)b_{n}$ is convergent\n3. $∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n} + ∑_(n = 1)^(12)b_{n}$ is convergent" "Given that $∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n}$ is convergent and $∑_(n = 1)^(∞)b_{n}$ is divergent, which of the following statements are true? Assume that $b_{n}$ is well-defined for $n⩾1$.\n\n1. $∑_(n = 12)^(∞)b_{n}$ is convergent\n2. $$\n\\displaystyle ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)b_{n}\n$$ is convergent\n3. $$\n\\displaystyle ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n} + ∑_(n = 1)^(12)b_{n}\n$$ is convergent"] [:db/cas 17592186193342 :question/problem "Given that $∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n}$ is convergent and $∑_(n = 1)^(∞)b_{n}$ is convergent, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)b_{n}$ is convergent\n2. $∑_(n = 20)^(∞)a_{n}$ is convergent\n3. $10^{20} \\cdot ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)b_{n}$ is divergent" "Given that $∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n}$ is convergent and $∑_(n = 1)^(∞)b_{n}$ is convergent, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $$\n\\displaystyle ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)b_{n}\n$$ is convergent\n2. $∑_(n = 20)^(∞)a_{n}$ is convergent\n3. $$\n\\displaystyle 10^{20} \\cdot ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)b_{n}\n$$ is divergent"] [:db/cas 17592186193369 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{z}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}z.$" "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{z}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186193369 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{k}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{k}\\right)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{z}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}z & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+{3}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{z}{3}\\right)]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{3}\\right)]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/24/2465f099a94aa966dbf33eb89aa00973e692fa97c887a9b79baff8260f5d31cd.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{z}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{3}\\right)] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{k}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{k}\\right)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{z}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}z & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+{3}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{z}{3}\\right)]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{3}\\right)]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/24/2465f099a94aa966dbf33eb89aa00973e692fa97c887a9b79baff8260f5d31cd.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{z}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{3}\\right)] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186193377 :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The second set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The third set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\\\\\text{ }0<y<{x}^{2},\\\\\\,0<x<1\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ only." "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The second set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The third set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\\\\\text{ }0<y<{x}^{2},\\\\\\,0<x<1\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186193387 :question/problem "Given that $∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n}$ is divergent and $∑_(n = 1)^(∞)b_{n}$ is convergent, which of the following statements are true? Assume that $a_{n}$ is well-defined for $n⩾1$.\n\n1. $∑_(n = 10)^(∞)a_{n}$ is convergent\n2. $∑_(n = 20)^(∞)b_{n}$ is convergent\n3. $∑_(n = 10)^(∞)a_{n} - ∑_(n = 20)^(∞)b_{n}$ is divergent" "Given that $∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n}$ is divergent and $∑_(n = 1)^(∞)b_{n}$ is convergent, which of the following statements are true? Assume that $a_{n}$ is well-defined for $n⩾1$.\n\n1. $∑_(n = 10)^(∞)a_{n}$ is convergent\n2. $∑_(n = 20)^(∞)b_{n}$ is convergent\n3. $$\n\\displaystyle ∑_(n = 10)^(∞)a_{n} - ∑_(n = 20)^(∞)b_{n}\n$$ is divergent"] [:db/cas 17592186193391 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ defined on a set $S$ to be a valid probability density function, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nIn our case, for the function $f(x)=k{e}^{-3x}$ on the interval $0\\le x<\\infty ,$ the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }k{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }k{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x={[-\\frac{k}{3}{e}^{-3x}]}_{0}^{\\infty }=\\frac{k}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result back into the first equation and solving for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{k}{3}=1\\,⟹\\,k=3.\n$$" "For a function $f(x)$ defined on a set $S$ to be a valid probability density function, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$\n\nIn our case, for the function $f(x)=k{e}^{-3x}$ on the interval $0\\le x<\\infty ,$ the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }k{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }k{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x={[-\\frac{k}{3}{e}^{-3x}]}_{0}^{\\infty }=\\frac{k}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result back into the first equation and solving for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{k}{3}=1\\,⟹\\,k=3.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186193393 :question/problem "Compute $P(X < 2)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$f ( x ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{18}} {x}^{2} , \\hspace{1em} & - 3 \\le  x \\le  3 , \\\\ 0 , \\hspace{1em} & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{array} \\right.$" "Compute $P(X < 2)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle f ( x ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{18}} {x}^{2} , \\hspace{1em} & - 3 \\le  x \\le  3 , \\\\ 0 , \\hspace{1em} & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{array} \\right.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186193394 :question/problem "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n$f ( x ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{k}{ {x}^{2}}} , \\hspace{1em} & x \\ge  4 , \\\\ 0 , \\hspace{1em} & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{array} \\right.$" "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n$$\n\\displaystyle f ( x ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{k}{ {x}^{2}}} , \\hspace{1em} & x \\ge  4 , \\\\ 0 , \\hspace{1em} & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{array} \\right.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186193397 :question/problem "Given that $X$ is a continuous random variable with the following probability density function, compute $E[X]$.\n$f ( x ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{ {x}^{2}}{12}} , & 0 < x < 2 \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{7}{9}} , & 2 \\le  x < 3\\end{array} \\right.$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." "Given that $X$ is a continuous random variable with the following probability density function, compute $E[X]$.\n$$\n\\displaystyle f ( x ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{ {x}^{2}}{12}} , & 0 < x < 2 \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{7}{9}} , & 2 \\le  x < 3\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186193399 :question/problem "Given that $X$ is a continuous random variable with the following probability density function, compute $E[X]$.\n$f ( x ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{8}} , & 0 < x < 2 \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{ {x}^{3}}} , & x \\ge  2\\end{array} \\right.$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." "Given that $X$ is a continuous random variable with the following probability density function, compute $E[X]$.\n$$\n\\displaystyle f ( x ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{8}} , & 0 < x < 2 \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{ {x}^{3}}} , & x \\ge  2\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186193802 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we will learn how to conduct hypothesis tests for the difference between the means of two normal populations with *unknown* and *unequal* population variances.\n\nSuppose we have two normal populations $X$ and $Y{:}$\n\n$X \\sim N(\\mu_x, \\sigma^2_x), \\qquad Y \\sim N(\\mu_y, \\sigma^2_y)$\nwhere $\\mu_x$ and $\\mu_y$ are the population means, and $\\sigma_x^2$ and $\\sigma_y^2$ are the population variances.\n\nIn a previous lesson, we saw that the difference between the sample means is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} - \\overline{Y} \\sim N\\left(\\mu_x - \\mu_y,\\dfrac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y} \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overline{X}$ and $\\overline{Y}$ are the sample means, and $n_x$ and $n_y$ are the sample sizes. By $z$-scoring this result, we can express this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\overline{Y}- (\\mu_x - \\mu_y)}{\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y}}} \\sim N(0,1)\n$$\n\nIn most practical situations, the population variances $\\sigma_x^2$ and $\\sigma_y^2$ are unknown and must be replaced with estimates $S_x^2$ and $S_y^2$. In this situation, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT &= ((X^(―) - Y^(―)) - (μ_{x} - μ_{y}))/(\\sqrt{\\frac{S_{x}^{2}}{n_{x}} + \\frac{S_{y}^{2}}{n_{y}}})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfollows a $t$-distribution with $k$ degrees of freedom.\n\nWe can approximate the number of degrees of freedom $k$ in various ways.\n\n- *Method 1:* We can approximate the degrees of freedom using the so-called **Welch-Satterthwaite approximation:**\n$k \\approx \\frac{\\left(\\dfrac{s_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{s_y^2}{n_y} \\right)^2}{\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{s_x^2}{n_x} \\right)^2}{n_x - 1} + \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{s_y^2}{n_y} \\right)^2}{n_y - 1}}$\nwhere the final result is rounded *down* to the nearest integer.\n- *Method 2:* We can also approximate the degrees of freedom using the formula\n$k = \\min(n_x - 1, n_y - 1)$\nwhere $\\min(a,b)$ denotes the **minimum** of $a$ and $b$.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll use the second method. You'll get practice applying the first method in separate lessons.\n\nPlease bear in mind that this method is simpler but also more conservative:\n\n- Under Method 2, our estimate for the degrees of freedom will be *smaller* than Method 1.\n- Thus, the tails of the resulting distribution will be fatter, leading to *larger* critical values for a given significance level $\\alpha$. Note that \"larger critical values\" here means larger in the absolute sense, i.e.,\n$\\begin{vmatrix}t_{k_1} & > & t_{k_2}\\end{vmatrix}$\nwhenever $k_1 < k_2$ for critical values $t_{k_1}$ and $t_{k_2}$.\n- Therefore, the probability of a Type I error (i.e., rejecting $H_0$ when it is true) *decreases.*\n\nRemember that as $k\\to\\infty$, the $t$-distribution approaches $N(0,1)$. The standard normal curve has *extremely thin* tails!\n\n![](images/5c/5c73c5fd8613e99814ab47a1a41a46a4511991d3c7eeca33eefe5913a907c10c.png)" "In this lesson, we will learn how to conduct hypothesis tests for the difference between the means of two normal populations with *unknown* and *unequal* population variances.\n\nSuppose we have two normal populations $X$ and $Y{:}$\n\n$X \\sim N(\\mu_x, \\sigma^2_x), \\qquad Y \\sim N(\\mu_y, \\sigma^2_y)$\nwhere $\\mu_x$ and $\\mu_y$ are the population means, and $\\sigma_x^2$ and $\\sigma_y^2$ are the population variances.\n\nIn a previous lesson, we saw that the difference between the sample means is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} - \\overline{Y} \\sim N\\left(\\mu_x - \\mu_y,\\dfrac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y} \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overline{X}$ and $\\overline{Y}$ are the sample means, and $n_x$ and $n_y$ are the sample sizes. By $z$-scoring this result, we can express this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\overline{Y}- (\\mu_x - \\mu_y)}{\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y}}} \\sim N(0,1)\n$$\n\nIn most practical situations, the population variances $\\sigma_x^2$ and $\\sigma_y^2$ are unknown and must be replaced with estimates $S_x^2$ and $S_y^2$. In this situation, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT &= ((X^(―) - Y^(―)) - (μ_{x} - μ_{y}))/(\\sqrt{\\frac{S_{x}^{2}}{n_{x}} + \\frac{S_{y}^{2}}{n_{y}}})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfollows a $t$-distribution with $k$ degrees of freedom.\n\nWe can approximate the number of degrees of freedom $k$ in various ways.\n\n- *Method 1:* We can approximate the degrees of freedom using the so-called **Welch-Satterthwaite approximation:**\n$$\n\\displaystyle k \\approx \\frac{\\left(\\dfrac{s_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{s_y^2}{n_y} \\right)^2}{\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{s_x^2}{n_x} \\right)^2}{n_x - 1} + \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{s_y^2}{n_y} \\right)^2}{n_y - 1}}\n$$\nwhere the final result is rounded *down* to the nearest integer.\n- *Method 2:* We can also approximate the degrees of freedom using the formula\n$k = \\min(n_x - 1, n_y - 1)$\nwhere $\\min(a,b)$ denotes the **minimum** of $a$ and $b$.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll use the second method. You'll get practice applying the first method in separate lessons.\n\nPlease bear in mind that this method is simpler but also more conservative:\n\n- Under Method 2, our estimate for the degrees of freedom will be *smaller* than Method 1.\n- Thus, the tails of the resulting distribution will be fatter, leading to *larger* critical values for a given significance level $\\alpha$. Note that \"larger critical values\" here means larger in the absolute sense, i.e.,\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}t_{k_1} & > & t_{k_2}\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\nwhenever $k_1 < k_2$ for critical values $t_{k_1}$ and $t_{k_2}$.\n- Therefore, the probability of a Type I error (i.e., rejecting $H_0$ when it is true) *decreases.*\n\nRemember that as $k\\to\\infty$, the $t$-distribution approaches $N(0,1)$. The standard normal curve has *extremely thin* tails!\n\n![](images/5c/5c73c5fd8613e99814ab47a1a41a46a4511991d3c7eeca33eefe5913a907c10c.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186193816 :tutorial/content "Let's return to our first example, where we wish to conduct a hypothesis test to determine whether a particular coin is unfair.\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- We defined $p$ as \"the probability that the coin lands on heads when tossed randomly.\"\n- Since we want to check whether the coin is unfair, we have the following null and alternative hypotheses:\n$H_{0}: p|= \\frac{1}{2}; H_{1}: p \\mid \\ne \\frac{1}{2}$\n- The significance level is $\\alpha = 5\\%$.\n\nUnder the conditions specified in the null hypothesis, the random variable $X$ is a binomial random variable given by\n\n$$\nX\\sim B\\left(10,\\dfrac12\\right)\n$$\n\nThe probability mass function of $X$ under the null hypothesis is shown below.\n\n![](images/79/79306d39f30f044beb30aaa7f6356b7a226c0f3958597d2e230601d51474b801.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- When we select a significance level $\\alpha$ in a two-tailed test, the convention is to allow $\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ at either tail.\n- So if $\\alpha = 5\\%$, we have $\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2} = 2.5\\%$ at either tail.\n\nNow, suppose we conduct our experiment and get $2$ heads out of $10$ tosses.\n\nIf the result $X=2$ is statistically significant, it will lie in the critical region in the *left* tail of the probability distribution of $f(x)$.\n\nWe can compute the probability of getting\n\n$$\nX\\leq 2\n$$\n\nheads under the null hypothesis using our knowledge of the binomial distribution:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle P ( X \\le  2 ) & \\displaystyle  = P ( X = 0 ) + P ( X = 1 ) + P ( X = 2 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{0} \\right) {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} )}^{0} {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} )}^{10 - 0} + \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{1} \\right) {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} )}^{1} {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} )}^{10 - 1} + \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{2} \\right) {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} )}^{2} {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} )}^{10 - 2} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 1 \\cdot  {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} )}^{10} + 10 \\cdot  {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} )}^{10} + 45 \\cdot  {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} )}^{10} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 56 \\cdot  {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} )}^{10} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 0.055 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 5.5 \\mathrm{\\% } .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nThis probability is *larger than*\n\n$$\n\\dfrac\\alpha2 = 2.5\\%\n$$\n\nThis means that our result is *not* statistically significant. In other words, it is \"likely enough\" that this event could have occurred if the null hypothesis is true.\n\nTherefore, we should *not* reject the null hypothesis. Consequently, we conclude that the coin is fair." "Let's return to our first example, where we wish to conduct a hypothesis test to determine whether a particular coin is unfair.\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- We defined $p$ as \"the probability that the coin lands on heads when tossed randomly.\"\n- Since we want to check whether the coin is unfair, we have the following null and alternative hypotheses:\n$$\n\\displaystyle H_{0}: p|= \\frac{1}{2}; H_{1}: p \\mid \\ne \\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n- The significance level is $\\alpha = 5\\%$.\n\nUnder the conditions specified in the null hypothesis, the random variable $X$ is a binomial random variable given by\n\n$$\nX\\sim B\\left(10,\\dfrac12\\right)\n$$\n\nThe probability mass function of $X$ under the null hypothesis is shown below.\n\n![](images/79/79306d39f30f044beb30aaa7f6356b7a226c0f3958597d2e230601d51474b801.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- When we select a significance level $\\alpha$ in a two-tailed test, the convention is to allow $\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ at either tail.\n- So if $\\alpha = 5\\%$, we have $\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2} = 2.5\\%$ at either tail.\n\nNow, suppose we conduct our experiment and get $2$ heads out of $10$ tosses.\n\nIf the result $X=2$ is statistically significant, it will lie in the critical region in the *left* tail of the probability distribution of $f(x)$.\n\nWe can compute the probability of getting\n\n$$\nX\\leq 2\n$$\n\nheads under the null hypothesis using our knowledge of the binomial distribution:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle P ( X \\le  2 ) & \\displaystyle  = P ( X = 0 ) + P ( X = 1 ) + P ( X = 2 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{0} \\right) {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} )}^{0} {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} )}^{10 - 0} + \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{1} \\right) {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} )}^{1} {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} )}^{10 - 1} + \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{2} \\right) {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} )}^{2} {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} )}^{10 - 2} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 1 \\cdot  {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} )}^{10} + 10 \\cdot  {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} )}^{10} + 45 \\cdot  {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} )}^{10} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 56 \\cdot  {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} )}^{10} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 0.055 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 5.5 \\mathrm{\\% } .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nThis probability is *larger than*\n\n$$\n\\dfrac\\alpha2 = 2.5\\%\n$$\n\nThis means that our result is *not* statistically significant. In other words, it is \"likely enough\" that this event could have occurred if the null hypothesis is true.\n\nTherefore, we should *not* reject the null hypothesis. Consequently, we conclude that the coin is fair."] [:db/cas 17592186193818 :tutorial/content "Consider the function $f(x)$, defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\sqrt{\\sin x}\n$$\n\nA sketch of this function is shown below.\n\n![](images/b1/b1fd56fbb40743ccf15288f79b5847bbd990a239ce8a2cb4fa7f1974d914edc3.png)\n\nThe finite area $\\mathcal A$ bounded by $f(x)$ and the $x$-axis over the interval $x\\in [0,\\pi]$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{\\sin x} \\: \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nLet's use our graphing calculator to approximate this area. Since the function we want to integrate contains trigonometric functions, your calculator *must* be in radians mode!\n\nWe bring up the definite integral option on most calculators in one of the following ways:\n\n1. Using the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\displaystyle\\color{gray}\\,\\displaystyle \\int_{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}}^{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}} \\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{\\big \\mid 0\\big \\mid}}\\,}$ button. You may need to press $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,2\\text{nd}\\,}$ (or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{shift}\\,}$) first.\n2. Using the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{math}\\,}$ (or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{calc}\\,}$) button and selecting the \"integration\" option. Note that this might be called $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{fnInt}\\,}$ or similar.\n\nThe next steps depend on whether your calculator uses derivative or function notation. We'll discuss both. You're encouraged to read both instructions as there are several parallels.\n\nIntegral Notation\n\nIf your calculator uses derivative notation, you should see a prompt that looks similar to the following:\n\n$$\n\\int_{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}}^{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}} \\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{\\big \\mid 0\\big \\mid}} \\,\\textrm{d}\\,{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we fill in the empty boxes as follows:\n\n- Enter $0$ for the lower integration limit. Then, press $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\blacktriangleright\\,}$ to move the cursor to the upper integration limit, and enter the value $\\pi$.The prompt should now look as follows:\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{\\big \\mid 0\\big \\mid}} \\,\\textrm{d}\\,{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}}$\n- Navigate the cursor to the next empty box by pressing $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\blacktriangleright\\,}$. Enter the function definition by pressing the following keys:\n$\\bbox[3px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\sqrt{\\phantom{0}}} \\quad\\:\\: \\bbox[3px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\sin\\,}\\quad\\:\\: \\bbox[3px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}X,T,\\theta,n} \\quad\\:\\: \\bbox[3px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,)\\,}$\nThe prompt should now look as follows:\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{\\sin(x)} \\,\\textrm{d}\\,{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}}$\n- Navigate the cursor to the next empty box by pressing $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\blacktriangleright\\,}$. Press $\\bbox[3px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}X,T,\\theta,n}$ to enter the variable that we're integrating with respect to.The prompt should now look as follows:\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{\\sin(x)} \\,\\textrm{d}x$\n- Finally, press $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\textrm{enter}\\,}$, and the calculator will return the numerical value of the integral.\n\nFollowing the above steps, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{\\sin x} \\: \\textrm{d}x\\approx 2.396\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nFunction Notation\n\nIf your calculator uses function notation, then instead of being presented with\n\n$$\n\\int_{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}}^{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}} \\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{\\big \\mid 0\\big \\mid}} \\,\\textrm{d}\\,{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}}\n$$\n\nyou'll instead see a prompt that looks similar to the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{integral}\\Big(\\bbox[gray]{\\color{white}\\phantom{\\Big \\mid}\\text{expression}\\phantom{\\Big \\mid}}, \\bbox[gray]{\\color{white}\\phantom{\\Big \\mid}\\text{variable}\\phantom{\\Big \\mid}}, \\bbox[gray]{\\color{white}\\phantom{\\Big \\mid}\\text{lowerBound}\\phantom{\\Big \\mid}}, \\bbox[gray]{\\color{white}\\phantom{\\Big \\mid}\\text{upperBound}\\phantom{\\Big \\mid}}\\Big)\n$$\n\nIn this case, we place the function definition in the first box, the variable in the second box, and the lower and upper limits in the third and fourth boxes, respectively.\n\nBy entering this information, the prompt will look as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{integral}\\Big(\\,\\sqrt{\\sin x}\\,, \\,x\\,, \\,0\\,, \\,\\pi\\, \\Big)\n$$\n\nOnce you're done, press $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\textrm{enter}\\,}$.\n\nFollowing the above steps, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{\\sin x} \\: \\textrm{d}x\\approx 2.396\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\n**Watch Out!**\n\n- It's important to remember that the graphing calculator finds a numerical *approximation* of the definite integral.\n- Moreover, different calculators use different algorithms to arrive at their approximations.\n- For that reason, different calculators may give slightly different answers when approximating the definite integral of a function.\n- Despite this, the answers returned by different calculators will agree when the answer is rounded to two or three decimal places in most cases." "Consider the function $f(x)$, defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\sqrt{\\sin x}\n$$\n\nA sketch of this function is shown below.\n\n![](images/b1/b1fd56fbb40743ccf15288f79b5847bbd990a239ce8a2cb4fa7f1974d914edc3.png)\n\nThe finite area $\\mathcal A$ bounded by $f(x)$ and the $x$-axis over the interval $x\\in [0,\\pi]$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{\\sin x} \\: \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nLet's use our graphing calculator to approximate this area. Since the function we want to integrate contains trigonometric functions, your calculator *must* be in radians mode!\n\nWe bring up the definite integral option on most calculators in one of the following ways:\n\n1. Using the $$\n\\displaystyle \\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\displaystyle\\color{gray}\\,\\displaystyle \\int_{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}}^{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}} \\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{\\big \\mid 0\\big \\mid}}\\,}\n$$ button. You may need to press $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,2\\text{nd}\\,}$ (or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{shift}\\,}$) first.\n2. Using the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{math}\\,}$ (or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{calc}\\,}$) button and selecting the \"integration\" option. Note that this might be called $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{fnInt}\\,}$ or similar.\n\nThe next steps depend on whether your calculator uses derivative or function notation. We'll discuss both. You're encouraged to read both instructions as there are several parallels.\n\nIntegral Notation\n\nIf your calculator uses derivative notation, you should see a prompt that looks similar to the following:\n\n$$\n\\int_{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}}^{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}} \\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{\\big \\mid 0\\big \\mid}} \\,\\textrm{d}\\,{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we fill in the empty boxes as follows:\n\n- Enter $0$ for the lower integration limit. Then, press $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\blacktriangleright\\,}$ to move the cursor to the upper integration limit, and enter the value $\\pi$.The prompt should now look as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{\\big \\mid 0\\big \\mid}} \\,\\textrm{d}\\,{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}}\n$$\n- Navigate the cursor to the next empty box by pressing $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\blacktriangleright\\,}$. Enter the function definition by pressing the following keys:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\bbox[3px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\sqrt{\\phantom{0}}} \\quad\\:\\: \\bbox[3px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\sin\\,}\\quad\\:\\: \\bbox[3px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}X,T,\\theta,n} \\quad\\:\\: \\bbox[3px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,)\\,}\n$$\nThe prompt should now look as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{\\sin(x)} \\,\\textrm{d}\\,{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}}\n$$\n- Navigate the cursor to the next empty box by pressing $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\blacktriangleright\\,}$. Press $\\bbox[3px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}X,T,\\theta,n}$ to enter the variable that we're integrating with respect to.The prompt should now look as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{\\sin(x)} \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n- Finally, press $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\textrm{enter}\\,}$, and the calculator will return the numerical value of the integral.\n\nFollowing the above steps, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{\\sin x} \\: \\textrm{d}x\\approx 2.396\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nFunction Notation\n\nIf your calculator uses function notation, then instead of being presented with\n\n$$\n\\int_{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}}^{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}} \\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{\\big \\mid 0\\big \\mid}} \\,\\textrm{d}\\,{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}}\n$$\n\nyou'll instead see a prompt that looks similar to the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{integral}\\Big(\\bbox[gray]{\\color{white}\\phantom{\\Big \\mid}\\text{expression}\\phantom{\\Big \\mid}}, \\bbox[gray]{\\color{white}\\phantom{\\Big \\mid}\\text{variable}\\phantom{\\Big \\mid}}, \\bbox[gray]{\\color{white}\\phantom{\\Big \\mid}\\text{lowerBound}\\phantom{\\Big \\mid}}, \\bbox[gray]{\\color{white}\\phantom{\\Big \\mid}\\text{upperBound}\\phantom{\\Big \\mid}}\\Big)\n$$\n\nIn this case, we place the function definition in the first box, the variable in the second box, and the lower and upper limits in the third and fourth boxes, respectively.\n\nBy entering this information, the prompt will look as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{integral}\\Big(\\,\\sqrt{\\sin x}\\,, \\,x\\,, \\,0\\,, \\,\\pi\\, \\Big)\n$$\n\nOnce you're done, press $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\textrm{enter}\\,}$.\n\nFollowing the above steps, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{\\sin x} \\: \\textrm{d}x\\approx 2.396\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\n**Watch Out!**\n\n- It's important to remember that the graphing calculator finds a numerical *approximation* of the definite integral.\n- Moreover, different calculators use different algorithms to arrive at their approximations.\n- For that reason, different calculators may give slightly different answers when approximating the definite integral of a function.\n- Despite this, the answers returned by different calculators will agree when the answer is rounded to two or three decimal places in most cases."] [:db/cas 17592186193830 :tutorial/content "We've seen how to approximate definite integrals using a graphing calculator. In this lesson, we'll apply the techniques we've learned to approximate improper integrals.\n\nSuppose we wish to evaluate the following improper integral to three decimal places:\n\n$$\n\\int_1^\\infty \\dfrac{\\sqrt{\\ln{x}}}{x^4}\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nYour graphing calculator is unlikely to include an $\\infty$ button, so we cannot directly enter this improper integral into our graphing calculator.\n\nHowever, recall that, by definition\n\n$$\n\\int_1^\\infty \\dfrac{\\sqrt{\\ln{x}}}{x^4}\\,\\textrm d x = \\lim\\limits_{a\\to\\infty} \\int_1^a \\dfrac{\\sqrt{\\ln{x}}}{x^4}\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nSo instead, let's consider the definite integral\n\n$$\n\\int_1^a \\dfrac{\\sqrt{\\ln{x}}}{x^4}\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nWe now use our graphing calculator to approximate the above integral for increasing values of $a{:}$\n\n- Setting $a=9$, we have\n$\\displaystyle \\int_1^9 \\dfrac{\\sqrt{\\ln{x}}}{x^4}\\,\\textrm d x \\approx 0.170$\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n- Setting $a=99$, we have\n$\\displaystyle \\int_1^{99} \\dfrac{\\sqrt{\\ln{x}}}{x^4}\\,\\textrm d x \\approx 0.171$\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n- Setting $a=999$, we have\n$\\displaystyle \\int_1^{999} \\dfrac{\\sqrt{\\ln{x}}}{x^4}\\,\\textrm d x \\approx 0.171$\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nSince the integrals with upper limits $99$ and $999$ both give $0.171$ to $3$ decimal places, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int_1^\\infty \\dfrac{\\sqrt{\\ln{x}}}{x^4}\\,\\textrm d x \\approx 0.171\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "We've seen how to approximate definite integrals using a graphing calculator. In this lesson, we'll apply the techniques we've learned to approximate improper integrals.\n\nSuppose we wish to evaluate the following improper integral to three decimal places:\n\n$$\n\\int_1^\\infty \\dfrac{\\sqrt{\\ln{x}}}{x^4}\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nYour graphing calculator is unlikely to include an $\\infty$ button, so we cannot directly enter this improper integral into our graphing calculator.\n\nHowever, recall that, by definition\n\n$$\n\\int_1^\\infty \\dfrac{\\sqrt{\\ln{x}}}{x^4}\\,\\textrm d x = \\lim\\limits_{a\\to\\infty} \\int_1^a \\dfrac{\\sqrt{\\ln{x}}}{x^4}\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nSo instead, let's consider the definite integral\n\n$$\n\\int_1^a \\dfrac{\\sqrt{\\ln{x}}}{x^4}\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nWe now use our graphing calculator to approximate the above integral for increasing values of $a{:}$\n\n- Setting $a=9$, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_1^9 \\dfrac{\\sqrt{\\ln{x}}}{x^4}\\,\\textrm d x \\approx 0.170\n$$\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n- Setting $a=99$, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_1^{99} \\dfrac{\\sqrt{\\ln{x}}}{x^4}\\,\\textrm d x \\approx 0.171\n$$\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n- Setting $a=999$, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_1^{999} \\dfrac{\\sqrt{\\ln{x}}}{x^4}\\,\\textrm d x \\approx 0.171\n$$\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nSince the integrals with upper limits $99$ and $999$ both give $0.171$ to $3$ decimal places, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int_1^\\infty \\dfrac{\\sqrt{\\ln{x}}}{x^4}\\,\\textrm d x \\approx 0.171\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."]]
